var e=[{topicId:`oc-bonding-effects`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Almost every organic question reduces to one idea: where are the electrons, and which way are they being pushed? Four electronic effects move electron density around a molecule — inductive, mesomeric (resonance), hyperconjugation and the electromeric effect. Once you can label them you can predict acidity, basicity, stability and reactivity without memorising individual compounds.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The four effects in plain words`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Inductive effect (I): permanent shift of sigma-bond electrons because one atom is more electronegative. It is transmitted through bonds and dies out after about three carbons.`,`-I groups pull electrons away: NO2 > CN > COOH > F > Cl > Br > I > OCH3 > OH > C6H5.`,`+I groups push electrons in: (CH3)3C- > (CH3)2CH- > CH3CH2- > CH3-, and anions such as COO- and O- are strongly +I.`,`Mesomeric or resonance effect (M or R): delocalisation of pi or lone-pair electrons through a conjugated system. It is not distance-limited the way induction is.`,`-M groups withdraw by resonance: NO2, CN, CHO, COR, COOH, COOR, SO3H.`,`+M groups donate by resonance: O-, NH2, NHR, NR2, OH, OR, NHCOR, halogens (weakly).`,`Hyperconjugation: overlap of a C-H sigma bond next to a positive centre or a double bond with the empty or pi orbital. Also called no-bond resonance or the Baker-Nathan effect.`,`Electromeric effect (E): a temporary, complete shift of a pi pair towards one atom, seen only while an attacking reagent is present.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Halogens do two opposite things`,text:`A halogen on benzene is -I (it withdraws through the sigma bond) but +M (it donates a lone pair by resonance). The -I wins for reactivity, so halobenzene is deactivated; the +M wins for orientation, so it is still ortho-para directing. That single sentence is a repeat GPAT question.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Why the effects matter: acids and bases`},{kind:`para`,text:`An acid is stronger when its conjugate base (the anion left after losing H+) is more stable. Electron-withdrawing groups spread the negative charge and stabilise the anion, so they increase acidity. Electron-donating groups pile more charge onto an already negative centre and decrease acidity. For bases the logic reverses: anything that makes the lone pair more available raises basicity.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Compound`,`pKa (approx)`,`Why`],rows:[[`Trichloroacetic acid`,`0.7`,`Three -I chlorines spread the carboxylate charge`],[`Chloroacetic acid`,`2.9`,`One -I chlorine`],[`Formic acid`,`3.75`,`No alkyl group to push electrons in`],[`Benzoic acid`,`4.2`,`sp2 ring is mildly -I, stabilises carboxylate`],[`Acetic acid`,`4.76`,`CH3 is +I and destabilises the anion`],[`Phenol`,`10`,`Phenoxide delocalised into the ring`],[`Ethanol`,`15.9`,`Ethoxide has no delocalisation`]],caption:`Lower pKa means stronger acid. Learn the order, not the decimals.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Acid strength ladder`,text:`Sulfonic acid > carboxylic acid > carbonic acid > phenol > water > alcohol > alkyne > ammonia > alkene > alkane. Read it as: the more the negative charge can spread, the stronger the acid.`}]},{topicId:`oc-bonding-effects`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`At exam level you must be able to rank a set of four substituted acids or amines instantly. The method is always the same: draw the conjugate base (for acids) or the free base (for amines), then count the stabilising and destabilising effects acting on it.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Effect comparison`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Inductive`,`Mesomeric`,`Hyperconjugation`],rows:[[`Electrons moved`,`Sigma`,`Pi and lone pairs`,`Sigma C-H with pi or empty p`],[`Requires conjugation`,`No`,`Yes`,`No, needs alpha C-H`],[`Distance`,`Falls off after 3 carbons`,`Travels whole conjugated chain`,`Alpha position only`],[`Permanent?`,`Permanent`,`Permanent`,`Permanent`],[`Relative strength`,`Weakest of the three`,`Strongest`,`Intermediate`],[`Charge developed`,`Partial only`,`Full charges in canonicals`,`Partial`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Carbocation and free-radical stability`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Carbocation order: benzyl and allyl (resonance) > 3 degree > 2 degree > 1 degree > methyl > vinyl and phenyl cations.`,`Number of alpha hydrogens available for hyperconjugation: tert-butyl cation 9, isopropyl 6, ethyl 3, methyl 0 — exactly the stability order.`,`Free radicals follow the same order: benzyl, allyl > 3 degree > 2 degree > 1 degree > methyl.`,`Carbanions are the reverse: methyl > 1 degree > 2 degree > 3 degree, because alkyl groups are +I and destabilise a negative centre.`,`Carbanion stability also rises with s character: sp (alkyne) > sp2 (alkene) > sp3 (alkane), which is why terminal alkynes are acidic.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Ka = [H+][A-] / [HA]   and   pKa = -log10 Ka`,caption:`Ka is the acid dissociation constant; HA the acid, A- its conjugate base. A lower pKa means a stronger acid.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Basicity of amines`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Series`,`Order observed`,`Reason`],rows:[[`Aliphatic amines in water`,`2 degree > 1 degree > 3 degree > NH3`,`Balance of +I and solvation of the cation`],[`Aliphatic amines in gas phase`,`3 degree > 2 degree > 1 degree > NH3`,`Pure inductive effect, no solvation`],[`Aromatic vs aliphatic`,`Alkylamine > NH3 > aniline`,`Aniline lone pair delocalised into the ring`],[`Substituted anilines`,`p-toluidine > aniline > p-chloroaniline > p-nitroaniline`,`Donating groups raise, withdrawing groups lower basicity`],[`Amides`,`Amide much weaker than amine`,`Lone pair pulled into the C=O`],[`Pyridine vs pyrrole`,`Pyridine much more basic`,`Pyridine lone pair is in an sp2 orbital, outside the ring sextet`]],caption:`The aliphatic anomaly (3 degree amine falling below 2 degree in water) is a classic GPAT single-answer question.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Guanidine and amidines`,text:`Guanidine (pKa of conjugate acid about 13.6) is the strongest neutral organic base commonly examined, because the guanidinium cation is stabilised by three equivalent resonance structures — a Y-delocalised system.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Phenol acidity ordering`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Picric acid (2,4,6-trinitrophenol, pKa 0.4) > 2,4-dinitrophenol > p-nitrophenol > phenol > p-cresol.`,`o- and p-nitrophenols are far more acidic than m-nitrophenol, because only ortho and para allow the negative charge to reach the nitro group by resonance.`,`o-nitrophenol is steam volatile and less soluble than the para isomer because of intramolecular hydrogen bonding (chelation); the para isomer forms intermolecular hydrogen bonds.`]}]},{topicId:`oc-bonding-effects`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The high-scoring layer is the set of exceptions where a simple -I or +M argument gives the wrong answer, plus the quantitative treatment of substituent effects.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`log (K / K0) = rho x sigma`,caption:`Hammett equation. K is the rate or equilibrium constant for the substituted compound, K0 for the unsubstituted one, sigma is the substituent constant (positive for withdrawing, e.g. p-NO2 = +0.78; negative for donating, e.g. p-OCH3 = -0.27) and rho is the reaction constant. Benzoic acid ionisation defines rho = 1.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`A positive rho means the reaction is helped by electron withdrawal, i.e. negative charge builds up in the transition state.`,`A negative rho means positive charge builds up, so electron donors accelerate it (e.g. SN1 solvolysis of benzyl chlorides).`,`sigma-plus and sigma-minus scales are used when the substituent talks directly to the reacting centre by resonance.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Exceptions worth marks`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Observation`,`Naive prediction`,`Real reason`],rows:[[`o-toluic acid stronger than benzoic`,`CH3 is +I so weaker`,`Ortho effect: steric twist of COOH out of plane`],[`All ortho-substituted benzoic acids stronger`,`Depends on group`,`Loss of conjugation with ring, steric inhibition of resonance`],[`2,6-dimethylaniline weakly basic despite +I`,`Should be strong base`,`Steric inhibition of solvation of the cation`],[`N,N-dimethyl-2,6-dinitroaniline is more basic than expected`,`Two -M groups`,`Nitro groups twisted out of plane, resonance lost`],[`Formic acid stronger than acetic`,`Both simple acids`,`No +I alkyl group on formic acid`],[`Fluoroacetic > chloroacetic > bromo > iodo`,`Similar halogens`,`Electronegativity order F > Cl > Br > I`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Bredt rule and anti-Bredt alkenes`,text:`A double bond cannot sit at the bridgehead of a small bicyclic system, because the p orbitals cannot align. This kills carbocation and alkene formation at bridgeheads, which is why 1-bromobicyclo[2.2.1]heptane will not undergo SN1 or E1 at all.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Cyclopentadiene (pKa about 16) is far more acidic than a typical alkene because its anion is aromatic with 6 pi electrons.`,`Cycloheptatriene loses H- readily to give the aromatic tropylium cation; that cation is stable enough to be isolated as a bromide salt.`,`Vinyl and aryl halides are unreactive to SN1 and SN2 because of partial double-bond character from lone-pair donation into the sp2 carbon, plus a shorter, stronger C-X bond.`,`Bond strength argument: sp C-H (alkyne) has 50 percent s character, holding the pair close to carbon, so its anion is stabilised — pKa 25 versus 44 for ethene and about 50 for ethane.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Steric inhibition of resonance`,text:`Whenever a question shows a bulky group ortho to a resonance-active substituent, the answer nearly always invokes steric inhibition of resonance — the group is forced out of the ring plane so its +M or -M is switched off, leaving only the inductive effect.`}]},{topicId:`oc-stereochem`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Stereochemistry is about molecules with the same atoms joined the same way but arranged differently in space. Because drug receptors are themselves chiral, one arrangement can be the medicine and the other useless or toxic — which is why this is the single heaviest organic topic in GPAT.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Vocabulary you must be able to define`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Isomers: same molecular formula, different compounds. Split into structural (constitutional) and stereoisomers.`,`Chiral: not superimposable on its mirror image. The commonest cause is a carbon with four different groups, called a stereocentre or asymmetric carbon.`,`Enantiomers: a pair of non-superimposable mirror images. Identical in every physical property except the direction they rotate plane-polarised light and their behaviour towards other chiral things.`,`Diastereomers: stereoisomers that are not mirror images. They have different melting points, solubilities and spectra, so they can be separated by ordinary means.`,`Meso compound: has stereocentres but also an internal mirror plane, so the molecule is achiral and optically inactive overall.`,`Racemic mixture (racemate): 50:50 of two enantiomers; optically inactive by external compensation, written as (+/-) or dl or RS.`,`Optical activity: rotation of the plane of polarised light, measured with a polarimeter. Dextrorotatory is (+) or d, laevorotatory is (-) or l.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Number of stereoisomers = 2^n  (n = number of stereocentres, no symmetry)`,caption:`n is the count of stereocentres. If an internal plane of symmetry is possible the real number is lower, because meso forms collapse pairs into one compound.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`D/L is not d/l and neither is R/S`,text:`Capital D and L are configurational labels tied to glyceraldehyde, used for sugars and amino acids. Small d and l (or + and -) describe the measured direction of rotation. R and S come from the CIP rules and describe the actual 3D arrangement. A D sugar can be laevorotatory: D-fructose is D but (-).`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Assigning R and S in four steps`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Rank the four groups by atomic number of the first atom attached; higher atomic number gets higher priority (a = highest, d = lowest).`,`If two first atoms tie, move outward one shell at a time and compare the sets of atoms attached, at the first point of difference.`,`Treat a double bond as duplicating the atom: C=O counts as (O, O, C) on that carbon.`,`Point the lowest priority group (d) away from you and read a to b to c. Clockwise is R (rectus), anticlockwise is S (sinister).`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`If the lowest group points at you`,text:`Read the rotation as normal, then reverse the answer. Clockwise seen with H towards you actually means S.`},{kind:`para`,text:`For double bonds, E and Z replace cis and trans. Rank the two groups on each carbon by CIP. If the two higher-priority groups are on the same side it is Z (zusammen, together); opposite sides is E (entgegen). Cis and trans only agree with Z and E when the compared groups happen to be the priority groups.`}]},{topicId:`oc-stereochem`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The exam level covers the specific rotation calculation, enantiomeric excess, the enantiomer versus diastereomer comparison table, and the conformational analysis of cyclohexane and its substituted forms.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`[alpha] = alpha(observed) / (l x c)`,caption:`Specific rotation. alpha(observed) in degrees, l is path length in decimetres, c is concentration in g/mL for solutions (or density for a neat liquid). Quoted as [alpha] at 20 degrees C with the sodium D line, 589 nm.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Enantiomeric excess (percent) = (observed rotation / rotation of pure enantiomer) x 100`,caption:`Also equals percent of major enantiomer minus percent of minor. A sample with 80 percent ee contains 90 percent of one enantiomer and 10 percent of the other.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Enantiomers versus diastereomers`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Property`,`Enantiomers`,`Diastereomers`],rows:[[`Mirror images`,`Yes, non-superimposable`,`No`],[`Melting point, bp, solubility`,`Identical`,`Different`],[`Specific rotation`,`Equal size, opposite sign`,`Different size and sign`],[`IR and NMR in achiral solvent`,`Identical`,`Different`],[`Separation`,`Needs a chiral method (resolution)`,`Ordinary crystallisation, distillation, chromatography`],[`Reaction with achiral reagent`,`Same rate`,`Different rates`],[`Number of stereocentres inverted`,`All of them`,`Some but not all`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Resolution methods`,text:`Mechanical picking, formation of diastereomeric salts with a chiral acid or base (brucine, quinine, tartaric acid, cinchonidine), biochemical or enzymatic resolution, and chiral chromatography. Pasteur used mechanical separation of sodium ammonium tartrate crystals in 1848.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Conformational analysis of cyclohexane`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Conformer`,`Relative energy (kJ/mol)`,`Comment`],rows:[[`Chair`,`0`,`Most stable, all bonds staggered, no angle strain`],[`Twist boat (skew)`,`about 23`,`Local minimum between boats`],[`Boat`,`about 29`,`Flagpole interaction plus eclipsing`],[`Half chair`,`about 45`,`Transition state of ring flip, least stable`]],caption:`Order of stability: chair > twist boat > boat > half chair. Ring flip converts every axial bond to equatorial and vice versa.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`A monosubstituted cyclohexane prefers the substituent equatorial, to avoid 1,3-diaxial interactions with the two axial hydrogens on carbons 3 and 5.`,`A value (conformational free energy) measures that preference: CH3 about 7.3 kJ/mol, isopropyl about 9.2, tert-butyl about 21 kJ/mol.`,`A tert-butyl group is so large it locks the ring, forcing itself equatorial — a conformationally anchored system.`,`cis-1,2-dimethylcyclohexane is axial-equatorial; trans-1,2 is diequatorial and therefore more stable.`,`For 1,3-disubstituted rings the cis isomer is the diequatorial and more stable one; for 1,4 the trans isomer is diequatorial.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Ethane and butane conformations`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Ethane: staggered is 12.5 kJ/mol more stable than eclipsed; the barrier is torsional strain, about 4.2 kJ/mol per eclipsed H-H pair.`,`Butane order: anti (180 degrees) > gauche (60 degrees) > eclipsed (120 degrees) > fully eclipsed syn (0 degrees).`,`Anti is about 3.8 kJ/mol below gauche; the syn-periplanar peak is about 19-25 kJ/mol above anti.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Optically inactive is not the same as achiral`,text:`A racemate is made of chiral molecules but shows no rotation (external compensation). A meso compound shows no rotation because the molecule itself is achiral (internal compensation). Only the racemate can be resolved into active components.`}]},{topicId:`oc-stereochem`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The advanced layer is chirality without a stereocentre, the prefixes used for sugars and amino acids, prochirality, and the drug examples GPAT quotes when it wants a stereochemistry question with a pharmacology flavour.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Chirality without an asymmetric carbon`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Example`,`Requirement`],rows:[[`Atropisomerism (axial)`,`Substituted biphenyls, BINAP`,`Bulky ortho groups block rotation`],[`Allenes (axial)`,`1,3-disubstituted allene`,`Both terminal carbons differently substituted`],[`Spiranes (axial)`,`Spiro[3.3]heptane derivatives`,`Perpendicular ring planes`],[`Planar chirality`,`trans-cyclooctene, paracyclophanes`,`Restricted plane, no symmetry`],[`Helical chirality`,`Hexahelicene`,`Right or left handed helix (P or M)`],[`Nitrogen stereocentre`,`Trogers base, quaternary ammonium salts`,`Inversion blocked or nitrogen quaternised`]],caption:`Simple amines are not resolvable because pyramidal nitrogen inverts about 10^11 times per second.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Sugar and amino acid conventions`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`D or L is decided by the highest-numbered stereocentre in the Fischer projection: OH on the right is D, on the left is L.`,`Natural sugars are D, natural amino acids are L (except glycine, which is achiral).`,`L-amino acids are S in CIP terms except L-cysteine, which is R because sulfur outranks the carboxyl carbon.`,`Epimers differ at exactly one stereocentre: D-glucose and D-mannose are C2 epimers, D-glucose and D-galactose are C4 epimers.`,`Anomers are epimers at the new hemiacetal carbon (C1 in aldoses); alpha and beta glucose differ only there.`,`Erythro and threo describe adjacent similar groups on the same or opposite sides in the eclipsed Fischer projection; syn and anti are the modern replacement.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Optical purity (percent) = 100 x [alpha](sample) / [alpha](pure enantiomer)`,caption:`Numerically equal to enantiomeric excess for a two-component mixture. If a mixture shows +10 degrees and the pure (+) form shows +50 degrees, optical purity is 20 percent, i.e. 60 percent (+) and 40 percent (-).`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Prochiral centre: becomes a stereocentre if one of two identical groups is replaced. The two hydrogens on the CH2 of ethanol are enantiotopic (pro-R and pro-S).`,`Homotopic groups give the same compound on replacement; enantiotopic give enantiomers; diastereotopic give diastereomers and are distinguishable by NMR.`,`Pseudoasymmetric centres take lower-case r and s.`,`Racemisation happens through a planar intermediate such as a carbocation, carbanion or enol; SN1 gives racemisation, SN2 gives inversion.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Drug stereochemistry facts asked repeatedly`,text:`Levodopa is the L (S) isomer and only it is active. (S)-thalidomide is teratogenic while (R) is sedative, but they interconvert in vivo. Esomeprazole is the S enantiomer of omeprazole, levocetirizine the R of cetirizine, dexketoprofen the S of ketoprofen, and levosalbutamol the R of salbutamol. Ethambutol is used as the (S,S) isomer; the (R,R) causes blindness.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Counting stereoisomers with symmetry`,text:`Tartaric acid has two stereocentres, so 2^2 = 4 is predicted, but only three exist: (+), (-) and one meso form. Whenever the two halves of a molecule are constitutionally identical, check for a meso form before answering 2^n.`}]},{topicId:`oc-substitution`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Nucleophilic substitution is the reaction where an electron-rich species (the nucleophile) replaces a group that leaves with the bonding pair (the leaving group). It happens by two limiting routes, SN1 and SN2, and telling them apart from the substrate, solvent and rate law is the commonest organic question in the paper.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Nucleophile: electron-pair donor attacking a carbon, e.g. OH-, CN-, I-, NH3, RO-, RS-.`,`Electrophile: electron-pair acceptor; here it is the carbon bearing the leaving group.`,`Leaving group: departs with the bonding electrons. A good leaving group is a weak base: I- > Br- > Cl- >> F-, and tosylate, mesylate and triflate are excellent.`,`Substrate: the alkyl halide or sulfonate being attacked, classified as methyl, primary, secondary or tertiary.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`SN2 in one line`},{kind:`para`,text:`One step. The nucleophile attacks the carbon from the face opposite the leaving group (backside attack) while the leaving group departs, passing through a five-coordinate transition state. The configuration turns inside out like an umbrella in the wind — this is Walden inversion.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`SN1 in one line`},{kind:`para`,text:`Two steps. The leaving group goes first, giving a flat carbocation; the nucleophile then attacks that cation from either face. Because the cation is planar you get roughly equal amounts of both configurations, so the product is racemised.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Substrate`,`Preferred route`,`Reason`],rows:[[`Methyl halide`,`SN2 only`,`No steric bulk, cation would be terrible`],[`Primary`,`SN2`,`Open to backside attack`],[`Secondary`,`Either, depends on solvent and nucleophile`,`Borderline`],[`Tertiary`,`SN1 only`,`Too crowded for SN2, cation is stable`],[`Allyl and benzyl`,`Both are fast`,`Resonance-stabilised cation and open backside`],[`Vinyl and aryl`,`Neither`,`sp2 C-X is short and strong, cation unstable`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Remember the numbers`,text:`The 1 and 2 are molecularities, not step counts. SN2 has two species in the rate-determining step; SN1 has one. SN1 has the extra step even though it carries the smaller number.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The classic one-liner`,text:`SN2 gives complete inversion; SN1 gives racemisation with a slight excess of inversion, because the leaving group shields one face of the ion pair before it fully separates.`}]},{topicId:`oc-substitution`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The full SN1 versus SN2 comparison is worth memorising as a single table — GPAT has asked almost every row of it at some point.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`SN1`,`SN2`],rows:[[`Steps`,`Two, via carbocation`,`One, concerted`],[`Rate law`,`Rate = k[substrate]`,`Rate = k[substrate][nucleophile]`],[`Kinetics`,`First order`,`Second order`],[`Molecularity`,`Unimolecular RDS`,`Bimolecular`],[`Substrate order`,`3 > 2 > 1 > methyl`,`Methyl > 1 > 2 > 3`],[`Stereochemistry`,`Racemisation (partial inversion)`,`Complete inversion (Walden)`],[`Intermediate`,`Planar carbocation`,`None; pentacoordinate TS`],[`Nucleophile strength`,`Unimportant`,`Critical, strong nucleophile needed`],[`Solvent`,`Polar protic favours it`,`Polar aprotic favours it`],[`Rearrangement`,`Common (hydride and methyl shifts)`,`Never`],[`Effect of [Nu]`,`No effect on rate`,`Rate is proportional to [Nu]`],[`Catalysis`,`Ag+ salts accelerate`,`Not applicable`]],caption:`One-line summary: SN1 is about cation stability, SN2 is about how crowded the carbon is.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Solvent effects`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Polar protic solvents (water, methanol, ethanol, formic acid) hydrogen bond to and stabilise both the carbocation and the leaving anion, so they speed SN1. They also cage the nucleophile, slowing SN2.`,`Polar aprotic solvents (DMSO, DMF, acetone, acetonitrile, HMPA) solvate the cation but leave the anion naked and very reactive, so they speed SN2 dramatically.`,`In protic solvents nucleophilicity follows polarisability: I- > Br- > Cl- > F-. In polar aprotic solvents it follows basicity, so the order reverses to F- > Cl- > Br- > I-.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Nucleophilicity rules`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Down a group nucleophilicity rises (larger, more polarisable): RS- > RO-, I- > Br-.`,`Across a period it follows basicity: R3C- > R2N- > RO- > F-.`,`A negative charge is a better nucleophile than its neutral acid: OH- > H2O, RO- > ROH, NH2- > NH3.`,`Alpha effect: HOO-, NH2NH2 and NH2OH are stronger nucleophiles than their basicity suggests, because of the lone pair on the adjacent atom.`,`Steric bulk kills nucleophilicity but not basicity — tert-butoxide is a strong base and a poor nucleophile, so it favours elimination.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`log (k / k0) = s x n   (Swain-Scott)`,caption:`k is the rate with the nucleophile, k0 with water, n is the nucleophilicity constant (water = 0, CN- about 5.1, I- about 5.0) and s is the substrate sensitivity (1.0 for methyl bromide).`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Neopentyl halide`,text:`Neopentyl bromide is a primary halide but reacts extremely slowly by SN2 because the adjacent quaternary carbon blocks backside attack. Under SN1 conditions it rearranges by a methyl shift to the tert-amyl cation. It is the standard trick question for the primary-equals-SN2 rule.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Related mechanisms`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`SNi (internal): ROH + SOCl2 without base gives retention of configuration through a chlorosulfite ion pair. Adding pyridine switches it to inversion.`,`SN2-prime: attack at the far end of an allylic system with allylic shift of the double bond.`,`Neighbouring group participation (anchimeric assistance): an internal nucleophile such as S, O, N, COO- or a phenyl group attacks first, then is displaced. Two inversions equal net retention, with a large rate acceleration.`,`Aromatic nucleophilic substitution needs strong -M groups ortho or para (SNAr, via a Meisenheimer complex) or very strong base (benzyne route).`]}]},{topicId:`oc-substitution`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced questions test the borderline cases, the evidence used to prove a mechanism, and the aromatic variants.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Evidence`,`Points to SN1`,`Points to SN2`],rows:[[`Rate doubles when [Nu] doubles`,`No`,`Yes`],[`Rate unchanged by [Nu]`,`Yes`,`No`],[`Common ion (added Cl-) slows reaction`,`Yes`,`No`],[`Product racemic`,`Yes`,`No`],[`Skeletal rearrangement seen`,`Yes`,`No`],[`Rate rises sharply with solvent polarity`,`Yes`,`Mild or opposite`],[`Alpha-deuterium isotope effect about 1.15`,`Yes (sp3 to sp2)`,`Near 1.0`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Aromatic nucleophilic substitution`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`SNAr is addition-elimination. The rate-determining step is nucleophile addition to give the anionic Meisenheimer complex, so electron-withdrawing groups ortho and para accelerate it.`,`Leaving-group order in SNAr is F > Cl > Br > I — the reverse of aliphatic substitution — because C-F polarises the ring most and C-X breaking is not rate determining.`,`Picryl chloride and 2,4-dinitrochlorobenzene (Sanger reagent for N-terminal amino acids) are the classic SNAr substrates.`,`The benzyne (elimination-addition) route needs NaNH2 in liquid ammonia or NaOH at 350 degrees C; labelling with 14C shows the nucleophile ends up on both the ipso and the adjacent carbon in equal amounts.`,`Cine substitution (product on the adjacent carbon) is the fingerprint of benzyne.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Ion-pair theory`,text:`Winstein explained partial inversion in SN1 with intimate ion pairs, solvent-separated ion pairs and free ions. Attack at the intimate ion-pair stage is blocked on the leaving-group face, so inversion always slightly exceeds retention.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Reactivity of alkyl halides towards SN1 by halogen: RI > RBr > RCl > RF, following C-X bond strength.`,`Allylic and benzylic halides are anomalously fast in both mechanisms; that dual reactivity is a favourite distractor.`,`Silver nitrate in ethanol is the classical SN1 test: tertiary and allylic halides give an instant AgX precipitate, primary ones need heat.`,`Sodium iodide in acetone (Finkelstein) is the SN2 test: primary halides precipitate NaCl or NaBr quickly, tertiary do not react.`,`Hydrolysis of an optically active halide that gives 60 percent racemisation plus 40 percent inversion means 30 percent of molecules went by SN1 with racemisation and 70 percent by inversion pathways.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Ambident nucleophiles`,text:`Cyanide gives nitrile with KCN (attack through carbon, the softer end) but isonitrile with AgCN. Nitrite gives nitroalkane with AgNO2 and alkyl nitrite with KNO2. Silver salts favour the harder atom because they push the reaction towards SN1 character.`}]},{topicId:`oc-elimination-addition`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Elimination removes two groups from adjacent atoms and creates a pi bond; addition does the opposite, breaking a pi bond to add two groups. They are the forward and reverse of the same equilibrium, and every alkene question in GPAT is one of the two.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Elimination basics`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Beta elimination (1,2-elimination) is the normal case: H comes off the beta carbon, the leaving group off the alpha carbon.`,`Dehydrohalogenation: alkyl halide plus alcoholic KOH gives an alkene. Aqueous KOH would instead give the alcohol by substitution.`,`Dehydration: alcohol plus concentrated H2SO4 or H3PO4 with heat gives an alkene. Ease of dehydration is 3 degree > 2 degree > 1 degree.`,`Dehalogenation: vicinal dibromide plus zinc dust gives the alkene back.`,`E2 is one concerted step needing a strong base; E1 goes through a carbocation, like SN1.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Saytzeff versus Hofmann`,text:`Saytzeff (Zaitsev) rule: the more substituted, more stable alkene is the major product — the normal outcome with small bases and halide leaving groups. Hofmann rule: the less substituted alkene wins when the base is bulky (tert-butoxide), the leaving group is bulky and positively charged (NR3+, SR2+), or the beta carbon carries an electron-withdrawing group.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Addition basics`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Markovnikov rule: in the addition of HX to an unsymmetrical alkene, hydrogen goes to the carbon already carrying more hydrogens, because that gives the more stable carbocation.`,`Anti-Markovnikov (peroxide or Kharasch effect): HBr plus organic peroxide adds by a free-radical chain, so Br lands on the less substituted carbon. This works only for HBr, not HCl, HI or HF.`,`Alkene stability: more alkyl substituents means more stable, so tetrasubstituted > trisubstituted > disubstituted > monosubstituted > ethene, and trans beats cis.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Reagent`,`Product from propene`,`Regiochemistry`],rows:[[`HBr`,`2-bromopropane`,`Markovnikov`],[`HBr with peroxide`,`1-bromopropane`,`Anti-Markovnikov`],[`H2O / H+`,`Propan-2-ol`,`Markovnikov, rearrangement possible`],[`B2H6 then H2O2/NaOH`,`Propan-1-ol`,`Anti-Markovnikov, syn addition`],[`Hg(OAc)2 / H2O then NaBH4`,`Propan-2-ol`,`Markovnikov, no rearrangement`],[`Br2 in CCl4`,`1,2-dibromopropane`,`Anti addition`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Markovnikov in one line`,text:`The rich get richer — the carbon with more hydrogens gets the hydrogen.`}]},{topicId:`oc-elimination-addition`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Exam questions turn on distinguishing E1, E2 and E1cb, and on the stereochemistry of each addition reagent — syn or anti, and whether a rearrangement is possible.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`E1`,`E2`,`E1cb`],rows:[[`Steps`,`Two, via carbocation`,`One, concerted`,`Two, via carbanion`],[`Rate law`,`k[substrate]`,`k[substrate][base]`,`k[substrate][base] usually`],[`Slow step`,`Loss of leaving group`,`Concerted removal`,`Loss of leaving group from carbanion`],[`Substrate order`,`3 > 2 > 1`,`3 > 2 > 1`,`Needs acidic beta H, poor LG`],[`Base needed`,`Weak base is enough`,`Strong base essential`,`Strong base, acidic beta H`],[`Stereochemistry`,`No requirement`,`Anti-periplanar H and LG`,`No requirement`],[`Orientation`,`Saytzeff`,`Saytzeff or Hofmann`,`Often Hofmann`],[`Isotope effect kH/kD`,`Near 1`,`2 to 8 (large)`,`Varies with step`],[`Rearrangement`,`Possible`,`No`,`No`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`E2 stereochemical requirement`,text:`The departing H and leaving group must be anti-periplanar, i.e. at a dihedral angle of 180 degrees. In a cyclohexane ring this means both must be axial and trans-diaxial. Neomenthyl chloride eliminates fast to the Saytzeff product, while menthyl chloride is slow and gives only the Hofmann alkene, because only one axial-axial arrangement is available.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Addition reagents and stereochemistry`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Reagent`,`Adds`,`Stereochemistry`,`Intermediate`],rows:[[`H2 / Pd, Pt, Ni`,`H and H`,`Syn`,`Surface adsorbed`],[`B2H6 then H2O2/NaOH`,`H and OH`,`Syn, anti-Markovnikov`,`Four-centre TS`],[`Br2 or Cl2`,`X and X`,`Anti`,`Bromonium ion`],[`Br2 in water`,`Br and OH`,`Anti, OH on more substituted C`,`Bromonium ion`],[`Cold dilute KMnO4`,`OH and OH`,`Syn diol`,`Cyclic manganate ester`],[`OsO4 then NaHSO3`,`OH and OH`,`Syn diol`,`Cyclic osmate ester`],[`Peracid (mCPBA) then H3O+`,`OH and OH`,`Anti diol`,`Epoxide`],[`Hg(OAc)2 / H2O then NaBH4`,`H and OH`,`Anti, Markovnikov`,`Mercurinium ion`]],caption:`The syn versus anti column is the single most-asked cell in this table.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Ozonolysis and cleavage`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`O3 then Zn/H2O (reductive workup) gives aldehydes and ketones.`,`O3 then H2O2 (oxidative workup) gives carboxylic acids and ketones; any aldehyde is oxidised further.`,`Hot acidic KMnO4 cleaves the alkene: a terminal =CH2 goes to CO2, a =CHR goes to RCOOH, and a =CR2 goes to a ketone.`,`Ozonolysis is the classical way to locate a double bond in an unknown structure.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Substitution or elimination?`,text:`Heat, a strong bulky base, a tertiary substrate and a less polar solvent all push towards elimination. A small, strongly nucleophilic but weakly basic species (CN-, I-, RS-, N3-) on a primary substrate pushes towards substitution. Alcoholic KOH eliminates; aqueous KOH substitutes.`}]},{topicId:`oc-elimination-addition`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The advanced material is syn eliminations, conjugate addition, and the quantitative side of alkene stability and radical chain addition.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Syn elimination`,`Substrate`,`Conditions`,`TS`],rows:[[`Cope elimination`,`Amine oxide`,`150 degrees C, no base`,`Five-membered`],[`Chugaev`,`Xanthate ester`,`150-200 degrees C`,`Six-membered`],[`Ester pyrolysis`,`Acetate ester`,`400-500 degrees C`,`Six-membered`],[`Selenoxide elimination`,`Alkyl selenoxide`,`Room temperature`,`Five-membered`],[`Hofmann exhaustive methylation`,`Quaternary ammonium hydroxide`,`Heat`,`E2, anti, Hofmann product`]],caption:`All the pyrolytic eliminations are syn and give the Hofmann (less substituted) alkene.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Conjugate (1,4 or Michael) addition to an alpha,beta-unsaturated carbonyl is favoured by soft nucleophiles (RS-, R2CuLi, amines, enolates, cyanide) and by thermodynamic control.`,`Direct (1,2) addition is favoured by hard nucleophiles (RMgX, RLi, LiAlH4) and low temperature — kinetic control.`,`Gilman reagents (R2CuLi) add 1,4; Grignards add 1,2. That contrast is a standard question.`,`Radical HBr addition proceeds by initiation (peroxide homolysis), propagation (Br radical adds to give the more stable carbon radical, then abstracts H from HBr) and termination. Only HBr works because H-Cl abstraction is endothermic and I-C addition is endothermic.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Heat of hydrogenation: ethene 137, propene 126, 2-butene (cis) 120, 2-butene (trans) 116 kJ/mol`,caption:`The lower the heat released on hydrogenation, the more stable the alkene. This is the experimental proof of the substitution and trans-beats-cis stability order.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Bredt rule again`,text:`Elimination cannot place a double bond at the bridgehead of a small bridged bicyclic system, so E1 and E2 simply fail there. Expect this as an except-type question.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Alpha elimination (1,1) gives carbenes: CHCl3 plus strong base gives dichlorocarbene, used in Reimer-Tiemann and Carbylamine reactions.`,`Simmons-Smith reagent (CH2I2 + Zn-Cu) delivers a carbenoid and gives stereospecific syn cyclopropanation.`,`Anti-Markovnikov hydration is achieved by hydroboration-oxidation; Markovnikov hydration without rearrangement is achieved by oxymercuration-demercuration.`,`Addition of Br2 to cis-2-butene gives the racemic (plus or minus) dibromide; the trans alkene gives the meso compound. The classic demonstration is maleic acid (cis) giving the racemate and fumaric acid (trans) giving the meso dibromosuccinic acid — the standard proof of anti addition through a bromonium ion. Get the direction the wrong way round and every question on this topic falls with it.`]}]},{topicId:`oc-aromatic`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Aromatic rings are unusually stable flat rings of delocalised electrons. That stability changes how they react: instead of adding across the ring like an alkene, they substitute, keeping the aromatic sextet intact.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Huckel rule`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`A compound is aromatic if it is cyclic, planar, fully conjugated (every ring atom has a p orbital) and contains (4n + 2) pi electrons where n is 0, 1, 2, 3...`,`So 2, 6, 10, 14 pi electrons are aromatic; benzene has 6.`,`Antiaromatic: cyclic, planar, conjugated but with 4n pi electrons — these are less stable than the open chain analogue. Cyclobutadiene (4 pi) is the standard example.`,`Non-aromatic: not cyclic, not planar or not fully conjugated. Cyclooctatetraene is non-aromatic because it takes a tub shape.`,`Charged aromatics: cyclopropenyl cation (2 pi), cyclopentadienyl anion (6 pi), tropylium cation (6 pi), cyclooctatetraene dianion (10 pi).`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Count only the electrons in the ring system`,text:`A lone pair counts only if it sits in a p orbital that is part of the cycle. In pyrrole the nitrogen lone pair is in the ring and counts, giving 6 pi. In pyridine the lone pair is in an sp2 orbital in the ring plane and does not count; the 6 pi come from the three double bonds.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Electrophilic aromatic substitution (SEAr)`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Generate the electrophile (usually with a Lewis or Bronsted acid).`,`The ring pi system attacks the electrophile to give a resonance-stabilised carbocation — the arenium ion or sigma complex, also called the Wheland intermediate. This step is slow and rate determining.`,`A base removes the proton from the sp3 carbon, restoring aromaticity. This step is fast.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Reaction`,`Reagents`,`Electrophile`,`Product`],rows:[[`Nitration`,`conc HNO3 + conc H2SO4`,`NO2+ (nitronium)`,`Nitrobenzene`],[`Sulfonation`,`Fuming H2SO4 (oleum)`,`SO3 or SO3H+`,`Benzenesulfonic acid`],[`Halogenation`,`Br2 / FeBr3`,`Br+ equivalent`,`Bromobenzene`],[`Friedel-Crafts alkylation`,`RCl / AlCl3`,`R+`,`Alkylbenzene`],[`Friedel-Crafts acylation`,`RCOCl / AlCl3`,`RCO+ (acylium)`,`Aryl ketone`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Sulfonation is reversible`,text:`Sulfonation is the only common SEAr that can be undone (dilute H2SO4 with steam removes SO3H). That makes the sulfonic acid group a usable blocking group for the para position.`}]},{topicId:`oc-aromatic`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The examinable core is directing effects: which position the next group enters and whether the ring has become faster or slower than benzene.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Group`,`Effect on rate`,`Directs to`,`Why`],rows:[[`NH2, NHR, NR2, OH, O-`,`Strongly activating`,`ortho, para`,`Strong +M outweighs -I`],[`NHCOR, OCOR, OR`,`Moderately activating`,`ortho, para`,`+M reduced by the carbonyl`],[`Alkyl, aryl`,`Weakly activating`,`ortho, para`,`+I and hyperconjugation`],[`F, Cl, Br, I`,`Deactivating`,`ortho, para`,`-I on rate, +M on orientation`],[`CHO, COR, COOH, COOR, CONH2`,`Moderately deactivating`,`meta`,`-M and -I`],[`CN, SO3H`,`Strongly deactivating`,`meta`,`-M and -I`],[`NO2, NR3+, CCl3`,`Very strongly deactivating`,`meta`,`Full or partial positive charge`]],caption:`All activators are ortho-para directors. All deactivators are meta directors except the halogens.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Directing rule in one line`,text:`Activators go ortho-para, deactivators go meta, halogens are the exception that deactivates yet still sends you ortho-para.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Reactivity ordering questions`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Towards nitration: aniline and phenol > toluene > benzene > chlorobenzene > nitrobenzene > benzoic acid (as a rough series).`,`Relative rate of nitration versus benzene: toluene about 25 times faster, chlorobenzene about 30 times slower, nitrobenzene about 10^-7 times.`,`Halobenzene order of reactivity in SEAr: PhF > PhCl > PhBr > PhI is often quoted for the para product ratio, but all are slower than benzene.`,`Phenol is so activated that it brominates three times in bromine water without a catalyst, giving 2,4,6-tribromophenol as a white precipitate.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Aniline in nitrating mixture`,text:`Aniline in conc H2SO4 is protonated to the anilinium ion, and NH3+ is a strongly deactivating meta director. That is why direct nitration of aniline gives a large amount of m-nitroaniline, and why acetylation to acetanilide is done first to get the para product cleanly.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Friedel-Crafts limitations`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Fails on rings deactivated more than a halobenzene, so nitrobenzene, benzoic acid and aniline give no reaction.`,`Fails on aniline for a second reason: the amine complexes irreversibly with AlCl3.`,`Alkylation suffers carbocation rearrangement: n-propyl chloride gives mainly isopropylbenzene (cumene).`,`Alkylation polyalkylates, because the alkyl product is more activated than the starting ring. Acylation stops after one step, because the ketone is deactivated.`,`Acylation needs more than one equivalent of AlCl3, since the ketone product complexes the catalyst.`,`A clean straight-chain alkyl group is therefore made by acylation followed by Clemmensen or Wolff-Kishner reduction.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Resonance energy of benzene = 150 kJ/mol (about 36 kcal/mol)`,caption:`The difference between the observed heat of hydrogenation of benzene (208 kJ/mol) and three times that of cyclohexene (3 x 120 = 360 kJ/mol).`}]},{topicId:`oc-aromatic`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced questions probe partial rate factors, ipso attack, orientation in polysubstituted rings and the reagents used for special substitutions.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`When two groups already present agree, orientation is easy. When they disagree, the stronger activator wins.`,`Positions between two meta-substituents are almost never substituted, for steric reasons.`,`ortho:para ratio is governed by sterics: tert-butylbenzene nitrates almost exclusively para, while toluene gives about 58 percent ortho and 37 percent para.`,`Ipso substitution replaces an existing group; sulfonic acid and tert-alkyl groups are commonly displaced.`,`Isotope effect in SEAr is normally close to 1, proving that the second (proton loss) step is not rate determining; a large effect (as in some sulfonations and Friedel-Crafts) means the second step has become slow.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Special reaction`,`Reagents`,`Group introduced`,`Note`],rows:[[`Gattermann-Koch`,`CO + HCl / AlCl3, CuCl`,`CHO`,`Fails on phenols and phenol ethers`],[`Gattermann`,`HCN + HCl / ZnCl2`,`CHO`,`Works on phenols`],[`Vilsmeier-Haack`,`DMF + POCl3`,`CHO`,`Needs an activated ring`],[`Reimer-Tiemann`,`CHCl3 + NaOH`,`CHO (ortho)`,`Via dichlorocarbene`],[`Kolbe-Schmitt`,`CO2, NaOH, pressure`,`COOH (ortho)`,`Gives salicylic acid`],[`Houben-Hoesch`,`RCN + HCl / ZnCl2`,`COR`,`Phenols and polyphenols`],[`Blanc chloromethylation`,`HCHO + HCl / ZnCl2`,`CH2Cl`,`Alkylation variant`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Sulfonation temperature control`,text:`Naphthalene sulfonated at 80 degrees C gives the 1-isomer (kinetic, faster because the alpha position gives a better-stabilised sigma complex); at 160 degrees C it gives the 2-isomer (thermodynamic, less peri strain). This kinetic-versus-thermodynamic example is asked directly.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Naphthalene is more reactive than benzene and substitutes at C1 (alpha); anthracene reacts at C9 and C10.`,`Birch reduction (Na in liquid NH3 with ethanol) gives 1,4-cyclohexadiene: with an electron-donating group the substituted carbons keep their double bonds (ipso-para unreduced), with an electron-withdrawing group they are reduced.`,`Side chain oxidation with hot KMnO4 converts any alkyl group with a benzylic hydrogen to COOH; tert-butylbenzene resists because it has no benzylic H.`,`Free-radical side-chain halogenation (NBS, light, no Lewis acid) attacks the benzylic position; ring halogenation needs a Lewis acid in the dark.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Antiaromatic traps`,text:`Cyclopentadienyl cation (4 pi) is antiaromatic and very unstable, whereas the anion (6 pi) is aromatic and easily formed. Tropylium cation (6 pi) is aromatic but the cycloheptatrienyl anion (8 pi) is antiaromatic. Always count electrons after the charge is applied.`}]},{topicId:`oc-carbonyl`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The carbonyl group C=O is polarised, with a partial positive carbon and a partial negative oxygen. Everything a carbonyl does follows from that: nucleophiles attack the carbon, acids protonate the oxygen, and the hydrogens on the neighbouring alpha carbon become acidic.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Aldehyde: at least one H on the carbonyl carbon (RCHO). Ketone: two carbon groups (RCOR).`,`Aldehydes are more reactive than ketones towards nucleophiles, for two reasons — less steric hindrance and less +I electron donation into the carbonyl.`,`Reactivity order: HCHO > CH3CHO > CH3COCH3 > C6H5CHO > C6H5COCH3 > C6H5COC6H5.`,`Alpha hydrogen: the H on the carbon next to C=O. Its pKa is about 20 for a ketone and about 25 for an ester, far more acidic than an alkane, because the anion (enolate) is delocalised onto oxygen.`,`Enol: the tautomer with C=C-OH. Keto and enol interconvert; the keto form dominates for simple carbonyls.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Nucleophilic addition products`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Reagent`,`Product`,`Use`],rows:[[`HCN`,`Cyanohydrin`,`Chain extension to alpha-hydroxy acid`],[`NaHSO3`,`Bisulfite addition compound`,`Purification of aldehydes and methyl ketones`],[`RMgX then H3O+`,`Alcohol`,`1 degree from HCHO, 2 degree from RCHO, 3 degree from ketone`],[`NH2OH`,`Oxime`,`Beckmann substrate, derivative for identification`],[`NH2NH2`,`Hydrazone`,`Wolff-Kishner substrate`],[`2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazine`,`Orange-red 2,4-DNP`,`Classic test for C=O`],[`Alcohol / dry HCl`,`Acetal or ketal`,`Protecting group`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Distinguishing aldehyde from ketone`,text:`Tollens reagent (ammoniacal silver nitrate) gives a silver mirror with aldehydes only. Fehling and Benedict solutions give a red Cu2O precipitate with aliphatic aldehydes but not with aromatic ones — benzaldehyde is Tollens positive and Fehling negative, which is a favourite question.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Iodoform test`,text:`I2 with NaOH gives a yellow precipitate of CHI3 with acetaldehyde, any methyl ketone (CH3CO-), ethanol and any CH3CH(OH)- alcohol. Acetone is positive, but propanal and benzophenone are negative.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Carboxylic acids are stronger acids than alcohols or phenols because the carboxylate anion is stabilised by two equivalent resonance structures with equal C-O bond lengths. Their derivatives — acid chloride, anhydride, ester, amide — all react by nucleophilic acyl substitution: addition of the nucleophile, then loss of the leaving group.`}]},{topicId:`oc-carbonyl`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The exam core is the family of enolate condensations. All of them are the same idea: make an enolate, add it to an electrophilic carbon, then lose water or an alkoxide.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Reaction`,`Substrates`,`Base or catalyst`,`Product`],rows:[[`Aldol`,`Two aldehydes or ketones with alpha H`,`Dilute NaOH`,`Beta-hydroxy carbonyl, then enone on heating`],[`Cannizzaro`,`Aldehyde with NO alpha H`,`Conc NaOH (50 percent)`,`Alcohol plus carboxylate salt`],[`Claisen condensation`,`Two esters with alpha H`,`Sodium ethoxide`,`Beta-keto ester`],[`Dieckmann`,`Diester, intramolecular`,`Sodium ethoxide`,`Cyclic beta-keto ester`],[`Perkin`,`Aromatic aldehyde + acid anhydride`,`Sodium salt of the acid`,`Alpha,beta-unsaturated aromatic acid`],[`Knoevenagel`,`Aldehyde + active methylene`,`Piperidine or pyridine`,`Alkene (Doebner gives acid)`],[`Michael`,`Enolate + alpha,beta-unsaturated carbonyl`,`NaOEt or amine`,`1,5-dicarbonyl (1,4-addition)`],[`Mannich`,`Ketone + HCHO + secondary amine`,`HCl`,`Beta-aminoketone (Mannich base)`],[`Wittig`,`Carbonyl + phosphorus ylide`,`n-BuLi to make the ylide`,`Alkene plus Ph3P=O`],[`Benzoin condensation`,`Two aromatic aldehydes`,`Cyanide ion or thiamine`,`Alpha-hydroxy ketone`]],caption:`Cannizzaro is the exception in this table: it needs the ABSENCE of an alpha hydrogen.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Cannizzaro detail`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`It is a disproportionation: one molecule is oxidised to the acid salt, the other reduced to the alcohol.`,`Formaldehyde, benzaldehyde, furfural and trimethylacetaldehyde all undergo it.`,`Crossed Cannizzaro with excess HCHO: formaldehyde is always the one oxidised, so the other aldehyde becomes the alcohol.`,`Intramolecular Cannizzaro of glyoxal gives glycolic acid.`,`The hydride is transferred directly; a deuterium label on the aldehyde carbon ends up on the alcohol carbon, not in the solvent.`,`Rate = k[RCHO]^2[OH-], i.e. third order overall.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Grignard reagent`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`RMgX made from R-X plus Mg in dry ether; it is destroyed by any acidic hydrogen (water, alcohols, amines, terminal alkynes, acids).`,`Formaldehyde gives a primary alcohol, any other aldehyde a secondary alcohol, a ketone a tertiary alcohol.`,`CO2 gives a carboxylic acid with one extra carbon; an ester or acid chloride gives a tertiary alcohol with two R groups added; a nitrile gives a ketone after hydrolysis.`,`An epoxide gives an alcohol two carbons longer, by attack at the less hindered carbon.`,`Organolithiums are more reactive than Grignards; Gilman cuprates (R2CuLi) give 1,4-addition to enones.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Wittig stereochemistry`,text:`Unstabilised (highly reactive) ylides give mainly the cis or Z alkene; stabilised ylides carrying an ester or ketone give mainly the trans or E alkene. The Schlosser modification gives E from an unstabilised ylide, and Horner-Wadsworth-Emmons phosphonates give E alkenes with a water-soluble by-product.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Aldol versus Claisen`,text:`Aldol keeps the C-O bond and gives an alcohol at the beta carbon; Claisen expels an alkoxide and gives a ketone. Aldol needs only catalytic base; Claisen needs a full equivalent, because the driving force is deprotonation of the acidic beta-keto ester product (pKa about 11).`}]},{topicId:`oc-carbonyl`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The advanced layer is enol content, hydrate equilibria, kinetic versus thermodynamic enolates and the acyl derivative reactivity ladder.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Compound`,`Enol content`,`Why`],rows:[[`Acetone`,`0.00025 percent`,`No stabilisation of the enol`],[`Ethyl acetoacetate`,`about 8 percent`,`Conjugation plus internal H bond`],[`Acetylacetone (pentane-2,4-dione)`,`about 80 percent`,`Strong six-membered internal H bond`],[`Phenol`,`100 percent enol`,`Enol form is aromatic`],[`Cyclohexane-1,3-dione`,`High`,`Conjugated enol`]],caption:`Enol content rises with conjugation, internal hydrogen bonding and aromaticity of the enol; non-polar solvents raise it further.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Kinetic enolate: LDA at -78 degrees C, deprotonates the less hindered alpha carbon, gives the less substituted enolate.`,`Thermodynamic enolate: NaOEt at room temperature under equilibrating conditions, gives the more substituted, more stable enolate.`,`Acyl reactivity ladder towards nucleophiles: acid chloride > anhydride > ester > carboxylic acid > amide > carboxylate. The better the leaving group, the more reactive the derivative.`,`Amide is least reactive because the nitrogen lone pair donates strongly into the carbonyl; this also gives the amide bond its partial double-bond character and restricted rotation.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Khydrate = [gem-diol] / [carbonyl]`,caption:`Formaldehyde is over 99 percent hydrated in water, acetaldehyde about 50 percent, acetone under 1 percent, and chloral is fully hydrated (chloral hydrate is an isolable solid) because three -I chlorines destabilise the carbonyl.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Acetoacetic ester synthesis gives methyl ketones after alkylation and ketonic hydrolysis (dilute base); acidic hydrolysis with concentrated base instead cleaves it to two acid fragments.`,`Malonic ester synthesis gives substituted acetic acids after alkylation, hydrolysis and decarboxylation at 150 degrees C.`,`Beta-keto acids and malonic acids decarboxylate readily through a six-membered cyclic transition state; simple carboxylic acids do not.`,`Haloform reaction: base-mediated trihalogenation of a methyl ketone, then cleavage by hydroxide to the carboxylate plus CHX3. Under acid, monohalogenation stops after one step.`,`HVZ reaction (Hell-Volhard-Zelinsky) alpha-brominates a carboxylic acid using Br2 with red phosphorus, via the acid bromide enol.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Aldol variants worth knowing`,text:`Intramolecular aldol of a 1,5-diketone gives a cyclohexenone; the Robinson annulation is a Michael addition followed by intramolecular aldol condensation, and it is the standard route to steroid ring systems.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Which reduction reaches which oxidation level`,text:`LiAlH4 takes an ester or acid all the way to the primary alcohol. DIBAL-H at -78 degrees C stops an ester or nitrile at the aldehyde. Rosenmund reduction (H2, Pd-BaSO4 poisoned with quinoline-S) stops an acid chloride at the aldehyde.`}]},{topicId:`oc-name-reactions`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Named reactions are free marks: the question gives you a name and asks for the product, the reagent or the intermediate. Learn each one as a triplet — substrate, reagent, product — and the intermediate where there is a famous one.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Reactions on the benzene ring`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Name`,`Substrate`,`Reagent`,`Product`],rows:[[`Sandmeyer`,`Aryl diazonium salt`,`CuCl, CuBr or CuCN`,`ArCl, ArBr or ArCN`],[`Gattermann (halide)`,`Aryl diazonium salt`,`Cu powder + HX`,`ArX`],[`Balz-Schiemann`,`Aryl diazonium salt`,`HBF4, then heat`,`ArF`],[`Reimer-Tiemann`,`Phenol`,`CHCl3 + NaOH`,`Salicylaldehyde (ortho)`],[`Kolbe-Schmitt`,`Sodium phenoxide`,`CO2, 125 degrees C, 4-7 atm`,`Salicylic acid`],[`Friedel-Crafts alkylation`,`Benzene`,`RX / anhydrous AlCl3`,`Alkylbenzene`],[`Friedel-Crafts acylation`,`Benzene`,`RCOCl / AlCl3`,`Aryl ketone`],[`Vilsmeier-Haack`,`Activated arene`,`DMF + POCl3`,`Aryl aldehyde`],[`Birch reduction`,`Benzene`,`Na or Li in liquid NH3 + EtOH`,`1,4-cyclohexadiene`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Making and shortening amines`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Name`,`Substrate`,`Reagent`,`Product`],rows:[[`Gabriel phthalimide`,`Alkyl halide`,`Potassium phthalimide, then hydrolysis`,`Pure primary amine`],[`Hofmann bromamide`,`Primary amide RCONH2`,`Br2 + NaOH`,`Amine RNH2, one carbon shorter`],[`Curtius`,`Acyl azide RCON3`,`Heat, then water`,`Amine RNH2, one carbon shorter`],[`Schmidt`,`Carboxylic acid`,`HN3 / H2SO4`,`Amine RNH2, one carbon shorter`],[`Lossen`,`Hydroxamic acid derivative`,`Base or heat`,`Amine, one carbon shorter`]],caption:`Hofmann, Curtius, Schmidt and Lossen all lose one carbon as CO2 and all go through an isocyanate.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Two ways to strip a carbonyl to CH2`,text:`Clemmensen is acidic (Zn-Hg amalgam with conc HCl); Wolff-Kishner is basic (NH2NH2 with KOH in ethylene glycol, Huang-Minlon modification). Use Clemmensen when the molecule has base-sensitive groups and Wolff-Kishner when it has acid-sensitive groups.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Gabriel synthesis limitation`,text:`It gives only primary amines and only from primary or secondary alkyl halides — not from aryl halides (no SN2) and not from tertiary halides (elimination instead).`}]},{topicId:`oc-name-reactions`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`At this level you need the intermediate of each reaction, because half the questions ask for it rather than the product.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Reaction`,`Key intermediate`,`Comment`],rows:[[`Reimer-Tiemann`,`Dichlorocarbene :CCl2`,`Generated from CHCl3 and OH-`],[`Sandmeyer`,`Aryl radical, Cu(I) redox cycle`,`Radical-nucleophilic mechanism`],[`Hofmann bromamide`,`Nitrene then isocyanate`,`N migration, retention of configuration`],[`Curtius`,`Acyl nitrene then isocyanate`,`Thermal, no base needed`],[`Wolff-Kishner`,`Hydrazone then diimide-type carbanion`,`Loses N2`],[`Clemmensen`,`Zinc carbenoid on the metal surface`,`No free alcohol intermediate`],[`Birch`,`Radical anion of the arene`,`Ammonia solvated electrons`],[`Friedel-Crafts acylation`,`Acylium ion RCO+`,`Resonance stabilised, no rearrangement`],[`Vilsmeier-Haack`,`Chloroiminium ion`,`Hydrolysed to CHO on workup`],[`Benzoin condensation`,`Cyanide-stabilised acyl anion`,`Umpolung, thiamine does it in vivo`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Reduction reactions worth separating`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Name`,`Converts`,`Reagent`],rows:[[`Clemmensen`,`C=O to CH2`,`Zn-Hg / conc HCl`],[`Wolff-Kishner`,`C=O to CH2`,`NH2NH2 / KOH, glycol, heat`],[`Mozingo (thioacetal)`,`C=O to CH2`,`Dithiol then Raney nickel`],[`Rosenmund`,`RCOCl to RCHO`,`H2 / Pd-BaSO4 + quinoline`],[`Stephen`,`RCN to RCHO`,`SnCl2 / HCl then H3O+`],[`Meerwein-Ponndorf-Verley`,`Ketone to alcohol`,`Aluminium isopropoxide`],[`Oppenauer`,`Alcohol to ketone`,`Aluminium tert-butoxide + acetone`],[`Bouveault-Blanc`,`Ester to alcohol`,`Na in ethanol`]],caption:`MPV and Oppenauer are the forward and reverse of the same hydride-transfer equilibrium.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Diazonium chemistry`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Diazotisation: primary aromatic amine plus NaNO2 and HCl at 0-5 degrees C. Above 5 degrees C the salt decomposes to phenol.`,`Replacement by OH (warm water), H (hypophosphorous acid or ethanol), I (KI, no catalyst needed), NO2 (NaNO2/Cu).`,`Coupling with phenol at pH 9-10 or with an aromatic amine at pH 4-5 gives an azo dye; coupling occurs para to the activating group.`,`Reduction with SnCl2/HCl or Na2SO3 gives the arylhydrazine, the starting material for Fischer indole synthesis.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Hofmann has three meanings`,text:`Hofmann bromamide degradation (amide to amine), Hofmann elimination rule (less substituted alkene), and Hofmann exhaustive methylation (making the quaternary ammonium hydroxide). Read the stem carefully before answering.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Carbylamine test`,text:`A primary amine plus CHCl3 and alcoholic KOH gives an isocyanide with a foul smell. It is specific for primary amines, aliphatic or aromatic, and it also goes through dichlorocarbene.`}]},{topicId:`oc-name-reactions`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced questions ask about stereochemistry, selectivity and the modern coupling reactions that now appear in GPAT medicinal-chemistry style stems.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Hofmann, Curtius, Schmidt and Lossen all proceed with complete retention of configuration at the migrating carbon, because migration and departure of the leaving group are concerted.`,`The migration in these degradations is intramolecular; crossover experiments show no scrambling between two labelled substrates.`,`Birch reduction regiochemistry: an electron-donating substituent (OMe, alkyl) stays on an sp2 carbon of the product, so you get the 1-substituted-2,5-cyclohexadiene; an electron-withdrawing substituent (COOH) ends on the sp3 carbon.`,`Sandmeyer with CuCN also works on rings that would refuse Friedel-Crafts, making it the standard route to hindered or deactivated aryl nitriles.`,`Reimer-Tiemann on p-cresol gives a cyclohexadienone by attack at the blocked para position — the abnormal Reimer-Tiemann product.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Coupling reaction`,`Partners`,`Catalyst`],rows:[[`Suzuki`,`Aryl halide + boronic acid`,`Pd(0) with base`],[`Heck`,`Aryl halide + alkene`,`Pd(0) with base`],[`Sonogashira`,`Aryl halide + terminal alkyne`,`Pd(0) + Cu(I)`],[`Stille`,`Aryl halide + organostannane`,`Pd(0)`],[`Buchwald-Hartwig`,`Aryl halide + amine`,`Pd(0) with ligand and base`],[`Ullmann`,`Two aryl halides`,`Cu, high temperature`],[`Wurtz-Fittig`,`Aryl halide + alkyl halide`,`Na in dry ether`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Nobel-flagged reactions`,text:`Wittig (1979), Sharpless asymmetric epoxidation and Noyori hydrogenation (2001), Grubbs olefin metathesis (2005) and the Heck-Negishi-Suzuki palladium couplings (2010) are the ones GPAT quotes when it wants a recognition question.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Sharpless epoxidation needs an allylic alcohol, Ti(OiPr)4, tert-butyl hydroperoxide and diethyl tartrate; (+)-DET and (-)-DET give opposite epoxide faces.`,`Baeyer-Villiger with a peracid converts a ketone to an ester with retention at the migrating carbon.`,`Swern oxidation (oxalyl chloride, DMSO, triethylamine at -78 degrees C) gives aldehydes without over-oxidation and without chromium.`,`Mitsunobu (PPh3 + DEAD) converts an alcohol to an ester, ether or amine with clean inversion of configuration.`,`Wolff rearrangement of a diazoketone (Arndt-Eistert with silver oxide) extends a carboxylic acid chain by one carbon.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Two carbon-count changes`,text:`Arndt-Eistert adds one carbon to an acid; Hofmann, Curtius and Schmidt remove one. If a stem shows a product with one fewer carbon and a nitrogen, the answer is almost always one of the three degradations.`}]},{topicId:`oc-rearrangements`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`A molecular rearrangement moves a group from one atom to a neighbouring atom, changing the carbon skeleton. Every rearrangement question is answered with three facts: what migrates, where it migrates to, and what happens to the stereochemistry.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Migration to an electron-deficient carbon: Pinacol-pinacolone, Wagner-Meerwein, Wolff.`,`Migration to an electron-deficient nitrogen: Beckmann, Hofmann, Curtius, Schmidt, Lossen.`,`Migration to an electron-deficient oxygen: Baeyer-Villiger, Dakin, Hock (cumene process).`,`Migration to an electron-rich carbon: Favorskii, Stevens, Wittig (1,2 rearrangement).`,`Pericyclic (no charged intermediate): Claisen and Cope sigmatropic shifts.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Rearrangement`,`Starting material`,`Product`],rows:[[`Pinacol-pinacolone`,`1,2-diol (pinacol) with acid`,`Ketone (pinacolone)`],[`Beckmann`,`Ketoxime with acid`,`Amide (or lactam)`],[`Baeyer-Villiger`,`Ketone with peracid`,`Ester (cyclic ketone gives lactone)`],[`Hofmann`,`Primary amide with Br2/NaOH`,`Amine with one carbon less`],[`Curtius`,`Acyl azide, heat`,`Amine or isocyanate`],[`Claisen`,`Allyl aryl ether, 200 degrees C`,`ortho-allyl phenol`],[`Fries`,`Phenyl ester with AlCl3`,`ortho or para hydroxy aryl ketone`],[`Favorskii`,`Alpha-halo ketone with alkoxide`,`Ester (ring contracted)`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Which way does the group jump?`,text:`Electrons flow towards the electron-poor atom, so the group with the most available electrons migrates first and it always takes its bonding pair with it.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Beckmann is the most asked of all`,text:`Cyclohexanone oxime with concentrated H2SO4 gives caprolactam, the monomer of nylon-6. That single conversion has appeared repeatedly.`},{kind:`para`,text:`The Fries rearrangement is temperature controlled: low temperature (below about 60 degrees C) with more solvent favours the para product, high temperature (above about 160 degrees C) favours the ortho product, which is steam volatile and easily separated.`}]},{topicId:`oc-rearrangements`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Learn the migrating group and stereochemical outcome for each rearrangement as one table — that is exactly how the question is framed.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Rearrangement`,`Migrating group`,`Migration terminus`,`Stereochemistry`],rows:[[`Pinacol-pinacolone`,`Group anti-periplanar to leaving OH2+`,`Electron-poor carbon`,`Retention in migrating group`],[`Wagner-Meerwein`,`Alkyl or hydride`,`Carbocation carbon`,`Retention in migrating group`],[`Beckmann`,`Group ANTI to the oxime OH`,`Nitrogen`,`Stereospecific, retention in migrant`],[`Baeyer-Villiger`,`More substituted alkyl group`,`Oxygen`,`Retention at migrating carbon`],[`Hofmann`,`R of RCONH2`,`Nitrogen`,`Retention, intramolecular`],[`Curtius`,`R of RCON3`,`Nitrogen`,`Retention, intramolecular`],[`Schmidt`,`R of RCOOH`,`Nitrogen`,`Retention`],[`Favorskii`,`Carbanion carbon`,`Carbon bearing halogen`,`Via cyclopropanone, loses stereo detail`],[`Claisen (aromatic)`,`Allyl group`,`ortho carbon`,`Inversion of the allyl unit`],[`Fries`,`Acyl group`,`ortho or para carbon`,`Intramolecular and intermolecular paths`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Migratory aptitude`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Pinacol and similar cationic rearrangements: aryl (especially p-methoxyphenyl) > vinyl > hydride > tertiary alkyl > secondary alkyl > primary alkyl > methyl.`,`Baeyer-Villiger: tertiary alkyl > cyclohexyl and secondary alkyl and benzyl and phenyl > primary alkyl > methyl. So acetophenone gives phenyl acetate, not methyl benzoate.`,`Electron-rich aryl groups migrate faster because they stabilise the positive charge that develops in the bridged transition state.`,`In an unsymmetrical pinacol, the more stable carbocation forms first, then the anti group on the other carbon migrates to it.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Beckmann is anti-specific`,text:`It is the group anti (trans) to the oxime hydroxyl that migrates, so the geometry of the oxime fixes the amide you get. Acetophenone oxime whose phenyl is anti to OH gives acetanilide (PhNHCOCH3); the syn isomer would give N-methylbenzamide.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Baeyer-Villiger detail`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Reagents: mCPBA, peracetic acid, trifluoroperacetic acid, or CF3CO3H for hindered ketones.`,`The Criegee intermediate is a peroxy hemiketal; migration and O-O cleavage are concerted.`,`Cyclohexanone gives epsilon-caprolactone; cyclopentanone gives delta-valerolactone.`,`Aldehydes are oxidised to carboxylic acids under the same conditions, because hydride migrates.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Claisen rearrangement is not the Claisen condensation`,text:`The Claisen condensation joins two esters with sodium ethoxide to give a beta-keto ester. The Claisen rearrangement is a thermal [3,3] sigmatropic shift of an allyl vinyl or allyl aryl ether. Same man, different reaction.`}]},{topicId:`oc-rearrangements`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The advanced layer is evidence for mechanism, pericyclic selection rules and the less common rearrangements that still appear.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Claisen rearrangement is a concerted, suprafacial-suprafacial [3,3] shift through a six-membered chair-like transition state, thermally allowed under the Woodward-Hoffmann rules.`,`The aromatic Claisen product tautomerises back to the phenol, which is the driving force; when both ortho positions are blocked, a second [3,3] shift gives the para-allyl phenol (para-Claisen).`,`Cope rearrangement is the all-carbon [3,3] analogue; the oxy-Cope with an alkoxide at C3 is accelerated by up to 10^17 times.`,`[1,5] hydride shifts are thermally allowed and suprafacial; [1,3] shifts are thermally forbidden suprafacially.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Rearrangement`,`Electron demand at terminus`,`Signature product`],rows:[[`Dakin`,`Electron-poor oxygen`,`ortho or para hydroxy aldehyde gives a catechol or quinol`],[`Hock (cumene process)`,`Electron-poor oxygen`,`Phenol plus acetone`],[`Stevens`,`Electron-rich carbon (ylide)`,`Rearranged tertiary amine`],[`Sommelet-Hauser`,`Electron-rich carbon`,`ortho-methylated benzyl amine`],[`Benzilic acid`,`Carbanion to carbonyl`,`Benzil gives benzilic acid with KOH`],[`Neber`,`Nitrogen, via azirine`,`Alpha-amino ketone from oxime tosylate`],[`Wolff`,`Electron-poor carbene carbon`,`Ketene, then acid one carbon longer`]]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Semipinacol: 1,2-amino alcohol + HNO2 gives ring-contracted or ring-expanded ketone`,caption:`The Tiffeneau-Demjanov variant: 1-aminomethylcyclohexanol with nitrous acid expands the ring to cycloheptanone. The diazonium is the leaving group.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Favorskii goes through a cyclopropanone (or an oxyallyl cation for hindered cases); alpha,alpha-dihaloketones give alpha,beta-unsaturated acids, and a ring-contracted product is the fingerprint from a cyclic halo ketone.`,`2-chlorocyclohexanone with sodium methoxide gives methyl cyclopentanecarboxylate — ring contraction by one carbon.`,`Retro-pinacol and Wagner-Meerwein shifts explain terpene skeletal rearrangements, e.g. camphene hydrochloride from isobornyl chloride.`,`Crossover experiments and 18O or 14C labelling are the standard proofs that a rearrangement is intramolecular.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Beckmann versus Schmidt on a ketone`,text:`Both convert a ketone to an amide. Beckmann needs the oxime made first; the Schmidt reaction with hydrazoic acid does it in one pot. On a carboxylic acid, Schmidt gives an amine instead.`}]},{topicId:`oc-heterocycles`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`A heterocycle is a ring containing at least one atom other than carbon, usually N, O or S. Most drugs contain one, so GPAT asks about their aromaticity, their reactivity towards electrophiles, and how they are made.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The five-membered trio`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Pyrrole (NH), furan (O) and thiophene (S) each have four pi electrons from two double bonds plus a lone pair donated by the heteroatom, giving an aromatic sextet.`,`Because the heteroatom pushes electrons into the ring, all three are pi-excessive and far more reactive than benzene towards electrophiles.`,`They substitute at C2 (the alpha position), because the sigma complex from alpha attack has three resonance structures against two for beta attack.`,`Pyridine has its nitrogen lone pair in an sp2 orbital in the ring plane, not in the pi system, so pyridine is pi-deficient and behaves like nitrobenzene.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Ring`,`Heteroatom`,`Aromatic?`,`Electrophilic reactivity`],rows:[[`Pyrrole`,`N-H`,`Yes, 6 pi`,`Very high, about 10^11 times benzene`],[`Furan`,`O`,`Yes, 6 pi`,`High, most diene-like`],[`Thiophene`,`S`,`Yes, 6 pi`,`High, most benzene-like of the three`],[`Pyridine`,`N (sp2 lone pair out of ring)`,`Yes, 6 pi`,`Low, meta (C3) directing`],[`Benzene`,`None`,`Yes, 6 pi`,`Reference`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Two orders you must not mix up`,text:`Reactivity towards electrophiles: pyrrole > furan > thiophene > benzene > pyridine. Aromatic character (resonance energy): benzene > thiophene > pyrrole > furan. Furan is the most reactive-looking but the least aromatic.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Pyrrole is not basic — it is acidic`,text:`Pyrrole nitrogen has committed its lone pair to the aromatic sextet, so protonating it destroys aromaticity. Its conjugate acid has a pKa of about -3.8, and the N-H itself is weakly acidic (pKa about 17.5), giving a potassium salt with KOH. Pyridine, with a free lone pair, has a conjugate acid pKa of 5.2.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Indole is a benzene ring fused to pyrrole. Its pyrrole-type nitrogen means it too is pi-excessive, but electrophiles attack at C3 (the beta position of the pyrrole ring), because attack there keeps the benzene ring aromatic in the intermediate. Indole is the core of tryptophan, serotonin, indomethacin and the triptans.`}]},{topicId:`oc-heterocycles`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The exam layer is the named syntheses, the position of attack for each ring, and basicity ordering across nitrogen heterocycles.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Synthesis`,`Starting materials`,`Product ring`],rows:[[`Paal-Knorr`,`1,4-dicarbonyl + NH3 / P2O5 / P2S5`,`Pyrrole / furan / thiophene`],[`Knorr pyrrole`,`Alpha-aminoketone + beta-ketoester`,`Pyrrole`],[`Hantzsch pyrrole`,`Alpha-haloketone + beta-ketoester + NH3`,`Pyrrole`],[`Hantzsch dihydropyridine`,`Aldehyde + 2 beta-ketoester + NH3`,`1,4-dihydropyridine (nifedipine type)`],[`Skraup`,`Aniline + glycerol + H2SO4 + nitrobenzene`,`Quinoline`],[`Doebner-Miller`,`Aniline + alpha,beta-unsaturated carbonyl`,`Substituted quinoline`],[`Friedlander`,`o-aminoaryl aldehyde + ketone`,`Quinoline`],[`Bischler-Napieralski`,`Beta-arylethylamide + POCl3`,`3,4-dihydroisoquinoline`],[`Pictet-Spengler`,`Beta-arylethylamine + aldehyde`,`Tetrahydroisoquinoline`],[`Fischer indole`,`Arylhydrazone + ZnCl2 or acid`,`Indole`],[`Madelung`,`o-toluidide + strong base, heat`,`Indole`],[`Chichibabin (pyridine)`,`Pyridine + NaNH2`,`2-aminopyridine`]],caption:`Skraup uses nitrobenzene as the oxidant and is famously violent; ferrous sulfate is added to moderate it.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Fischer indole synthesis goes through an ene-hydrazine, then a [3,3] sigmatropic shift, then loss of ammonia. Phenylhydrazine plus a ketone with an alpha CH2 gives the indole.`,`Chichibabin amination is a nucleophilic addition-elimination on pyridine that expels hydride as H2; it needs no activating group.`,`Pyridine N-oxide (made with H2O2 or a peracid) reverses reactivity — it nitrates at C4 and also allows nucleophilic attack there.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Position of attack`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Ring`,`Electrophile attacks`,`Nucleophile attacks`],rows:[[`Pyrrole, furan, thiophene`,`C2 (alpha)`,`Not applicable, ring too electron rich`],[`Pyridine`,`C3 (beta), harsh conditions`,`C2 and C4`],[`Quinoline`,`C5 and C8 (benzene ring)`,`C2`],[`Isoquinoline`,`C5 and C8`,`C1`],[`Indole`,`C3`,`Not typical`],[`Imidazole`,`C4 or C5`,`C2 (after deprotonation)`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Basicity ordering of azoles`,text:`Imidazole (conjugate acid pKa 7.0) is more basic than pyridine (5.2), which is far more basic than thiazole (2.5), oxazole (0.8) and pyrrole (-3.8). The imidazolium cation is stabilised by two equivalent resonance forms — this is why histidine buffers at physiological pH.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Pyrrole and acid`,text:`Pyrrole polymerises in strong mineral acid to pyrrole red, so nitration and sulfonation must use mild reagents — acetyl nitrate for nitration and the pyridine-SO3 complex for sulfonation. Furan behaves similarly and undergoes Diels-Alder as a diene, which thiophene and pyrrole normally do not.`}]},{topicId:`oc-heterocycles`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced material covers fused and six-membered systems with two heteroatoms, tautomerism, and the drug scaffolds built on each ring.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Ring system`,`Description`,`Drug or natural example`],rows:[[`Pyrimidine`,`Two N at 1,3`,`Thymine, cytosine, trimethoprim, phenobarbitone`],[`Purine`,`Pyrimidine fused to imidazole`,`Adenine, guanine, caffeine, allopurinol`],[`Pteridine`,`Pyrimidine fused to pyrazine`,`Folic acid, methotrexate`],[`Quinoline`,`Benzene fused to pyridine, N at 1`,`Quinine, chloroquine, ciprofloxacin core`],[`Isoquinoline`,`Fused, N at 2`,`Papaverine, morphine skeleton`],[`Indole`,`Benzene fused to pyrrole`,`Serotonin, reserpine, indomethacin`],[`Phenothiazine`,`Two benzene rings on N and S`,`Chlorpromazine, promethazine`],[`Benzodiazepine`,`Benzene fused to 7-membered diazepine`,`Diazepam, alprazolam`],[`Imidazole`,`Two N at 1,3, five-membered`,`Histamine, metronidazole, ketoconazole`],[`Thiazole`,`N and S at 1,3`,`Thiamine, penicillin nucleus`],[`Tropane`,`Bicyclic N-bridged`,`Atropine, cocaine`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Annular tautomerism: imidazole N1-H and N3-H forms interconvert rapidly, so 4- and 5-substituted imidazoles are the same compound at room temperature.`,`2- and 4-hydroxypyridine exist almost entirely as the pyridone (amide) tautomer; the same lactam preference explains why uracil and thymine are drawn as diketo forms.`,`Pyrimidine is less basic than pyridine (pKa 1.3) because the second nitrogen is -I; purine is amphoteric.`,`Piperidine (pKa 11.1) and pyrrolidine (11.3) are saturated, so their nitrogen is a normal sp3 amine and strongly basic; morpholine (8.4) is weaker because of the ring oxygen.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Resonance energy (kJ/mol): benzene 150, thiophene 122, pyrrole 90, furan 67`,caption:`Explains the aromaticity ordering. Furan is closest to a simple diene and is the only one of the three that readily does Diels-Alder chemistry.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Reactions that fingerprint a ring`,text:`Pyrrole gives a red colour with a pine splint moistened with HCl. Indole gives the Ehrlich reaction (violet with p-dimethylaminobenzaldehyde) and the Van Urk reaction is used for ergot alkaloids. Pyridine gives no such colour but is recognised by its foul smell and its use as a base and catalyst.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Which nitrogen gets alkylated`,text:`In a ring with both a pyrrole-type and a pyridine-type nitrogen (imidazole, purine, benzimidazole), the electrophile always goes to the pyridine-type nitrogen, because only that one has an available lone pair.`}]},{topicId:`oc-protection-reagents`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`A synthesis often has to touch one group while leaving another alone. Two tools make that possible: selective reagents that attack only certain groups, and protecting groups that temporarily mask a group you want untouched.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The two common hydride reducers`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Functional group`,`LiAlH4`,`NaBH4`],rows:[[`Aldehyde`,`Reduced to 1 degree alcohol`,`Reduced to 1 degree alcohol`],[`Ketone`,`Reduced to 2 degree alcohol`,`Reduced to 2 degree alcohol`],[`Ester`,`Reduced to 1 degree alcohol`,`No reaction`],[`Carboxylic acid`,`Reduced to 1 degree alcohol`,`No reaction`],[`Acid chloride`,`Reduced to 1 degree alcohol`,`Reduced`],[`Amide`,`Reduced to amine`,`No reaction`],[`Nitrile`,`Reduced to primary amine`,`No reaction`],[`Nitro (aliphatic)`,`Reduced to amine`,`No reaction`],[`Alkene, alkyne`,`No reaction`,`No reaction`],[`Solvent`,`Dry ether or THF only`,`Water or alcohol acceptable`]],caption:`One line to remember: LiAlH4 reduces almost everything with a C=O or C=N; NaBH4 reduces only aldehydes, ketones and acid chlorides.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Strength and safety`,text:`LiAlH4 is the bulldozer — powerful, reacts violently with water, needs dry ether. NaBH4 is the scalpel — mild, safe enough to use in methanol or water.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Why protect a group`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`A protecting group must go on easily, survive the reaction you plan, and come off cleanly without disturbing anything else.`,`Alcohols are protected as ethers (methyl, benzyl, tert-butyl, silyl) or as esters (acetate, benzoate).`,`Carbonyls are protected as acetals or ketals with ethylene glycol and acid.`,`Amines are protected as amides (acetyl, benzoyl) or as carbamates (Boc, Cbz, Fmoc).`,`Carboxylic acids are protected as simple esters (methyl, benzyl, tert-butyl).`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The textbook example`,text:`To make a Grignard from a bromo ketone, first protect the ketone as its ethylene ketal, then form the Grignard, then hydrolyse the ketal back with dilute acid. Without protection the Grignard destroys its own carbonyl.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Aniline is acetylated to acetanilide before nitration for the same reason: the amide is a weaker activator, so it stops the ring from being over-oxidised or over-substituted and it directs cleanly to para. Hydrolysis with acid or base then returns the free amine.`}]},{topicId:`oc-protection-reagents`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The exam level is a memory job: what each oxidant converts to what, and which protecting group comes off under which condition. Both are best learned as tables.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Oxidising agent`,`Converts`,`Notes`],rows:[[`PCC or PDC in CH2Cl2`,`1 degree alcohol to aldehyde`,`Stops at the aldehyde, anhydrous`],[`Jones reagent (CrO3/H2SO4)`,`1 degree alcohol to acid`,`Aqueous, over-oxidises`],[`Swern (DMSO, oxalyl chloride, Et3N)`,`1 degree alcohol to aldehyde`,`No metal, -78 degrees C`],[`Dess-Martin periodinane`,`1 degree alcohol to aldehyde`,`Mild, room temperature`],[`KMnO4 hot alkaline`,`Alkyl side chain to COOH`,`Also cleaves alkenes`],[`KMnO4 cold dilute (Baeyer)`,`Alkene to syn diol`,`Test for unsaturation`],[`OsO4 / NMO`,`Alkene to syn diol`,`Catalytic, expensive`],[`O3 then Zn/H2O`,`Alkene to two carbonyls`,`Reductive workup`],[`HIO4 (periodic acid)`,`1,2-diol to two carbonyls`,`Cleaves the C-C bond`],[`SeO2`,`Ketone to 1,2-diketone`,`Riley oxidation, allylic hydroxylation`],[`mCPBA`,`Alkene to epoxide, ketone to ester`,`Baeyer-Villiger on ketones`],[`MnO2`,`Allylic and benzylic alcohol to carbonyl`,`Selective, leaves saturated alcohols`],[`Tollens or Fehling`,`Aldehyde to acid`,`Diagnostic tests`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Protecting groups and their deprotection`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Group protected`,`Protecting group`,`Put on with`,`Removed by`],rows:[[`Alcohol`,`TMS ether`,`TMSCl + Et3N`,`Dilute acid or F- (TBAF)`],[`Alcohol`,`TBDMS ether`,`TBDMSCl + imidazole`,`TBAF, more robust than TMS`],[`Alcohol`,`Benzyl ether`,`BnBr + NaH`,`H2 / Pd-C (hydrogenolysis)`],[`Alcohol`,`THP ether`,`Dihydropyran + H+`,`Aqueous acid`],[`Alcohol`,`Acetate ester`,`Ac2O + pyridine`,`K2CO3 in methanol, or NaOH`],[`Diol`,`Acetonide`,`Acetone + acid`,`Aqueous acid`],[`Aldehyde/ketone`,`Cyclic acetal or ketal`,`Ethylene glycol + TsOH`,`Aqueous acid`],[`Aldehyde/ketone`,`Dithiane / thioacetal`,`1,3-propanedithiol + Lewis acid`,`HgCl2 or Raney Ni`],[`Amine`,`Boc (tert-butoxycarbonyl)`,`Boc2O`,`TFA or dilute HCl (acid)`],[`Amine`,`Cbz (benzyloxycarbonyl)`,`Cbz-Cl`,`H2 / Pd-C or HBr in acetic acid`],[`Amine`,`Fmoc`,`Fmoc-Cl`,`Piperidine (base) - orthogonal to Boc`],[`Amine`,`Acetyl`,`Acetic anhydride`,`Acid or base hydrolysis, harsh`],[`Carboxylic acid`,`Methyl or benzyl ester`,`MeOH/H+ or BnBr`,`NaOH, or H2/Pd-C for benzyl`]],caption:`Boc off with acid, Fmoc off with base, Cbz off with hydrogen — the orthogonality that makes solid-phase peptide synthesis possible.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Chemoselective reduction question`,text:`To reduce a ketone while leaving an ester untouched, use NaBH4. To reduce the ester and not the alkene, use LiAlH4. To reduce the alkene and not the carbonyl, use H2 with Pd-C. To reduce a nitro group with an ester present, use H2/Pd-C or Sn/HCl, not LiAlH4.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Acetal versus thioacetal`,text:`Acetals are stable to base and destroyed by aqueous acid. Thioacetals are stable to both acid and base and need mercury(II) or Raney nickel, which is why a dithiane doubles as an acyl anion equivalent (umpolung) and as the Mozingo route to CH2.`}]},{topicId:`oc-protection-reagents`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced questions test the modified hydrides, the selective catalysts and the reagents whose whole purpose is one narrow transformation.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Reagent`,`Specific job`,`Why it is chosen`],rows:[[`DIBAL-H at -78 degrees C`,`Ester or nitrile to aldehyde`,`Bulky, stops at the tetrahedral intermediate`],[`NaBH3CN at pH 5-6`,`Reductive amination`,`Reduces iminium but not the carbonyl`],[`NaBH(OAc)3`,`Reductive amination`,`Mild, tolerant of many groups`],[`L-selectride`,`Ketone to axial alcohol`,`Very bulky hydride`],[`LiAlH(OtBu)3`,`Acid chloride to aldehyde`,`Attenuated LiAlH4`],[`Lindlar catalyst`,`Alkyne to cis alkene`,`Pd-CaCO3 poisoned with quinoline`],[`Na in liquid NH3`,`Alkyne to trans alkene`,`Radical anion route`],[`Raney nickel`,`Desulfurisation, full reduction`,`Removes C-S bonds`],[`Rosenmund catalyst`,`Acid chloride to aldehyde`,`Pd-BaSO4 with quinoline-S`],[`SnCl2/HCl or Fe/HCl`,`Nitro to amine`,`Leaves other reducible groups`],[`Zn dust / NH4Cl`,`Nitro to hydroxylamine`,`Partial reduction, controlled`],[`Na2S or (NH4)2S`,`One nitro of a dinitro compound`,`Selective partial reduction`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Nitrobenzene reduction depends entirely on the medium: acidic (Sn/HCl) gives aniline, neutral (Zn/NH4Cl) gives phenylhydroxylamine, alkaline (Zn/NaOH) gives hydrazobenzene, and electrolytic reduction in strong acid gives p-aminophenol by the Bamberger rearrangement.`,`Phenylhydroxylamine rearranges to p-aminophenol in acid (Bamberger); hydrazobenzene rearranges to benzidine in acid.`,`Clemmensen and Wolff-Kishner are the two ways to remove a carbonyl completely; the Mozingo thioacetal route is the neutral third option.`,`Selective silyl deprotection: TMS is cleaved by mild acid or fluoride, TBDMS needs TBAF or stronger acid, TIPS and TBDPS are the most robust. That graded stability enables sequential deprotection.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Overall yield = product of individual step yields`,caption:`Two extra steps of protection and deprotection at 90 percent each cost about 19 percent of the overall yield, which is why chemoselective reagents are preferred over protecting groups whenever they exist.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Orthogonal protection in peptide synthesis`,text:`Merrifield solid-phase synthesis uses Boc chemistry (TFA deprotection, HF cleavage) or the now-standard Fmoc chemistry (piperidine deprotection, TFA cleavage). Side chains are protected with tert-butyl or trityl groups removed at the final TFA step.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Benzyl groups and hydrogenation`,text:`Any benzyl-based protecting group (Bn ether, Cbz carbamate, benzyl ester) comes off by catalytic hydrogenolysis. If your molecule also has an alkene or a nitro group, hydrogenation will reduce those too, so a different protecting group must be chosen.`}]}],t=[{id:`q-oc-bonding-effects-1`,topicId:`oc-bonding-effects`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which of the following groups shows a +I (electron-releasing inductive) effect?`,options:[`-NO2`,`-CN`,`-COOH`,`-C(CH3)3`],answer:3,explanation:`Alkyl groups release electron density through the sigma framework, and the order is tert-butyl > isopropyl > ethyl > methyl. Nitro, cyano and carboxyl are all electron-withdrawing (-I) because of their electronegative atoms and, for nitro and cyano, a strong -M effect as well.`},{id:`q-oc-bonding-effects-2`,topicId:`oc-bonding-effects`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Arrange in increasing order of acidity: acetic acid, chloroacetic acid, trichloroacetic acid, formic acid.`,options:[`Acetic < formic < chloroacetic < trichloroacetic`,`Trichloroacetic < chloroacetic < formic < acetic`,`Formic < acetic < chloroacetic < trichloroacetic`,`Acetic < chloroacetic < formic < trichloroacetic`],answer:0,explanation:`pKa values are acetic 4.76, formic 3.75, chloroacetic 2.86 and trichloroacetic 0.7, so acidity increases in that order. Acetic acid is the weakest because its methyl group is +I and destabilises the carboxylate; formic acid has no such group, and each added chlorine adds -I stabilisation of the anion.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-bonding-effects-3`,topicId:`oc-bonding-effects`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The stability order of carbocations tert-butyl > isopropyl > ethyl > methyl is best explained by the number of:`,options:[`Lone pairs available for donation`,`Alpha C-H bonds available for hyperconjugation`,`Resonance structures of the cation`,`Pi bonds conjugated with the empty orbital`],answer:1,explanation:`Tert-butyl, isopropyl, ethyl and methyl cations have 9, 6, 3 and 0 alpha hydrogens respectively, matching the stability order exactly through hyperconjugation (no-bond resonance). Resonance is the tempting distractor but simple alkyl cations have no pi system to delocalise into; only benzyl and allyl cations are resonance stabilised.`},{id:`q-oc-bonding-effects-4`,topicId:`oc-bonding-effects`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In aqueous solution, the basicity order of methylamines is:`,options:[`(CH3)3N > (CH3)2NH > CH3NH2 > NH3`,`CH3NH2 > (CH3)2NH > (CH3)3N > NH3`,`(CH3)2NH > CH3NH2 > (CH3)3N > NH3`,`NH3 > CH3NH2 > (CH3)2NH > (CH3)3N`],answer:2,explanation:`In water the observed order is secondary > primary > tertiary > ammonia, because the tertiary amine gains the most from +I but loses the most from steric hindrance to solvation and hydrogen bonding of its cation. The sequence tertiary > secondary > primary > ammonia is the gas-phase order, where solvation plays no part, and it is the classic distractor.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-bonding-effects-5`,topicId:`oc-bonding-effects`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which explains why chlorobenzene is less reactive than benzene towards electrophiles yet still gives mainly ortho and para products?`,options:[`Chlorine is +I but -M`,`Chlorine is both -I and -M`,`Chlorine is both +I and +M`,`Chlorine is -I but +M`],answer:3,explanation:`Chlorine withdraws electron density through the sigma bond (-I), which dominates the overall ring reactivity and deactivates it. Its lone pair can still be donated by resonance (+M) specifically to the ortho and para positions, so orientation stays ortho-para. Getting the two effects the wrong way round is the standard error.`},{id:`q-oc-bonding-effects-6`,topicId:`oc-bonding-effects`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which of these is the strongest neutral organic base?`,options:[`Aniline`,`Pyridine`,`Guanidine`,`Acetamide`],answer:2,explanation:`Guanidine has a conjugate acid pKa of about 13.6 because the guanidinium ion is stabilised by three equivalent resonance structures sharing the positive charge over three nitrogens. Pyridine (pKa 5.2) is basic but far weaker; aniline and acetamide are both very weak because their nitrogen lone pairs are delocalised away.`},{id:`q-oc-bonding-effects-7`,topicId:`oc-bonding-effects`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`o-toluic acid is a stronger acid than benzoic acid, even though the methyl group is electron-releasing. This is because:`,options:[`The methyl group is actually -I in the ortho position`,`Intramolecular hydrogen bonding stabilises the anion`,`The ortho substituent twists COOH out of the ring plane, removing its conjugation with the ring`,`The methyl group increases the hydration of the anion`],answer:2,explanation:`This is the ortho effect, a steric phenomenon. Any ortho substituent forces the carboxyl group out of coplanarity with the ring, so the electron-donating conjugation between ring and COOH is lost, and the carboxylate becomes easier to form. A methyl group does not become electron-withdrawing simply by moving to the ortho position, so any answer invoking a change in its inductive character is wrong.`,tag:`Ortho effect`},{id:`q-oc-bonding-effects-8`,topicId:`oc-bonding-effects`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In the Hammett equation log(k/k0) = rho x sigma, a large negative value of rho for a reaction series indicates that:`,options:[`Negative charge builds up at the reaction centre in the transition state`,`Positive charge builds up at the reaction centre in the transition state`,`The reaction is insensitive to substituents`,`The reaction proceeds through a free radical`],answer:1,explanation:`A negative rho means electron-donating substituents (negative sigma) increase the rate, which happens when positive charge develops at the reacting centre — as in SN1 solvolysis of substituted benzyl chlorides. A positive rho, as for benzoic acid ionisation (rho = 1 by definition), signals developing negative charge.`,tag:`Numerical concept`},{id:`q-oc-stereochem-1`,topicId:`oc-stereochem`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A compound with three dissimilar stereocentres and no internal symmetry will have how many stereoisomers?`,options:[`3`,`6`,`8`,`9`],answer:2,explanation:`The maximum number of stereoisomers is 2^n, so 2^3 = 8, arranged as four pairs of enantiomers. Six is the answer to 2n and nine to n squared plus n — both common arithmetic slips. If an internal mirror plane were possible the real total would be lower because of meso forms.`},{id:`q-oc-stereochem-2`,topicId:`oc-stereochem`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A meso compound is best described as one that:`,options:[`Contains stereocentres but is achiral because of an internal plane of symmetry`,`Is an equimolar mixture of two enantiomers`,`Contains no stereocentres and rotates light`,`Is a pair of diastereomers that cannot be separated`],answer:0,explanation:`A meso compound has stereocentres but also an internal mirror plane, so the two halves cancel and the molecule itself is achiral and optically inactive by internal compensation. An equimolar mixture of two enantiomers is a racemate, which is inactive by external compensation and can, unlike a meso compound, be resolved into optically active halves.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-stereochem-3`,topicId:`oc-stereochem`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A 1 dm polarimeter tube containing a solution of 0.10 g/mL shows an observed rotation of +6.6 degrees. The specific rotation is:`,options:[`+0.66 degrees`,`+6.6 degrees`,`+660 degrees`,`+66 degrees`],answer:3,explanation:`Specific rotation = observed rotation / (path length in dm x concentration in g/mL) = 6.6 / (1 x 0.10) = +66 degrees. Forgetting to divide by the concentration gives +6.6 and mishandling the decimal gives +0.66; always check that concentration is in g per mL, not percent.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-oc-stereochem-4`,topicId:`oc-stereochem`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A sample of a chiral drug shows a rotation of +30 degrees; the pure (+) enantiomer shows +50 degrees. What is the composition of the sample?`,options:[`60 percent (+) and 40 percent (-)`,`70 percent (+) and 30 percent (-)`,`80 percent (+) and 20 percent (-)`,`30 percent (+) and 70 percent (-)`],answer:2,explanation:`Optical purity, which equals enantiomeric excess, is 30/50 x 100 = 60 percent. An ee of 60 percent means 60 percent of pure (+) plus 40 percent racemate, so the excess splits 20/20 and the sample is 80 percent (+) and 20 percent (-). Answering 60 percent (+) is the trap of confusing ee with the percentage of the major enantiomer.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-oc-stereochem-5`,topicId:`oc-stereochem`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which property differs between a pair of enantiomers measured in an achiral solvent?`,options:[`Melting point`,`Solubility in ethanol`,`Direction of rotation of plane-polarised light`,`Infrared spectrum`],answer:2,explanation:`Enantiomers have identical scalar physical properties — melting point, boiling point, solubility, density, IR and NMR in achiral media. They differ only in the sign of optical rotation and in their interaction with other chiral entities such as enzymes, receptors and chiral chromatographic phases. Diastereomers, in contrast, differ in all of the listed properties.`},{id:`q-oc-stereochem-6`,topicId:`oc-stereochem`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which conformation of cyclohexane is the least stable?`,options:[`Chair`,`Twist boat`,`Boat`,`Half chair`],answer:3,explanation:`The half chair sits about 45 kJ/mol above the chair and is the transition state of the ring flip, making it the highest-energy form. The boat (about 29 kJ/mol) is the tempting answer because it is the one students draw, but it is a shallow maximum that relaxes to the twist boat, not the global maximum.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-stereochem-7`,topicId:`oc-stereochem`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which of the following L-amino acids has the R configuration by CIP rules?`,options:[`L-alanine`,`L-serine`,`L-cysteine`,`L-valine`],answer:2,explanation:`All standard L-amino acids are S except L-cysteine, which is R, because the CH2SH side chain outranks the COOH group when sulfur is compared with oxygen at the first point of difference. L-serine has CH2OH, where oxygen loses to the two oxygens of the carboxyl, so it stays S.`,tag:`Exception`},{id:`q-oc-stereochem-8`,topicId:`oc-stereochem`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which compound is chiral despite containing no asymmetric carbon atom?`,options:[`cis-1,2-dimethylcyclohexane`,`2,2-dimethylbutane`,`6,6-dinitro-2,2-diphenic acid (a substituted biphenyl)`,`meso-tartaric acid`],answer:2,explanation:`An ortho,ortho-disubstituted biphenyl with bulky groups cannot rotate freely about the central bond and becomes chiral by atropisomerism, an axial chirality that requires no stereocentre. Meso-tartaric acid does contain stereocentres but is achiral, and cis-1,2-dimethylcyclohexane is achiral because of its internal mirror plane.`},{id:`q-oc-substitution-1`,topicId:`oc-substitution`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which alkyl halide undergoes SN2 substitution fastest?`,options:[`tert-butyl bromide`,`isopropyl bromide`,`n-propyl bromide`,`methyl bromide`],answer:3,explanation:`SN2 needs an unhindered backside approach, so reactivity falls as substitution rises: methyl > primary > secondary > tertiary. Tert-butyl bromide is the fastest under SN1 conditions, which is why it is such a tempting answer, but it is essentially inert to SN2.`},{id:`q-oc-substitution-2`,topicId:`oc-substitution`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The stereochemical outcome of an SN2 reaction at a stereocentre is:`,options:[`Complete inversion of configuration`,`Complete retention of configuration`,`Complete racemisation`,`Partial racemisation with excess retention`],answer:0,explanation:`Backside attack by the nucleophile inverts the carbon like an umbrella turning inside out, giving 100 percent Walden inversion. Racemisation is the SN1 outcome, because the planar carbocation can be attacked from either face.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-substitution-3`,topicId:`oc-substitution`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which solvent would most accelerate an SN2 reaction between sodium cyanide and 1-bromobutane?`,options:[`Water`,`Methanol`,`Formic acid`,`Dimethyl sulfoxide`],answer:3,explanation:`DMSO is polar aprotic: it solvates the sodium cation strongly but leaves the cyanide anion poorly solvated and therefore highly nucleophilic, which speeds SN2 by orders of magnitude. Water, methanol and formic acid are polar protic and hydrogen bond to the cyanide, caging it and slowing the reaction while favouring SN1.`},{id:`q-oc-substitution-4`,topicId:`oc-substitution`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The rate of hydrolysis of a certain alkyl bromide is unaffected when the hydroxide concentration is doubled. This indicates:`,options:[`An SN2 mechanism`,`An SN1 mechanism`,`An E2 mechanism`,`A radical chain mechanism`],answer:1,explanation:`If the nucleophile does not appear in the rate law, it is not present in the rate-determining step, which is the definition of SN1 (rate = k[substrate]). Both SN2 and E2 are second order and would double in rate when the hydroxide concentration doubles.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-substitution-5`,topicId:`oc-substitution`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In a polar aprotic solvent, the correct order of nucleophilicity of the halides is:`,options:[`I- > Br- > Cl- > F-`,`Br- > I- > F- > Cl-`,`Cl- > F- > Br- > I-`,`F- > Cl- > Br- > I-`],answer:3,explanation:`In polar aprotic media the anions are not hydrogen bonded, so nucleophilicity follows basicity and charge density, making fluoride the best nucleophile. The sequence I- > Br- > Cl- > F- is the familiar protic-solvent order, where polarisability and weak solvation of iodide dominate — this reversal is the point of the question.`},{id:`q-oc-substitution-6`,topicId:`oc-substitution`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Neopentyl bromide, although a primary halide, reacts extremely slowly by SN2 because:`,options:[`The C-Br bond is unusually strong`,`The adjacent quaternary carbon blocks backside attack`,`It forms a stable carbocation instantly`,`Bromide is a poor leaving group in this compound`],answer:1,explanation:`The neighbouring tert-butyl group sterically shields the 180-degree trajectory the nucleophile must take, raising the SN2 transition state energy enormously. It does not form a stable cation as a primary halide; under forcing ionising conditions it instead rearranges by a methyl shift.`,tag:`Exception`},{id:`q-oc-substitution-7`,topicId:`oc-substitution`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In nucleophilic aromatic substitution (SNAr) on 1-halo-2,4-dinitrobenzene, the leaving group order is:`,options:[`F > Cl > Br > I`,`I > Br > Cl > F`,`Cl > Br > I > F`,`Br > Cl > I > F`],answer:0,explanation:`SNAr is addition-elimination and the slow step is nucleophile addition to give the Meisenheimer complex, not C-X bond breaking. Fluorine, being the most electronegative, makes the ipso carbon most electrophilic and so reacts fastest. The sequence I > Br > Cl > F is the aliphatic SN1 and SN2 order and is the intended trap.`,tag:`Exception`},{id:`q-oc-substitution-8`,topicId:`oc-substitution`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Treating an optically active secondary alcohol with thionyl chloride in the absence of pyridine gives a chloride with:`,options:[`Inversion of configuration`,`Racemisation`,`Retention of configuration`,`No reaction at the stereocentre`],answer:2,explanation:`This is the SNi mechanism: the alkyl chlorosulfite collapses through a tight ion pair in which chloride is delivered to the same face the oxygen left from, so configuration is retained. Adding pyridine mops up HCl and supplies free chloride, which then attacks from the back and gives inversion instead.`,tag:`Mechanism`},{id:`q-oc-elimination-addition-1`,topicId:`oc-elimination-addition`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Addition of HBr to propene in the presence of benzoyl peroxide gives mainly:`,options:[`2-bromopropane`,`1-bromopropane`,`1,2-dibromopropane`,`Propan-2-ol`],answer:1,explanation:`Peroxides start a free-radical chain in which bromine adds first to the terminal carbon, generating the more stable secondary radical, so bromine ends up on C1 — the anti-Markovnikov or Kharasch product. Without peroxide the ionic route gives 2-bromopropane, and the peroxide effect is seen with HBr only.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-elimination-addition-2`,topicId:`oc-elimination-addition`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which reagent combination converts propene to propan-1-ol?`,options:[`Dilute H2SO4 then water`,`Hg(OAc)2 in water, then NaBH4`,`B2H6 in THF, then H2O2 and NaOH`,`Cold dilute alkaline KMnO4`],answer:2,explanation:`Hydroboration-oxidation adds H and OH across the double bond in a syn, anti-Markovnikov fashion, placing the hydroxyl on the less substituted carbon. Acid-catalysed hydration and oxymercuration-demercuration both give the Markovnikov product propan-2-ol, and cold KMnO4 gives a diol.`},{id:`q-oc-elimination-addition-3`,topicId:`oc-elimination-addition`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The E2 reaction requires the departing hydrogen and leaving group to be:`,options:[`Syn-periplanar (0 degrees)`,`Gauche (60 degrees)`,`At 90 degrees to each other`,`Anti-periplanar (180 degrees)`],answer:3,explanation:`E2 is concerted and needs the C-H sigma bond aligned with the C-LG sigma bond so the developing p orbitals can overlap; the anti-periplanar geometry achieves this with the least strain. In a cyclohexane ring this translates into a trans-diaxial arrangement, which is why menthyl chloride eliminates so much more slowly than neomenthyl chloride.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-elimination-addition-4`,topicId:`oc-elimination-addition`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`2-bromobutane treated with potassium tert-butoxide gives mainly but-1-ene rather than but-2-ene because:`,options:[`But-1-ene is thermodynamically more stable`,`Tert-butoxide is a better nucleophile than a base`,`A carbocation rearrangement occurs first`,`The bulky base abstracts the more accessible terminal hydrogen (Hofmann orientation)`],answer:3,explanation:`Tert-butoxide is too hindered to reach the internal beta hydrogens, so it removes a primary hydrogen and gives the less substituted Hofmann product. But-2-ene is in fact the more stable alkene, which is exactly why it is the Saytzeff product formed with small bases such as ethoxide.`},{id:`q-oc-elimination-addition-5`,topicId:`oc-elimination-addition`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which reagent converts an alkene to a syn (cis) 1,2-diol?`,options:[`mCPBA followed by dilute acid`,`Br2 in water`,`Osmium tetroxide followed by sodium bisulfite`,`Ozone followed by zinc and water`],answer:2,explanation:`OsO4 forms a cyclic osmate ester across one face of the alkene, so both hydroxyls are delivered to the same side — a syn diol; cold dilute alkaline KMnO4 does the same thing. Epoxidation with mCPBA followed by acidic ring opening gives the anti diol, and ozonolysis cleaves the double bond entirely.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-elimination-addition-6`,topicId:`oc-elimination-addition`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Ozonolysis of 2-methylbut-2-ene followed by oxidative workup with H2O2 gives:`,options:[`Acetone and acetaldehyde`,`Acetone and acetic acid`,`Acetic acid and propanoic acid`,`Butane-2,3-dione`],answer:1,explanation:`Cleaving the double bond of (CH3)2C=CHCH3 gives an acetone fragment from the disubstituted carbon and an acetaldehyde fragment from the other; the oxidative workup then takes the aldehyde on to acetic acid while the ketone is unchanged. A reductive workup with Zn/H2O would have stopped at acetone plus acetaldehyde, which is the distractor to avoid.`},{id:`q-oc-elimination-addition-7`,topicId:`oc-elimination-addition`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Addition of bromine to cis-but-2-ene gives:`,options:[`The meso 2,3-dibromobutane`,`The racemic 2,3-dibromobutane`,`An equal mixture of meso and racemic forms`,`Only one enantiomer of 2,3-dibromobutane`],answer:1,explanation:`Bromination goes through a cyclic bromonium ion opened by anti attack from the opposite face. Applying anti addition to the cis alkene gives the racemic (plus or minus) pair; the trans alkene under the same anti addition gives the meso compound. The classical demonstration is maleic acid (cis) yielding racemic 2,3-dibromosuccinic acid while fumaric acid (trans) yields the meso acid — which is exactly the stereochemical proof of the bromonium ion intermediate. Reversing the two is the commonest error on this topic.`,tag:`Stereochemistry`},{id:`q-oc-elimination-addition-8`,topicId:`oc-elimination-addition`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which elimination proceeds by a syn pathway through a cyclic transition state and needs no added base?`,options:[`E2 with sodium ethoxide`,`Cope elimination of an amine oxide`,`E1 dehydration with H2SO4`,`Dehydrohalogenation with alcoholic KOH`],answer:1,explanation:`An amine oxide heated to about 150 degrees C eliminates through a five-membered cyclic transition state in which the oxide oxygen removes the beta hydrogen on the same side, so it is intramolecular, syn and base-free, and it gives the Hofmann alkene. E2 and E1 both need an external base or acid and E2 is strictly anti-periplanar.`,tag:`Mechanism`},{id:`q-oc-aromatic-1`,topicId:`oc-aromatic`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`According to Huckel rule, which species is aromatic?`,options:[`Cyclobutadiene`,`Cyclooctatetraene`,`Cyclopentadienyl anion`,`Cyclopentadienyl cation`],answer:2,explanation:`The cyclopentadienyl anion is planar, cyclic and fully conjugated with 6 pi electrons, satisfying 4n + 2 with n = 1. Cyclobutadiene (4 pi) and the cyclopentadienyl cation (4 pi) are antiaromatic, and cyclooctatetraene escapes antiaromaticity by adopting a non-planar tub shape, making it non-aromatic.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-aromatic-2`,topicId:`oc-aromatic`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The electrophile generated in the nitration of benzene by a mixture of concentrated nitric and sulfuric acids is:`,options:[`NO2 radical`,`NO2- (nitrite ion)`,`HNO3 itself`,`NO2+ (nitronium ion)`],answer:3,explanation:`Sulfuric acid protonates nitric acid, which then loses water to give the linear nitronium ion, the species that attacks the ring. The nitrite ion is nucleophilic rather than electrophilic, and free nitric acid is not electrophilic enough to attack benzene on its own.`},{id:`q-oc-aromatic-3`,topicId:`oc-aromatic`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which of the following is a deactivating group yet still an ortho-para director?`,options:[`-NH2`,`-CN`,`-NO2`,`-Cl`],answer:3,explanation:`Halogens deactivate the ring overall because their strong -I effect withdraws electron density, but their lone-pair donation (+M) can stabilise the sigma complex only for ortho and para attack, so orientation stays ortho-para. Nitro and cyano are deactivating meta directors, and the amino group is a strong activator.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-aromatic-4`,topicId:`oc-aromatic`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Friedel-Crafts alkylation fails with which of the following substrates?`,options:[`Toluene`,`Chlorobenzene`,`Biphenyl`,`Nitrobenzene`],answer:3,explanation:`Friedel-Crafts reactions do not work on rings that are more deactivated than a halobenzene, and nitrobenzene is so electron poor that it is used as an inert solvent for these reactions. Chlorobenzene is deactivated but still reacts slowly, while toluene and biphenyl are activated.`},{id:`q-oc-aromatic-5`,topicId:`oc-aromatic`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Aniline gives an appreciable amount of the meta-nitro product on direct nitration because:`,options:[`The amino group is a meta director`,`In strong acid the amine is protonated to the meta-directing anilinium ion`,`The nitronium ion attacks the nitrogen first`,`The ring is oxidised before nitration`],answer:1,explanation:`In the sulfuric acid medium a large fraction of aniline exists as PhNH3+, and the positively charged NH3+ group is strongly deactivating and meta directing, so a substantial meta product appears alongside the ortho and para from unprotonated aniline. Acetylating the amine first gives the well-behaved para-nitroacetanilide.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-aromatic-6`,topicId:`oc-aromatic`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which of the following is the correct order of reactivity towards electrophilic aromatic substitution?`,options:[`Phenol > toluene > benzene > chlorobenzene > nitrobenzene`,`Nitrobenzene > chlorobenzene > benzene > toluene > phenol`,`Toluene > phenol > benzene > nitrobenzene > chlorobenzene`,`Benzene > phenol > toluene > chlorobenzene > nitrobenzene`],answer:0,explanation:`Reactivity tracks electron density: the strongly +M hydroxyl of phenol activates most, the weakly +I methyl of toluene next, then unsubstituted benzene, then the -I deactivated chlorobenzene, and finally the strongly -M nitrobenzene, which is about 10 million times slower than benzene. Placing toluene above phenol is the commonest slip.`},{id:`q-oc-aromatic-7`,topicId:`oc-aromatic`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Sulfonation of naphthalene at 160 degrees C gives mainly naphthalene-2-sulfonic acid, whereas at 80 degrees C the 1-isomer predominates. This is an example of:`,options:[`Steric inhibition of resonance`,`Kinetic versus thermodynamic control`,`Ipso substitution`,`The ortho effect`],answer:1,explanation:`The 1-position reacts faster because its sigma complex is better stabilised, so it is the kinetic product at low temperature, but it suffers peri strain; at high temperature sulfonation becomes reversible and the system settles on the more stable 2-isomer. Ipso substitution describes replacement of an existing group, which is not what governs this ratio.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-aromatic-8`,topicId:`oc-aromatic`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Birch reduction of anisole (methoxybenzene) with sodium in liquid ammonia and ethanol gives:`,options:[`1-methoxycyclohexa-1,4-diene`,`3-methoxycyclohexa-1,4-diene`,`Cyclohexanone`,`Methoxycyclohexane`],answer:0,explanation:`With an electron-donating substituent the ipso and para carbons keep their double bonds, so the methoxy group remains attached to an sp2 carbon and the product is 1-methoxycyclohexa-1,4-diene. Electron-withdrawing substituents such as COOH show the opposite regiochemistry and end up on the sp3 carbon; complete reduction to the cyclohexane would require catalytic hydrogenation.`,tag:`Mechanism`},{id:`q-oc-carbonyl-1`,topicId:`oc-carbonyl`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which aldehyde will undergo the Cannizzaro reaction?`,options:[`Acetaldehyde`,`Propanal`,`Benzaldehyde`,`Butanal`],answer:2,explanation:`Cannizzaro is a base-induced disproportionation that requires an aldehyde with no alpha hydrogen, and benzaldehyde has none because the carbonyl is attached directly to the ring. Acetaldehyde, propanal and butanal all carry alpha hydrogens and would undergo aldol condensation with base instead.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-carbonyl-2`,topicId:`oc-carbonyl`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which compound gives a positive iodoform test?`,options:[`Propan-2-ol`,`Propan-1-ol`,`Benzophenone`,`Diethyl ketone`],answer:0,explanation:`The iodoform test is positive for a methyl ketone or for an alcohol that can be oxidised to one, meaning a CH3-CO- or CH3-CH(OH)- fragment; propan-2-ol satisfies the second. Propan-1-ol oxidises to propanal, and neither benzophenone nor pentan-3-one carries a methyl group on the carbonyl.`},{id:`q-oc-carbonyl-3`,topicId:`oc-carbonyl`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The Claisen condensation of two molecules of ethyl acetate with sodium ethoxide gives:`,options:[`Ethyl 3-hydroxybutanoate`,`But-2-enoic acid`,`Acetic anhydride`,`Ethyl acetoacetate`],answer:3,explanation:`The ester enolate attacks the carbonyl of a second ester and the ethoxide leaving group is expelled, giving the beta-keto ester ethyl acetoacetate. Ethyl 3-hydroxybutanoate would be the aldol-type product where the alkoxide is retained, which does not happen because the tetrahedral intermediate has a leaving group.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-carbonyl-4`,topicId:`oc-carbonyl`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Benzaldehyde condensed with acetic anhydride in the presence of sodium acetate gives cinnamic acid. This is the:`,options:[`Knoevenagel reaction`,`Perkin reaction`,`Claisen condensation`,`Benzoin condensation`],answer:1,explanation:`The Perkin reaction condenses an aromatic aldehyde with an acid anhydride using the sodium salt of the corresponding acid as base, giving an alpha,beta-unsaturated aromatic acid. Knoevenagel uses an active methylene compound with an amine base, and benzoin condensation joins two aromatic aldehydes using cyanide.`},{id:`q-oc-carbonyl-5`,topicId:`oc-carbonyl`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which reagent adds 1,4 (conjugate addition) to cyclohexenone rather than 1,2?`,options:[`Methylmagnesium bromide`,`Methyllithium`,`Lithium dimethylcuprate`,`Lithium aluminium hydride`],answer:2,explanation:`Gilman cuprates are soft nucleophiles and deliver the alkyl group to the beta carbon, giving the 1,4 or Michael product. Grignards, organolithiums and LiAlH4 are hard nucleophiles that attack the carbonyl carbon directly and give 1,2-addition, and this Grignard-versus-cuprate contrast is a standard exam pairing.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-carbonyl-6`,topicId:`oc-carbonyl`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A Mannich reaction requires which three components?`,options:[`A ketone with an alpha hydrogen, formaldehyde and a secondary amine`,`Two esters and a strong base`,`An aldehyde, an anhydride and its sodium salt`,`An aldehyde and a phosphorus ylide`],answer:0,explanation:`The Mannich reaction is a three-component aminomethylation: formaldehyde and the amine form an iminium ion, which is then attacked by the enol of the ketone to give a beta-aminoketone. An aldehyde with an anhydride and its sodium salt describes the Perkin reaction, and an aldehyde with a phosphorus ylide describes the Wittig reaction.`},{id:`q-oc-carbonyl-7`,topicId:`oc-carbonyl`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which compound has the highest percentage of enol tautomer at equilibrium?`,options:[`Acetone`,`Ethyl acetoacetate`,`Pentane-2,4-dione`,`Cyclohexanone`],answer:2,explanation:`Pentane-2,4-dione (acetylacetone) is roughly 80 percent enol because its enol is conjugated and locked by a strong six-membered intramolecular hydrogen bond between the enol OH and the remaining carbonyl. Ethyl acetoacetate has the same features but a weaker one and sits near 8 percent, while acetone is about 0.00025 percent.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-carbonyl-8`,topicId:`oc-carbonyl`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Treating 2-methylcyclohexanone with LDA at -78 degrees C followed by methyl iodide gives predominantly:`,options:[`The 2,6-dimethyl product (alkylation at the less substituted carbon)`,`The 2,2-dimethyl product (alkylation at the more substituted carbon)`,`The aldol dimer`,`The enol ether`],answer:0,explanation:`LDA is bulky and used at low temperature under irreversible conditions, so it removes the more accessible proton and gives the kinetic, less substituted enolate, which alkylates to the 2,6-dimethyl product. A protic base such as sodium ethoxide at room temperature would equilibrate to the more substituted thermodynamic enolate and give the 2,2-product.`,tag:`Mechanism`},{id:`q-oc-name-reactions-1`,topicId:`oc-name-reactions`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Salicylaldehyde is prepared from phenol using chloroform and aqueous sodium hydroxide. This is the:`,options:[`Kolbe-Schmitt reaction`,`Reimer-Tiemann reaction`,`Gattermann-Koch reaction`,`Vilsmeier-Haack reaction`],answer:1,explanation:`The Reimer-Tiemann reaction generates dichlorocarbene from chloroform and hydroxide, which attacks the phenoxide ortho position and hydrolyses to the aldehyde. Kolbe-Schmitt uses carbon dioxide under pressure and gives salicylic acid, not the aldehyde.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-name-reactions-2`,topicId:`oc-name-reactions`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The Gabriel phthalimide synthesis is used to prepare:`,options:[`Pure secondary amines`,`Pure tertiary amines`,`Quaternary ammonium salts`,`Pure primary amines`],answer:3,explanation:`Potassium phthalimide displaces halide by SN2 and subsequent hydrolysis or hydrazinolysis releases a primary amine uncontaminated by higher alkylation products, which is the whole point of the method. It cannot be used on aryl halides or on tertiary halides, where elimination takes over.`},{id:`q-oc-name-reactions-3`,topicId:`oc-name-reactions`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Propanamide treated with bromine and sodium hydroxide gives:`,options:[`Propylamine`,`Propanenitrile`,`Propanoic acid`,`Ethylamine`],answer:3,explanation:`The Hofmann bromamide degradation converts a primary amide to an amine with one carbon fewer, because the carbonyl carbon is lost as carbonate after the isocyanate intermediate is hydrolysed. Propylamine would need a reduction of the amide with LiAlH4, which retains the carbon count.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-name-reactions-4`,topicId:`oc-name-reactions`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Acetophenone is converted to ethylbenzene by which reagent set?`,options:[`LiAlH4 in dry ether`,`NaBH4 in methanol`,`Zinc amalgam and concentrated hydrochloric acid`,`H2 with Lindlar catalyst`],answer:2,explanation:`The Clemmensen reduction with Zn-Hg and concentrated HCl takes a carbonyl all the way down to a methylene group, so acetophenone becomes ethylbenzene. LiAlH4 and NaBH4 stop at the secondary alcohol, and the Lindlar catalyst is for partially reducing alkynes.`},{id:`q-oc-name-reactions-5`,topicId:`oc-name-reactions`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Benzenediazonium chloride treated with cuprous cyanide gives benzonitrile. This conversion is named after:`,options:[`Sandmeyer`,`Balz-Schiemann`,`Ullmann`,`Rosenmund`],answer:0,explanation:`The Sandmeyer reaction replaces the diazonium group with Cl, Br or CN using the corresponding copper(I) salt, through a radical process. Balz-Schiemann uses fluoroboric acid and heat to install fluorine, and Rosenmund is a reduction of an acid chloride to an aldehyde.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-name-reactions-6`,topicId:`oc-name-reactions`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which reaction converts a carboxylic acid directly to an amine with one fewer carbon in a single operation?`,options:[`Curtius reaction`,`Schmidt reaction`,`Arndt-Eistert synthesis`,`Hofmann bromamide reaction`],answer:1,explanation:`The Schmidt reaction treats the acid with hydrazoic acid in sulfuric acid and delivers the amine in one pot, whereas the Curtius route needs the acid chloride and then the acyl azide to be made first and Hofmann starts from the amide. Arndt-Eistert goes the other way and lengthens the chain by one carbon.`},{id:`q-oc-name-reactions-7`,topicId:`oc-name-reactions`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Hofmann, Curtius, Schmidt and Lossen rearrangements share which common intermediate?`,options:[`A carbene`,`A carbocation`,`An enol`,`An isocyanate`],answer:3,explanation:`All four generate an electron-deficient acyl nitrene or its equivalent, from which the R group migrates to nitrogen to give an isocyanate; hydrolysis of that isocyanate liberates the amine and carbon dioxide. Because migration and departure are concerted, the migrating carbon keeps its configuration in every one of them.`,tag:`Mechanism`},{id:`q-oc-name-reactions-8`,topicId:`oc-name-reactions`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`The Suzuki coupling joins an aryl halide with which partner?`,options:[`An organoboronic acid`,`A terminal alkyne`,`An organostannane`,`A secondary amine`],answer:0,explanation:`Suzuki coupling uses a palladium(0) catalyst and base to join an aryl or vinyl halide with a boronic acid or ester, and its low toxicity is why it dominates modern drug synthesis. Terminal alkynes belong to Sonogashira, stannanes to Stille and amines to Buchwald-Hartwig.`},{id:`q-oc-rearrangements-1`,topicId:`oc-rearrangements`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Cyclohexanone oxime treated with concentrated sulfuric acid gives:`,options:[`Adipic acid`,`Caprolactam`,`Cyclohexylamine`,`Cyclohexanone`],answer:1,explanation:`This is the Beckmann rearrangement, in which the group anti to the oxime hydroxyl migrates to nitrogen; in a cyclic oxime that produces a ring-expanded lactam, here caprolactam, the monomer for nylon-6. Cyclohexylamine would be the product of reducing the oxime, not rearranging it.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-rearrangements-2`,topicId:`oc-rearrangements`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Pinacol (2,3-dimethylbutane-2,3-diol) on treatment with acid gives pinacolone, which is:`,options:[`3,3-dimethylbutan-2-one`,`2,3-dimethylbutan-2-ol`,`2,3-dimethylbut-2-ene`,`Butane-2,3-dione`],answer:0,explanation:`Loss of water gives a tertiary carbocation, a methyl group migrates from the adjacent carbon, and the resulting oxocarbenium loses a proton to give the ketone 3,3-dimethylbutan-2-one. Simple dehydration to the alkene is the tempting alternative, but the methyl shift to give a much more stable oxygen-stabilised cation outcompetes it.`},{id:`q-oc-rearrangements-3`,topicId:`oc-rearrangements`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In the Beckmann rearrangement, which group migrates to nitrogen?`,options:[`The group syn (cis) to the oxime hydroxyl`,`Always the smaller group regardless of geometry`,`Always the larger group regardless of geometry`,`The group anti (trans) to the oxime hydroxyl`],answer:3,explanation:`The migration is concerted with departure of the protonated hydroxyl, so only the group anti-periplanar to it can migrate, making the reaction stereospecific on the oxime geometry. Because bulky groups often prefer the anti arrangement, the larger group frequently does migrate, but that is a consequence rather than the rule.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-rearrangements-4`,topicId:`oc-rearrangements`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Baeyer-Villiger oxidation of acetophenone with a peracid gives mainly:`,options:[`Methyl benzoate`,`Phenyl acetate`,`Benzoic acid`,`Benzaldehyde`],answer:1,explanation:`Migratory aptitude in the Baeyer-Villiger runs tertiary alkyl > secondary alkyl, benzyl and phenyl > primary alkyl > methyl, so the phenyl group migrates to oxygen in preference to the methyl group, giving phenyl acetate. Methyl benzoate would require methyl migration, which is the slowest of all.`},{id:`q-oc-rearrangements-5`,topicId:`oc-rearrangements`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Allyl phenyl ether heated to about 200 degrees C gives 2-allylphenol. This transformation is:`,options:[`A Claisen condensation`,`A Fries rearrangement`,`A [3,3] sigmatropic Claisen rearrangement`,`A Cope elimination`],answer:2,explanation:`The allyl group migrates to the ortho carbon through a concerted six-membered cyclic transition state, a thermally allowed [3,3] sigmatropic shift, and tautomerisation back to the phenol drives it. The Claisen condensation is an entirely different ester reaction, and the Fries rearrangement moves an acyl group from an ester oxygen with a Lewis acid.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-rearrangements-6`,topicId:`oc-rearrangements`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The Fries rearrangement of phenyl acetate at low temperature with aluminium chloride gives predominantly:`,options:[`The para hydroxyacetophenone`,`The ortho hydroxyacetophenone`,`Phenol and acetic acid`,`Acetanilide`],answer:0,explanation:`Fries selectivity is temperature dependent: low temperatures with more solvent favour the para isomer, while high temperatures favour the ortho isomer, which is steam volatile and easily separated. Simple hydrolysis to phenol and acetic acid needs aqueous acid or base rather than anhydrous aluminium chloride.`},{id:`q-oc-rearrangements-7`,topicId:`oc-rearrangements`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`2-chlorocyclohexanone treated with sodium methoxide gives methyl cyclopentanecarboxylate. The intermediate responsible is:`,options:[`A carbene`,`A cyclopropanone`,`An epoxide`,`A ketene`],answer:1,explanation:`This is the Favorskii rearrangement: the enolate displaces the chloride intramolecularly to give a strained cyclopropanone, which the methoxide then opens at the carbonyl to give the ring-contracted ester. A ketene would be the Wolff rearrangement intermediate, and an epoxide would come from the alkoxide of a halohydrin.`,tag:`Mechanism`},{id:`q-oc-rearrangements-8`,topicId:`oc-rearrangements`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which of the following rearrangements involves migration to an electron-deficient oxygen?`,options:[`Hofmann rearrangement`,`Wagner-Meerwein rearrangement`,`Stevens rearrangement`,`Baeyer-Villiger oxidation`],answer:3,explanation:`In the Baeyer-Villiger the Criegee intermediate has a weak O-O bond, and the alkyl group migrates from carbon to the oxygen as the carboxylate leaves, so the terminus is an electron-deficient oxygen. Hofmann migrates to nitrogen, Wagner-Meerwein to an electron-deficient carbon, and Stevens moves a group to an electron-rich carbanionic carbon.`},{id:`q-oc-heterocycles-1`,topicId:`oc-heterocycles`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Electrophilic substitution in pyrrole occurs preferentially at which position?`,options:[`C2 (alpha)`,`C3 (beta)`,`Nitrogen`,`C4`],answer:0,explanation:`Attack at C2 gives a sigma complex stabilised by three resonance structures, whereas attack at C3 gives only two, so the alpha position is strongly preferred. Substitution on nitrogen is not electrophilic aromatic substitution and requires prior deprotonation of the N-H.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-heterocycles-2`,topicId:`oc-heterocycles`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Pyridine is far more basic than pyrrole because:`,options:[`Pyridine has a larger ring`,`Pyridine is not aromatic`,`Pyrrole has no nitrogen lone pair at all`,`The pyridine lone pair is in an sp2 orbital outside the aromatic sextet`],answer:3,explanation:`Pyridine keeps its nitrogen lone pair in an sp2 orbital in the ring plane, so it is fully available for protonation without disturbing the aromatic sextet, giving a conjugate acid pKa of about 5.2. Pyrrole has donated its lone pair into the sextet, so protonating nitrogen would destroy aromaticity, and its conjugate acid pKa is about -3.8.`},{id:`q-oc-heterocycles-3`,topicId:`oc-heterocycles`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which is the correct order of reactivity towards electrophilic substitution?`,options:[`Benzene > thiophene > furan > pyrrole`,`Pyrrole > furan > thiophene > benzene`,`Furan > pyrrole > benzene > thiophene`,`Thiophene > pyrrole > furan > benzene`],answer:1,explanation:`Electron availability decides electrophilic reactivity and it falls in the order pyrrole > furan > thiophene > benzene > pyridine, because nitrogen donates its lone pair most readily and sulfur least. The resonance-energy or aromaticity order is different — benzene > thiophene > pyrrole > furan — and confusing the two orders is the classic error.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-heterocycles-4`,topicId:`oc-heterocycles`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Aniline heated with glycerol, concentrated sulfuric acid and nitrobenzene gives quinoline. This is the:`,options:[`Skraup synthesis`,`Fischer indole synthesis`,`Paal-Knorr synthesis`,`Hantzsch synthesis`],answer:0,explanation:`In the Skraup synthesis glycerol is dehydrated to acrolein, which undergoes conjugate addition with the aniline, followed by cyclisation and oxidation by nitrobenzene to aromatise the ring. Ferrous sulfate is added to moderate what is otherwise a violently exothermic reaction.`},{id:`q-oc-heterocycles-5`,topicId:`oc-heterocycles`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Phenylhydrazine condensed with a ketone and then heated with zinc chloride gives an indole. The name of this synthesis and its key step are:`,options:[`Paal-Knorr, via a 1,4-dicarbonyl`,`Fischer indole, via a [3,3] sigmatropic shift with loss of ammonia`,`Madelung, via an intramolecular acylation`,`Bischler-Napieralski, via a nitrilium ion`],answer:1,explanation:`The Fischer indole synthesis tautomerises the arylhydrazone to an ene-hydrazine, which undergoes a [3,3] sigmatropic rearrangement forming the new carbon-carbon bond, and then cyclises with loss of ammonia. Paal-Knorr builds five-membered rings from 1,4-dicarbonyls and Bischler-Napieralski gives dihydroisoquinolines.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-heterocycles-6`,topicId:`oc-heterocycles`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Pyridine heated with sodium amide gives 2-aminopyridine. This reaction is known as:`,options:[`The Chichibabin reaction`,`The Skraup reaction`,`The Hantzsch reaction`,`The Knorr reaction`],answer:0,explanation:`The Chichibabin reaction is a nucleophilic addition-elimination in which the amide ion attacks the electron-poor C2 of pyridine and hydride is finally expelled as hydrogen gas. It needs no activating group precisely because pyridine is already pi-deficient, which is the opposite of what benzene requires.`},{id:`q-oc-heterocycles-7`,topicId:`oc-heterocycles`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which heterocycle has the highest conjugate acid pKa, that is, is the most basic?`,options:[`Pyridine`,`Thiazole`,`Imidazole`,`Oxazole`],answer:2,explanation:`Imidazole has a conjugate acid pKa of about 7.0 because the imidazolium cation is stabilised by two equivalent resonance structures that share the positive charge between the two nitrogens, and this is why histidine buffers near physiological pH. Pyridine is about 5.2, thiazole about 2.5 and oxazole about 0.8, since the electronegative sulfur or oxygen withdraws electron density.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-heterocycles-8`,topicId:`oc-heterocycles`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Electrophilic substitution on indole occurs at C3 rather than C2 because:`,options:[`C3 is the most sterically accessible position`,`The nitrogen lone pair is localised at C2`,`C3 carries a formal negative charge`,`Attack at C3 gives an intermediate that keeps the benzene ring aromatic`],answer:3,explanation:`Substitution at C3 lets the positive charge in the sigma complex be delocalised onto nitrogen without breaking the aromaticity of the fused benzene ring, whereas attack at C2 forces the benzene ring to give up its aromatic sextet. Sterics are not the deciding factor, since C2 is at least as open as C3.`},{id:`q-oc-protection-reagents-1`,topicId:`oc-protection-reagents`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which functional group is reduced by lithium aluminium hydride but NOT by sodium borohydride?`,options:[`Aldehyde`,`Ketone`,`Ester`,`Acid chloride`],answer:2,explanation:`NaBH4 is mild enough to reduce only aldehydes, ketones and acid chlorides, while LiAlH4 also reduces esters, carboxylic acids, amides and nitriles. This selectivity is the standard way to reduce a ketone in the presence of an ester.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-protection-reagents-2`,topicId:`oc-protection-reagents`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A ketone is commonly protected during a Grignard reaction as its:`,options:[`Oxime`,`Cyclic acetal (ketal) with ethylene glycol`,`Enol acetate`,`Hydrazone`],answer:1,explanation:`The ethylene ketal is made with ethylene glycol under acid catalysis, is completely inert to strong bases and organometallics, and is removed cleanly with dilute aqueous acid at the end. Oximes and hydrazones still have a polarised C=N that organometallics attack, so they do not protect the carbon.`},{id:`q-oc-protection-reagents-3`,topicId:`oc-protection-reagents`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which reagent oxidises a primary alcohol to the aldehyde without going on to the carboxylic acid?`,options:[`Jones reagent`,`Hot alkaline KMnO4`,`Pyridinium chlorochromate in dichloromethane`,`Acidified potassium dichromate in water`],answer:2,explanation:`PCC works under anhydrous conditions, so no aldehyde hydrate can form and the reaction stops cleanly at the aldehyde; Swern and Dess-Martin oxidations do the same job. Jones reagent, hot permanganate and aqueous dichromate are all aqueous and take the alcohol through to the acid.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-protection-reagents-4`,topicId:`oc-protection-reagents`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The Boc protecting group on an amine is removed by:`,options:[`Trifluoroacetic acid`,`Piperidine in DMF`,`Hydrogenation over palladium`,`Aqueous sodium hydroxide`],answer:0,explanation:`Boc is a tert-butyl carbamate that fragments under mild acid such as TFA or HCl in dioxane, releasing isobutylene and carbon dioxide. Piperidine removes Fmoc and hydrogenolysis removes Cbz, and this three-way orthogonality is what makes stepwise peptide synthesis possible.`},{id:`q-oc-protection-reagents-5`,topicId:`oc-protection-reagents`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which reagent cleaves a 1,2-diol into two carbonyl compounds?`,options:[`Periodic acid`,`Osmium tetroxide`,`mCPBA`,`Sodium borohydride`],answer:0,explanation:`Periodic acid (HIO4), and equally lead tetraacetate, breaks the carbon-carbon bond of a vicinal diol through a cyclic periodate ester to give two carbonyl fragments. Osmium tetroxide and mCPBA make diols and epoxides respectively, so they run in the opposite direction.`},{id:`q-oc-protection-reagents-6`,topicId:`oc-protection-reagents`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`To convert an ester into an aldehyde without over-reduction, the reagent of choice is:`,options:[`LiAlH4 at reflux`,`NaBH4 in ethanol`,`H2 over Raney nickel`,`DIBAL-H at -78 degrees C`],answer:3,explanation:`DIBAL-H is bulky, and at -78 degrees C with one equivalent the tetrahedral alkoxide intermediate survives until aqueous workup, releasing the aldehyde. LiAlH4 would take the ester right down to the primary alcohol, and NaBH4 does not touch esters at all.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-protection-reagents-7`,topicId:`oc-protection-reagents`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Reduction of nitrobenzene with zinc dust in neutral ammonium chloride solution gives:`,options:[`Aniline`,`Phenylhydroxylamine`,`Hydrazobenzene`,`Azoxybenzene`],answer:1,explanation:`The medium decides the product: neutral zinc and ammonium chloride stop the reduction at N-phenylhydroxylamine, acidic Sn/HCl goes all the way to aniline, and zinc with sodium hydroxide gives hydrazobenzene. Phenylhydroxylamine is also the substrate that rearranges to p-aminophenol under acid in the Bamberger rearrangement.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-protection-reagents-8`,topicId:`oc-protection-reagents`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A molecule contains a benzyl ether, an alkene and an ester. Catalytic hydrogenation over palladium on carbon is a poor choice for deprotection because it will:`,options:[`Hydrolyse the ester`,`Convert the ester to an aldehyde`,`Fail to cleave the benzyl ether`,`Also reduce the alkene`],answer:3,explanation:`Hydrogenolysis does cleave the benzyl ether efficiently, but the same conditions saturate any alkene present, so the protecting group cannot be removed selectively. Esters survive catalytic hydrogenation, so a silyl or THP ether should be chosen instead when an alkene must be preserved.`},{id:`q-oc-bonding-effects-9`,topicId:`oc-bonding-effects`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`How many degrees of unsaturation (double bond equivalents) does 4-nitrotoluene, C7H7NO2, contain?`,options:[`3`,`4`,`5`,`6`],answer:2,explanation:`Degrees of unsaturation = (2C + 2 + N - H - X) / 2 = (14 + 2 + 1 - 7) / 2 = 5, and oxygen is ignored entirely in the formula. Counting 4 is the usual slip: it accounts for the benzene ring plus its three double bonds but forgets the N=O of the nitro group, which is itself one degree of unsaturation.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-oc-bonding-effects-10`,topicId:`oc-bonding-effects`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which of the following carbanions is the most stable?`,options:[`tert-butyl carbanion`,`isopropyl carbanion`,`ethyl carbanion`,`methyl carbanion`],answer:3,explanation:`Alkyl groups are +I and push electron density onto an already negative carbon, so carbanion stability falls as substitution rises and the methyl carbanion is the most stable of the four. The tert-butyl carbanion is the tempting choice because tert-butyl heads the carbocation stability list, but for a carbanion the whole order is reversed.`},{id:`q-oc-bonding-effects-11`,topicId:`oc-bonding-effects`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Arrange in increasing order of acidity: phenol, p-cresol, p-chlorophenol, p-nitrophenol.`,options:[`p-cresol < phenol < p-chlorophenol < p-nitrophenol`,`phenol < p-cresol < p-nitrophenol < p-chlorophenol`,`p-nitrophenol < p-chlorophenol < phenol < p-cresol`,`p-chlorophenol < p-cresol < phenol < p-nitrophenol`],answer:0,explanation:`The +I methyl of p-cresol destabilises the phenoxide and makes it the weakest acid; unsubstituted phenol comes next; the -I chlorine of p-chlorophenol stabilises the anion further; and the strongly -M nitro group of p-nitrophenol (pKa about 7.1) accepts the charge directly by resonance, making it the strongest. The sequence that puts p-chlorophenol above p-nitrophenol underestimates resonance withdrawal, which is always stronger than a bare inductive pull.`},{id:`q-oc-bonding-effects-12`,topicId:`oc-bonding-effects`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Arrange in increasing order of basicity in aqueous solution: aniline, p-nitroaniline, p-toluidine, cyclohexylamine.`,options:[`aniline < p-nitroaniline < p-toluidine < cyclohexylamine`,`p-nitroaniline < aniline < p-toluidine < cyclohexylamine`,`cyclohexylamine < p-toluidine < aniline < p-nitroaniline`,`p-toluidine < aniline < cyclohexylamine < p-nitroaniline`],answer:1,explanation:`Any aromatic amine is weaker than an aliphatic one because the lone pair is delocalised into the ring, so cyclohexylamine is the strongest base here. Within the anilines the -M nitro group of p-nitroaniline drains the lone pair further and makes it the weakest, while the +I methyl of p-toluidine makes it slightly stronger than aniline. Placing p-nitroaniline at the strong end confuses acid-strengthening with base-strengthening substituents.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-bonding-effects-13`,topicId:`oc-bonding-effects`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`p-nitrophenol (pKa about 7.1) is much more acidic than m-nitrophenol (pKa about 8.4) because:`,options:[`The meta isomer is stabilised by intramolecular hydrogen bonding`,`The nitro group exerts a +M effect from the meta position`,`The meta isomer experiences no inductive effect at all`,`Only from the ortho and para positions can the nitro group accept the negative charge of the phenoxide by resonance`],answer:3,explanation:`Resonance delocalisation of the phenoxide charge onto the nitro oxygens is geometrically possible only when the nitro group is ortho or para to the oxygen; from the meta position just the distance-limited -I effect operates, so the acid is weaker. The nitro group is -M in every position, so any explanation making it +M is wrong, and intramolecular hydrogen bonding is a feature of the ortho isomer, not the meta.`},{id:`q-oc-bonding-effects-14`,topicId:`oc-bonding-effects`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which of the following has the most acidic C-H bond?`,options:[`Ethane`,`Ethene`,`Ethyne`,`Cyclopenta-1,3-diene`],answer:3,explanation:`Cyclopentadiene has a pKa of about 16 because losing a proton generates the cyclopentadienyl anion, which is aromatic with 6 pi electrons — an enormous stabilisation. Ethyne is the tempting answer with pKa 25, and it is indeed the most acidic of the simple hydrocarbons because of the 50 percent s character of its sp carbon, but aromatic stabilisation beats hybridisation here; ethene is about 44 and ethane about 50.`,tag:`Exception`},{id:`q-oc-bonding-effects-15`,topicId:`oc-bonding-effects`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A reaction series obeys the Hammett equation with rho = +2.0. For the para-nitro substituent sigma = +0.78. By roughly what factor does the para-nitro compound react faster than the unsubstituted compound?`,options:[`About 1.6 times`,`About 36 times`,`About 3.6 times`,`About 156 times`],answer:1,explanation:`log(k/k0) = rho x sigma = 2.0 x 0.78 = 1.56, so k/k0 = 10 raised to 1.56, which is about 36. Answering about 1.6 times is the error of quoting the logarithm itself as the rate ratio, and 156 comes from treating the product as a percentage. A positive rho with an electron-withdrawing substituent must give an acceleration, so any answer below 1 would be immediately wrong.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-oc-bonding-effects-16`,topicId:`oc-bonding-effects`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`2,6-dimethylaniline is a weaker base than aniline itself, even though the two methyl groups are electron-releasing. The accepted explanation is:`,options:[`The two flanking methyl groups hinder solvation and hydrogen bonding of the anilinium cation that forms on protonation`,`A methyl group becomes electron-withdrawing when placed ortho`,`The ring loses its aromaticity when the nitrogen is protonated`,`The nitrogen lone pair is delocalised into the ring only in this compound`],answer:0,explanation:`Basicity in water depends on how well the protonated cation can be stabilised by solvent, and two ortho methyl groups shield the NH3+ centre so effectively that the loss of solvation outweighs the +I gain. A methyl group keeps its +I character wherever it sits, and protonating an aniline nitrogen does not disturb the ring sextet, since the lone pair sits on nitrogen rather than in the ring.`,tag:`Exception`},{id:`q-oc-stereochem-9`,topicId:`oc-stereochem`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Arrange in decreasing order of CIP (Cahn-Ingold-Prelog) priority: -CH3, -COOH, -NH2, -OH.`,options:[`-COOH > -OH > -NH2 > -CH3`,`-OH > -NH2 > -COOH > -CH3`,`-NH2 > -OH > -COOH > -CH3`,`-OH > -COOH > -NH2 > -CH3`],answer:1,explanation:`Priority is decided by the atomic number of the first atom attached, so oxygen beats nitrogen beats carbon: -OH first, -NH2 second, and the two carbon-attached groups last. Between -COOH and -CH3 the tie is broken one shell out, where (O, O, O) on the carboxyl carbon outranks (H, H, H) on the methyl. Ranking -COOH above -OH is the standard slip — the carboxyl group is bulkier and more oxygenated, but its point of attachment is still only a carbon.`},{id:`q-oc-stereochem-10`,topicId:`oc-stereochem`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which statement about stereochemical notation is correct?`,options:[`A compound labelled D must be dextrorotatory`,`The R configuration always corresponds to a (+) rotation`,`D and L describe configuration relative to glyceraldehyde while (+) and (-) report the measured rotation, so D-fructose is D yet laevorotatory`,`All L-amino acids are R by CIP rules`],answer:2,explanation:`D/L is a relative configurational label tied to glyceraldehyde, whereas (+)/(-) is an experimental measurement made on a polarimeter, and the two are independent — D-fructose is the standard counterexample. Sign of rotation cannot be predicted from either D/L or R/S, and among the L-amino acids only L-cysteine is R, all the others being S.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-stereochem-11`,topicId:`oc-stereochem`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A sample contains 75 percent of the (R) enantiomer and 25 percent of the (S). The pure (R) form has a specific rotation of +40 degrees. The specific rotation of the sample is:`,options:[`+20 degrees`,`+30 degrees`,`+10 degrees`,`+40 degrees`],answer:0,explanation:`Enantiomeric excess = 75 - 25 = 50 percent, and observed specific rotation = ee x rotation of the pure enantiomer = 0.50 x 40 = +20 degrees. Answering +30 degrees comes from multiplying by 0.75, the fraction of the major enantiomer, which double counts the 25 percent that is cancelled out by the minor enantiomer.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-oc-stereochem-12`,topicId:`oc-stereochem`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The (2R,3R) and (2R,3S) forms of 3-bromobutan-2-ol are best described as:`,options:[`Enantiomers`,`Identical compounds`,`Conformers`,`Diastereomers`],answer:3,explanation:`Only one of the two stereocentres has been inverted, so the pair cannot be mirror images; stereoisomers that are not mirror images are diastereomers, and they differ in melting point, solubility and NMR. They would be enantiomers only if both centres were inverted together, that is if the second compound were (2S,3S).`},{id:`q-oc-stereochem-13`,topicId:`oc-stereochem`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which dimethylcyclohexane can place both methyl groups equatorial in a chair conformation?`,options:[`cis-1,2-dimethylcyclohexane`,`cis-1,4-dimethylcyclohexane`,`trans-1,3-dimethylcyclohexane`,`trans-1,4-dimethylcyclohexane`],answer:3,explanation:`For 1,2- and 1,4-disubstituted rings the trans isomer is the diequatorial one, while for 1,3-disubstituted rings it is the cis isomer; hence trans-1,4-dimethylcyclohexane is diequatorial and the most stable of the four. The cis-1,2 and cis-1,4 isomers and trans-1,3 are all locked in an axial-equatorial arrangement and pay a 1,3-diaxial penalty.`},{id:`q-oc-stereochem-14`,topicId:`oc-stereochem`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A racemic carboxylic acid is to be resolved into its enantiomers. The classical laboratory method is to:`,options:[`Fractionally distil the racemate, since the enantiomers boil at different temperatures`,`Convert it into a pair of diastereomeric salts with an optically pure base such as brucine or quinine, crystallise them apart, then regenerate the free acids`,`Recrystallise repeatedly from ethanol until one enantiomer is left pure`,`Chromatograph the mixture on ordinary silica gel`],answer:1,explanation:`Enantiomers have identical boiling points, solubilities and affinities for an achiral stationary phase, so distillation, plain recrystallisation and ordinary silica chromatography can never separate them. Reacting the racemic acid with a single enantiomer of a chiral base creates diastereomeric salts, which do differ in solubility and can be crystallised apart before the acid is liberated with mineral acid.`},{id:`q-oc-stereochem-15`,topicId:`oc-stereochem`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`How many stereoisomers does 2,3,4-trihydroxyglutaric acid, HOOC-CHOH-CHOH-CHOH-COOH, actually have?`,options:[`8`,`6`,`4`,`3`],answer:2,explanation:`Three stereocentres would predict 2^3 = 8, but C2 and C4 carry constitutionally identical halves, so several arrangements have an internal mirror plane. Only 4 distinct compounds exist: one pair of enantiomers plus two meso forms, the latter differing at the pseudoasymmetric C3 (labelled lower-case r and s). Answering 8 ignores the internal symmetry that always has to be checked before applying 2^n.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-oc-stereochem-16`,topicId:`oc-stereochem`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which statement about single-enantiomer drugs is correct?`,options:[`Esomeprazole is the (R) enantiomer of omeprazole`,`Levocetirizine is the (R) enantiomer of cetirizine`,`Dexketoprofen is the (R) enantiomer of ketoprofen`,`Levosalbutamol is the (S) enantiomer of salbutamol`],answer:1,explanation:`Levocetirizine is indeed (R)-cetirizine, the eutomer with the higher H1 receptor affinity. The other three statements invert the assignment: esomeprazole is the (S) enantiomer of omeprazole, dexketoprofen is the (S) enantiomer of ketoprofen, and levosalbutamol is the (R) enantiomer of salbutamol — note that the levo and dextro prefixes here are trade conventions and do not track the R/S label in a fixed way.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-substitution-9`,topicId:`oc-substitution`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which of the following is the best leaving group?`,options:[`Fluoride`,`Hydroxide`,`Amide (NH2-)`,`Tosylate (p-toluenesulfonate)`],answer:3,explanation:`A good leaving group is a weak base that can carry the electron pair away comfortably, and the tosylate anion is superb because the charge is delocalised over three sulfonate oxygens. Fluoride is the poorest of the halides because the C-F bond is strong and fluoride is relatively basic, while hydroxide and the amide ion are strong bases and leave only after being protonated or converted to a sulfonate ester.`},{id:`q-oc-substitution-10`,topicId:`oc-substitution`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Chlorobenzene and vinyl chloride are inert to both SN1 and SN2 substitution because:`,options:[`Chlorine is not a leaving group in these compounds`,`The C-Cl bond has partial double-bond character from lone-pair donation into the sp2 carbon, and the resulting cation would be very unstable`,`They are too sterically crowded for backside attack`,`They react only through free-radical pathways`],answer:1,explanation:`Lone-pair donation from chlorine into the adjacent sp2 system shortens and strengthens the C-Cl bond, and any vinyl or aryl cation formed would have its empty orbital in the ring or alkene plane, unable to be stabilised. Steric crowding is not the issue, since the sp2 carbon is quite open; these substrates need SNAr or benzyne conditions instead.`},{id:`q-oc-substitution-11`,topicId:`oc-substitution`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`1-bromopropane treated with silver cyanide (AgCN) gives mainly:`,options:[`Butanenitrile`,`Propan-1-ol`,`Propyl isocyanide (propyl isonitrile)`,`Propene`],answer:2,explanation:`Cyanide is an ambident nucleophile: with the ionic salt KCN the softer carbon end attacks and the nitrile is formed, but the covalent silver salt pushes the reaction towards SN1 character so the harder nitrogen end bonds, giving the isocyanide. The same logic explains AgNO2 giving nitroalkanes while KNO2 gives alkyl nitrites.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-substitution-12`,topicId:`oc-substitution`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Bis(2-chloroethyl) sulfide (mustard gas) hydrolyses thousands of times faster than 1-chlorobutane, and with net retention of configuration at the reacting carbon. The reason is:`,options:[`Anchimeric assistance: the sulfur lone pair displaces chloride intramolecularly to give a cyclic sulfonium ion, which water then opens`,`The C-Cl bond is unusually weak in this compound`,`Sulfur makes the carbon more electrophilic by induction`,`The reaction switches to a free-radical chain`],answer:0,explanation:`This is neighbouring group participation: the internal sulfur attacks first with inversion to give a three-membered episulfonium ion, and water then opens that ring with a second inversion, so two inversions give net retention plus a huge rate acceleration. A simple inductive argument predicts nothing about stereochemistry and cannot account for a rate enhancement of this size.`,tag:`Mechanism`},{id:`q-oc-substitution-13`,topicId:`oc-substitution`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which combination will proceed predominantly by an SN1 mechanism?`,options:[`1-bromobutane with sodium cyanide in dimethyl sulfoxide`,`2-bromo-2-methylpropane warmed in aqueous ethanol`,`Bromomethane with sodium iodide in acetone`,`1-bromopropane with sodium methoxide in dimethylformamide`],answer:1,explanation:`SN1 needs a substrate that gives a stable cation and a polar protic, weakly nucleophilic solvent to act as both medium and nucleophile, which is exactly tertiary bromide solvolysis in aqueous ethanol. The other three pair primary or methyl substrates with strong anionic nucleophiles in polar aprotic solvents, which is textbook SN2.`},{id:`q-oc-substitution-14`,topicId:`oc-substitution`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Chlorobenzene labelled with 14C only at the carbon bearing chlorine is treated with sodium amide in liquid ammonia. The aniline obtained carries the label equally at the carbon bearing NH2 and at the adjacent carbon. This proves the reaction goes by:`,options:[`A concerted SN2 displacement at the ring carbon`,`An SNAr addition-elimination through a Meisenheimer complex`,`A free-radical chain substitution`,`Elimination-addition through a symmetrical benzyne intermediate`],answer:3,explanation:`A symmetrical benzyne has an equivalent pair of carbons across its extra bond, so ammonia adds to either one with equal probability and the label is split 50:50 — the appearance of product on the adjacent carbon is called cine substitution. Both SN2 at an sp2 ring carbon and SNAr addition-elimination would place the amino group only on the originally labelled carbon.`,tag:`Mechanism`},{id:`q-oc-substitution-15`,topicId:`oc-substitution`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which halide fails to undergo SN1 solvolysis even under strongly ionising conditions, despite the carbon bearing the halogen being fully substituted?`,options:[`tert-butyl bromide`,`2-bromo-2-methylbutane`,`1-bromobicyclo[2.2.1]heptane (a bridgehead halide)`,`Triphenylmethyl bromide`],answer:2,explanation:`A bridgehead carbon in a small bicyclic cage cannot flatten to the planar sp2 geometry a carbocation demands, and by Bredt rule the empty p orbital cannot be aligned, so ionisation is prohibitively slow. tert-butyl bromide and 2-bromo-2-methylbutane ionise easily, and triphenylmethyl bromide is the fastest of all because its cation is stabilised by three rings.`,tag:`Exception`},{id:`q-oc-substitution-16`,topicId:`oc-substitution`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Optically active (S)-2-bromooctane slowly loses all of its optical activity when simply stirred with sodium bromide in acetone, even though no new product is formed. The best explanation is:`,options:[`The bromide ionises to a planar carbocation that is recaptured from both faces`,`Repeated SN2 displacement of bromide by bromide, each event inverting the stereocentre, eventually gives a 50:50 mixture`,`An E2 elimination followed by re-addition of HBr`,`An SNi process delivering bromide to the same face it left from`],answer:1,explanation:`Bromide is both a good nucleophile and a good leaving group, so every successful backside attack swaps one bromine for another with inversion; after enough exchanges the two enantiomers are present in equal amounts and the rotation falls to zero. Acetone is polar aprotic and a secondary halide is a poor cation precursor, so an ionisation route is unlikely, and an SNi pathway would preserve configuration rather than destroy it.`,tag:`Mechanism`},{id:`q-oc-elimination-addition-9`,topicId:`oc-elimination-addition`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Arrange in increasing order of stability: ethene, propene, trans-but-2-ene, 2-methylbut-2-ene.`,options:[`trans-but-2-ene < ethene < propene < 2-methylbut-2-ene`,`2-methylbut-2-ene < trans-but-2-ene < propene < ethene`,`ethene < propene < trans-but-2-ene < 2-methylbut-2-ene`,`propene < ethene < 2-methylbut-2-ene < trans-but-2-ene`],answer:2,explanation:`Alkene stability rises with the number of alkyl substituents on the double bond because of hyperconjugation and the sigma-pi stabilisation of the substituted carbons, so unsubstituted ethene is least stable, then monosubstituted propene, then disubstituted trans-but-2-ene, then trisubstituted 2-methylbut-2-ene. Heats of hydrogenation confirm this: ethene 137, propene 126 and trans-but-2-ene 116 kJ/mol.`},{id:`q-oc-elimination-addition-10`,topicId:`oc-elimination-addition`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which reagent converts but-2-yne into cis-but-2-ene?`,options:[`Hydrogen with Lindlar catalyst (Pd on CaCO3 poisoned with quinoline)`,`Sodium in liquid ammonia`,`Hydrogen over platinum at 3 atm`,`Lithium aluminium hydride in ether`],answer:0,explanation:`The Lindlar catalyst is deliberately poisoned so hydrogenation stops at the alkene, and because both hydrogens are delivered from the metal surface the addition is syn and the cis alkene results. Sodium in liquid ammonia works through a radical anion and gives the trans alkene, while unpoisoned platinum takes the alkyne all the way to butane.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-elimination-addition-11`,topicId:`oc-elimination-addition`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A substrate has a strongly acidic beta hydrogen (activated by an adjacent nitro or carbonyl group) and a poor leaving group such as fluoride. Its elimination will most likely follow which mechanism?`,options:[`E2, concerted and anti-periplanar`,`E1, through a carbocation`,`E1cb, through a discrete carbanion formed before the leaving group departs`,`A syn pyrolytic elimination`],answer:2,explanation:`When the beta proton is unusually acidic and the leaving group is reluctant, deprotonation runs ahead of C-X cleavage and a stabilised carbanion (conjugate base) is formed as a real intermediate, which then expels the leaving group — hence E1cb. E2 would need the two events to be concerted, and E1 would need a good leaving group and a stable cation, neither of which is present.`,tag:`Mechanism`},{id:`q-oc-elimination-addition-12`,topicId:`oc-elimination-addition`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`2-methylbutan-2-ol heated with concentrated sulfuric acid at about 170 degrees C gives mainly:`,options:[`2-methylbut-1-ene`,`2-methylbut-2-ene`,`3-methylbut-1-ene`,`2-methylbutane`],answer:1,explanation:`Acid-catalysed dehydration is an E1 process: the protonated alcohol loses water to a tertiary cation, and loss of a proton from the more substituted side gives the trisubstituted Saytzeff alkene 2-methylbut-2-ene. 2-methylbut-1-ene is the minor Hofmann-type product formed by removing a proton from the methyl group, and it would only dominate under bulky-base E2 conditions.`},{id:`q-oc-elimination-addition-13`,topicId:`oc-elimination-addition`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`sec-butyltrimethylammonium hydroxide heated to about 150 degrees C gives mainly but-1-ene rather than but-2-ene. The reason is:`,options:[`The bulky, positively charged NMe3 leaving group forces removal of the least hindered beta proton, so the Hofmann alkene forms`,`But-1-ene is the thermodynamically more stable alkene`,`The quaternary salt first rearranges to a carbocation`,`Hydroxide is too weak a base to reach an internal hydrogen`],answer:0,explanation:`In a Hofmann exhaustive methylation the leaving group is a bulky cationic trimethylamine, which both hinders approach to the internal beta hydrogens and makes the terminal C-H more acidic, so elimination takes E1cb-like character and gives the less substituted alkene. But-2-ene is in fact the more stable alkene, which is exactly why it would be the Saytzeff product with a halide leaving group.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-elimination-addition-14`,topicId:`oc-elimination-addition`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`3-methylbut-1-ene treated with hydrogen chloride gives mainly:`,options:[`1-chloro-3-methylbutane`,`2-chloro-3-methylbutane`,`2-chloro-2-methylbutane`,`1-chloro-2-methylbutane`],answer:2,explanation:`Markovnikov protonation of the terminal carbon gives a secondary cation, which undergoes a 1,2-hydride shift to the adjacent tertiary carbon because the tertiary cation is much more stable; chloride then captures it to give 2-chloro-2-methylbutane. 2-chloro-3-methylbutane is the unrearranged Markovnikov product and is only the minor component — skeletal rearrangement is the hallmark of any carbocation intermediate.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-elimination-addition-15`,topicId:`oc-elimination-addition`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`1-methylcyclohexene is treated with diborane in THF and then with alkaline hydrogen peroxide. The major product is:`,options:[`1-methylcyclohexan-1-ol`,`cis-2-methylcyclohexan-1-ol`,`2-methylcyclohexanone`,`trans-2-methylcyclohexan-1-ol`],answer:3,explanation:`Boron adds to the less substituted alkene carbon (anti-Markovnikov) and hydrogen to the more substituted one, and because the two add across the same face the added hydrogen and the boron, which is later replaced by OH with retention, end up cis to each other. That places the hydroxyl at C2 cis to the new hydrogen at C1 and therefore trans to the methyl group, giving trans-2-methylcyclohexan-1-ol. 1-methylcyclohexan-1-ol would be the Markovnikov product of acid-catalysed hydration.`,tag:`Stereochemistry`},{id:`q-oc-elimination-addition-16`,topicId:`oc-elimination-addition`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Buta-1,3-diene treated with one equivalent of HBr gives mostly 3-bromobut-1-ene at -80 degrees C but mostly 1-bromobut-2-ene at 40 degrees C. This is because:`,options:[`Two different allylic cations are formed at the two temperatures`,`The 1,2-adduct forms faster (kinetic control) while the 1,4-adduct is the more stable and takes over once the addition becomes reversible (thermodynamic control)`,`A free-radical mechanism operates at the higher temperature`,`The diene isomerises to but-2-yne before addition`],answer:1,explanation:`Both products come from the same resonance-stabilised allylic cation; bromide attaches faster to the carbon nearer the site of protonation, giving the 1,2-adduct 3-bromobut-1-ene at low temperature. Warming makes the addition reversible so the system equilibrates to the 1,4-adduct 1-bromobut-2-ene, whose internal double bond is disubstituted and therefore more stable. Only one cation is involved, so any explanation based on two different intermediates is wrong.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-aromatic-9`,topicId:`oc-aromatic`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Cycloheptatriene loses a hydride to give the tropylium ion. How many pi electrons does that ion hold, and is it aromatic?`,options:[`8 pi electrons, antiaromatic`,`6 pi electrons, aromatic`,`7 pi electrons, non-aromatic`,`6 pi electrons, but non-aromatic because the ring is seven-membered`],answer:1,explanation:`The seven-membered ring becomes planar and fully conjugated with 6 pi electrons spread over seven carbons, satisfying 4n + 2 with n = 1, so tropylium is aromatic and stable enough to be isolated as its bromide salt. Huckel rule places no restriction on ring size, only on planarity, full conjugation and the electron count, so ruling it out for having seven members is the trap.`},{id:`q-oc-aromatic-10`,topicId:`oc-aromatic`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which reagent set converts benzene into acetophenone?`,options:[`Acetic acid with concentrated sulfuric acid`,`Acetaldehyde with aluminium chloride`,`Ethyl chloride with aluminium chloride`,`Acetyl chloride with anhydrous aluminium chloride`],answer:3,explanation:`Friedel-Crafts acylation generates the resonance-stabilised acylium ion CH3CO+ from acetyl chloride and the Lewis acid, and it attaches to the ring to give acetophenone. Ethyl chloride with aluminium chloride is an alkylation and would give ethylbenzene, while acetic acid with sulfuric acid does not generate an electrophile strong enough to attack benzene.`},{id:`q-oc-aromatic-11`,topicId:`oc-aromatic`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Benzene treated with 1-chloropropane and anhydrous aluminium chloride gives predominantly:`,options:[`Isopropylbenzene (cumene)`,`n-propylbenzene`,`Propiophenone`,`1-phenylpropan-1-ol`],answer:0,explanation:`The primary propyl cation formed on complexation is unstable and undergoes a 1,2-hydride shift to the secondary isopropyl cation before the ring attacks, so cumene is the major product. n-propylbenzene is what a naive one-to-one substitution would predict, and this rearrangement, together with polyalkylation, is why Friedel-Crafts acylation followed by Clemmensen reduction is the reliable route to straight-chain alkylbenzenes.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-aromatic-12`,topicId:`oc-aromatic`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`To prepare m-bromonitrobenzene from benzene, the correct order of steps is:`,options:[`Brominate first, then nitrate`,`Nitrate and brominate simultaneously in one pot`,`Nitrate first, then brominate`,`Sulfonate, then brominate, then remove the sulfonic acid group`],answer:2,explanation:`The nitro group already on the ring is a meta director, so the bromine then enters meta and the desired isomer is obtained. Brominating first is the trap: bromine is an ortho-para director, so the subsequent nitration would deliver mainly o- and p-bromonitrobenzene. Sequence questions like this always turn on which group is installed first, since that group controls the orientation of the next one.`},{id:`q-oc-aromatic-13`,topicId:`oc-aromatic`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Toluene gives benzyl chloride with chlorine under ultraviolet light but o- and p-chlorotoluene with chlorine and iron(III) chloride. The reason is:`,options:[`Light isomerises toluene before the reaction`,`Ultraviolet light homolyses Cl2 to chlorine radicals, which abstract the benzylic hydrogen, whereas FeCl3 polarises Cl2 into an electrophile that attacks the ring`,`Iron(III) chloride blocks the side chain sterically`,`The side chain is more electron rich than the ring`],answer:1,explanation:`Ultraviolet light starts a free-radical chain, and the benzylic C-H is abstracted preferentially because the resulting benzyl radical is resonance stabilised over the ring. A Lewis acid instead generates a chloronium-like electrophile, so the reaction becomes ordinary electrophilic aromatic substitution directed ortho and para by the methyl group. The mnemonic is that light attacks the side chain and a Lewis acid attacks the ring.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-aromatic-14`,topicId:`oc-aromatic`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Phenol shaken with excess bromine water immediately gives a white precipitate of:`,options:[`o-bromophenol`,`p-bromophenol`,`2,4-dibromophenol`,`2,4,6-tribromophenol`],answer:3,explanation:`The hydroxyl group is so strongly activating that no catalyst is needed and bromination continues until both ortho positions and the para position are substituted, giving 2,4,6-tribromophenol, which is insoluble and drops out as a white solid used as a qualitative test. p-bromophenol is obtained only when the reaction is deliberately slowed by using bromine in a non-polar solvent such as carbon disulfide at low temperature.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-aromatic-15`,topicId:`oc-aromatic`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Cyclodecapentaene ([10]annulene) has 10 pi electrons, which fits 4n + 2 with n = 2, yet it is not aromatic. Why?`,options:[`It has an odd number of double bonds`,`It is antiaromatic instead`,`Transannular strain between the internal hydrogens prevents the ring from being planar, so the p orbitals cannot overlap continuously`,`It has no conjugated pi system`],answer:2,explanation:`Huckel rule has three requirements — cyclic, planar and fully conjugated — before the electron count matters, and the two inward-pointing hydrogens of the all-cis and mono-trans [10]annulenes clash so badly that the ring buckles. This is exactly the reason cyclooctatetraene adopts a tub shape, and both are classed as non-aromatic rather than antiaromatic because the loss of planarity removes the destabilisation as well.`,tag:`Exception`},{id:`q-oc-aromatic-16`,topicId:`oc-aromatic`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Friedel-Crafts acylation stops cleanly at the mono-substituted product while alkylation gives mixtures of poly-substituted products. The explanation is:`,options:[`The acyl group introduced is deactivating (-I and -M), so the product is less reactive than the starting arene, whereas the alkyl group is activating`,`The acylium ion is too large to attack twice`,`Aluminium chloride is consumed only in acylation`,`Acylation is reversible while alkylation is not`],answer:0,explanation:`Once a carbonyl group is attached the ring is electron poor and resists a second electrophilic attack, so acylation is self-limiting. An alkyl group is weakly electron releasing, so the mono-alkylated ring is more reactive than benzene itself and is attacked again — a genuine mechanistic difference, not a question of the size of the electrophile. Aluminium chloride is in fact required in more than stoichiometric amount for acylation because the ketone product complexes it.`},{id:`q-oc-carbonyl-9`,topicId:`oc-carbonyl`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Benzaldehyde treated with the ylide (C6H5)3P=CH2 gives:`,options:[`Benzyl alcohol`,`Phenylacetaldehyde`,`Styrene`,`Toluene`],answer:2,explanation:`The Wittig reaction replaces the carbonyl oxygen with the carbon of the ylide, so PhCHO plus CH2 gives PhCH=CH2, styrene, along with triphenylphosphine oxide, whose formation is the driving force. Benzyl alcohol would be a reduction product and toluene a full deoxygenation, neither of which a phosphorus ylide performs.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-carbonyl-10`,topicId:`oc-carbonyl`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Arrange in decreasing order of reactivity towards nucleophilic acyl substitution: ethyl acetate, acetamide, acetic anhydride, acetyl chloride.`,options:[`acetyl chloride > acetic anhydride > ethyl acetate > acetamide`,`acetamide > ethyl acetate > acetic anhydride > acetyl chloride`,`acetic anhydride > acetyl chloride > acetamide > ethyl acetate`,`ethyl acetate > acetyl chloride > acetic anhydride > acetamide`],answer:0,explanation:`Reactivity tracks how good the leaving group is and how little the substituent donates into the carbonyl: chloride is the best leaving group and donates almost nothing, while the nitrogen of an amide donates strongly and its leaving group would be the amide ion. This ordering is why an acid chloride can be converted to any of the others but never the reverse without activation.`},{id:`q-oc-carbonyl-11`,topicId:`oc-carbonyl`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Arrange in increasing order of reactivity towards nucleophilic addition of HCN: acetone, acetaldehyde, formaldehyde, di-tert-butyl ketone.`,options:[`formaldehyde < acetaldehyde < acetone < di-tert-butyl ketone`,`acetone < acetaldehyde < formaldehyde < di-tert-butyl ketone`,`acetaldehyde < formaldehyde < di-tert-butyl ketone < acetone`,`di-tert-butyl ketone < acetone < acetaldehyde < formaldehyde`],answer:3,explanation:`Two factors act together: alkyl groups are +I and reduce the partial positive charge on the carbonyl carbon, and they also crowd the approach of the nucleophile to the tetrahedral transition state. Formaldehyde has neither handicap and is the most reactive, while di-tert-butyl ketone is so hindered it barely adds at all. Any sequence that puts formaldehyde at the unreactive end has the electronic argument backwards.`},{id:`q-oc-carbonyl-12`,topicId:`oc-carbonyl`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Two equivalents of benzaldehyde condensed with one of acetone in aqueous sodium hydroxide give:`,options:[`Benzoin`,`Dibenzylideneacetone (1,5-diphenylpenta-1,4-dien-3-one)`,`Cinnamic acid`,`Benzalacetophenone from self-condensation of benzaldehyde`],answer:1,explanation:`This is a crossed (Claisen-Schmidt) aldol condensation: benzaldehyde has no alpha hydrogen so it can only be the electrophile, and each of the two alpha carbons of acetone attacks one molecule of it, with dehydration each time giving the doubly conjugated dienone. Benzoin requires cyanide catalysis and cinnamic acid requires acetic anhydride with sodium acetate under Perkin conditions.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-carbonyl-13`,topicId:`oc-carbonyl`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Benzaldehyde and formaldehyde together in concentrated sodium hydroxide give mainly:`,options:[`Benzoic acid and methanol`,`Sodium benzoate and formaldehyde unchanged`,`Benzyl alcohol and sodium formate`,`Benzaldehyde dimer and sodium carbonate`],answer:2,explanation:`In a crossed Cannizzaro the better hydride donor is oxidised, and formaldehyde is the best of all because it is the least hindered and most electrophilic, so it takes the oxidation and becomes formate while benzaldehyde is reduced to benzyl alcohol. Expecting benzaldehyde to be oxidised to benzoate is the natural but wrong guess; that happens only in the self-Cannizzaro of benzaldehyde alone.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-carbonyl-14`,topicId:`oc-carbonyl`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Cyclohexanone treated with methyl vinyl ketone and base gives a bicyclic enone. The two mechanistic steps involved are:`,options:[`Michael (conjugate) addition of the enolate to the enone, then an intramolecular aldol condensation — the Robinson annulation`,`Claisen condensation followed by decarboxylation`,`Mannich reaction followed by Hofmann elimination`,`Wittig olefination followed by Diels-Alder cycloaddition`],answer:0,explanation:`The cyclohexanone enolate adds 1,4 to methyl vinyl ketone to give a 1,5-diketone, and a second enolate then closes the six-membered ring by intramolecular aldol addition and dehydrates to the conjugated enone. A Claisen condensation needs two ester molecules and gives a beta-keto ester, so it cannot build a new carbocyclic ring here.`,tag:`Mechanism`},{id:`q-oc-carbonyl-15`,topicId:`oc-carbonyl`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which carbonyl compound exists almost entirely as its stable, isolable gem-diol (hydrate) in water?`,options:[`Acetone`,`Benzaldehyde`,`Chloral (trichloroacetaldehyde)`,`Acetaldehyde`],answer:2,explanation:`Three -I chlorines on the alpha carbon destabilise the carbonyl and strongly stabilise the tetrahedral hydrate, so chloral hydrate is a crystalline solid used as a sedative. Formaldehyde is over 99 percent hydrated but its hydrate is not isolable; acetaldehyde is about 50 percent hydrated, acetone under 1 percent, and benzaldehyde essentially not at all because conjugation with the ring stabilises its carbonyl.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-carbonyl-16`,topicId:`oc-carbonyl`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`The benzoin condensation of two molecules of benzaldehyde requires cyanide ion because cyanide:`,options:[`Simply acts as a base to remove an alpha hydrogen`,`Adds to the carbonyl and, being both a good leaving group and able to stabilise the adjacent carbanion, reverses the polarity of the carbonyl carbon so it can act as a nucleophile`,`Oxidises one aldehyde molecule to the acid`,`Forms a cyanohydrin that is the final product`],answer:1,explanation:`Cyanide adds reversibly, the resulting cyanohydrin anion loses the aldehydic proton, and the -CN group stabilises the carbanion so that carbon — normally electrophilic — attacks the second aldehyde; cyanide is then expelled to reveal the alpha-hydroxy ketone. This polarity reversal is called umpolung, and thiamine pyrophosphate does the same job biologically with its thiazolium ylide. Benzaldehyde has no alpha hydrogen at all, so a simple base explanation cannot apply.`,tag:`Mechanism`},{id:`q-oc-name-reactions-9`,topicId:`oc-name-reactions`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Sodium phenoxide heated with carbon dioxide at about 125 degrees C and 4-7 atm, followed by acidification, gives:`,options:[`Salicylaldehyde`,`Salicylic acid`,`Benzoic acid`,`Phenyl carbonate`],answer:1,explanation:`This is the Kolbe-Schmitt reaction: the electron-rich phenoxide attacks carbon dioxide at its ortho position and tautomerisation gives 2-hydroxybenzoic acid, the precursor of aspirin. Salicylaldehyde comes from the Reimer-Tiemann reaction with chloroform and alkali, which installs a CHO group rather than a COOH group.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-name-reactions-10`,topicId:`oc-name-reactions`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A ketone that also carries an acid-sensitive acetal group must be reduced to the corresponding methylene compound. The appropriate conditions are:`,options:[`Zinc amalgam with concentrated hydrochloric acid`,`Red phosphorus with hydroiodic acid`,`Sodium borohydride in methanol`,`Hydrazine with potassium hydroxide in diethylene glycol at about 180 degrees C`],answer:3,explanation:`Clemmensen and Wolff-Kishner both take C=O to CH2, but Clemmensen uses strongly acidic zinc amalgam and hydrochloric acid, which would hydrolyse the acetal, whereas Wolff-Kishner runs under strongly basic conditions through the hydrazone and is the correct choice for acid-sensitive substrates. Sodium borohydride only reaches the alcohol and does not remove the oxygen.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-name-reactions-11`,topicId:`oc-name-reactions`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which reagent set converts benzoyl chloride to benzaldehyde without over-reduction?`,options:[`Hydrogen over palladium on barium sulfate poisoned with quinoline or sulfur`,`Lithium aluminium hydride in refluxing ether`,`Sodium borohydride in aqueous ethanol`,`Hydrogen over Raney nickel at 5 atm`],answer:0,explanation:`The Rosenmund reduction uses a deliberately poisoned palladium catalyst so hydrogenation halts at the aldehyde stage. Lithium aluminium hydride and hydrogen over Raney nickel would carry the acid chloride through to benzyl alcohol, and sodium borohydride also reduces acid chlorides to the alcohol; the Stephen reduction with tin(II) chloride and HCl reaches the aldehyde but starts from a nitrile, not an acid chloride.`},{id:`q-oc-name-reactions-12`,topicId:`oc-name-reactions`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`N,N-dimethylaniline treated with N,N-dimethylformamide and phosphorus oxychloride, followed by aqueous workup, gives:`,options:[`N,N-dimethylaniline N-oxide`,`4-nitro-N,N-dimethylaniline`,`4-(dimethylamino)benzaldehyde`,`4-(dimethylamino)benzoic acid`],answer:2,explanation:`The Vilsmeier-Haack reaction generates a chloroiminium electrophile from DMF and POCl3 that formylates strongly activated rings, and the para position is attacked because the dimethylamino group is a powerful ortho-para director. The product is the aldehyde, not the acid, since hydrolysis of the iminium salt stops at the CHO stage.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-name-reactions-13`,topicId:`oc-name-reactions`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which sequence converts a carboxylic acid into its next higher homologue, gaining one carbon?`,options:[`Bromine and sodium hydroxide on the derived amide`,`Thionyl chloride, then diazomethane, then silver oxide in water`,`Hydrazoic acid in concentrated sulfuric acid`,`Sodium azide on the acid chloride, then heat and hydrolyse`],answer:1,explanation:`This is the Arndt-Eistert synthesis: the acid chloride reacts with diazomethane to give a diazoketone, and silver oxide promotes the Wolff rearrangement to a ketene, which water traps as the acid with one extra carbon. The other three options are all chain-shortening degradations — Hofmann bromamide, the Schmidt reaction and the Curtius reaction respectively — each losing a carbon as carbon dioxide.`},{id:`q-oc-name-reactions-14`,topicId:`oc-name-reactions`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`An amine warmed with chloroform and alcoholic potassium hydroxide produces an extremely offensive smell. This test identifies:`,options:[`A tertiary amine`,`A quaternary ammonium salt`,`An aromatic amine of any class`,`A primary amine, aliphatic or aromatic`],answer:3,explanation:`The carbylamine (isocyanide) test works only for primary amines, because dichlorocarbene generated from chloroform and alkali needs two hydrogens on nitrogen to be converted into the foul-smelling isocyanide RNC. Secondary and tertiary amines give no such odour, which is why the test is a classification tool rather than an aromatic-versus-aliphatic test.`},{id:`q-oc-name-reactions-15`,topicId:`oc-name-reactions`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which reagent reduces a ketone to a secondary alcohol by hydride transfer through a six-membered cyclic transition state, leaving carbon-carbon double bonds and nitro groups untouched?`,options:[`Aluminium isopropoxide in isopropanol`,`Lithium aluminium hydride in ether`,`Hydrogen over palladium on carbon`,`Zinc amalgam with hydrochloric acid`],answer:0,explanation:`The Meerwein-Ponndorf-Verley reduction coordinates the ketone to aluminium and transfers a hydride from the isopropoxide ligand through a cyclic transition state; because no free hydride or hydrogen gas is involved, alkenes, nitro groups and halides survive. Catalytic hydrogenation would saturate the alkene, and its exact reverse — aluminium tert-butoxide with acetone as the hydride acceptor — is the Oppenauer oxidation.`,tag:`Mechanism`},{id:`q-oc-name-reactions-16`,topicId:`oc-name-reactions`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Benzonitrile is converted to benzaldehyde by:`,options:[`Lithium aluminium hydride followed by aqueous workup`,`Anhydrous tin(II) chloride with hydrogen chloride, then hydrolysis of the imine salt`,`Sodium borohydride in ethanol`,`Hydrogen over Raney nickel in ammonia`],answer:1,explanation:`The Stephen reduction delivers just enough hydride to reach an aldimine hydrochloride, which hydrolyses on workup to the aldehyde. Lithium aluminium hydride and hydrogenation over Raney nickel both take a nitrile through to the primary amine, while sodium borohydride does not reduce nitriles at all; the other reagent that stops at the aldehyde is DIBAL-H at -78 degrees C.`},{id:`q-oc-rearrangements-9`,topicId:`oc-rearrangements`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Neopentyl alcohol (2,2-dimethylpropan-1-ol) treated with concentrated hydrochloric acid gives mainly:`,options:[`2-chloro-2-methylbutane`,`Neopentyl chloride`,`2,2-dimethylpropanal`,`2-methylbut-2-ene`],answer:0,explanation:`Protonation and loss of water would give a very unstable primary cation, so a methyl group migrates from the adjacent quaternary carbon in a Wagner-Meerwein shift to give the tertiary tert-amyl cation, which chloride then captures. Neopentyl chloride is the unrearranged product and forms only in traces, because the rearrangement is far faster than direct capture.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-rearrangements-10`,topicId:`oc-rearrangements`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Benzil (PhCO-COPh) heated with potassium hydroxide gives benzilic acid. The key step is:`,options:[`Loss of carbon monoxide from one carbonyl`,`Enolisation followed by dehydration`,`Migration of a phenyl group from one carbonyl carbon to the adjacent carbonyl carbon after hydroxide has added`,`Migration of a hydride from oxygen to carbon`],answer:2,explanation:`Hydroxide adds to one carbonyl to give an alkoxide, and a phenyl group then migrates with its bonding pair to the neighbouring carbonyl carbon, producing the alpha-hydroxy acid salt after proton transfer. There is no enolisable hydrogen anywhere in benzil, so an enolisation-dehydration route is impossible.`},{id:`q-oc-rearrangements-11`,topicId:`oc-rearrangements`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`2-methylcyclohexanone treated with a peracid such as mCPBA gives a seven-membered lactone. The oxygen is inserted:`,options:[`Between the carbonyl carbon and the unsubstituted (CH2) alpha carbon`,`Into the carbon-hydrogen bond of the methyl group`,`Across the ring to give an epoxide`,`Between the carbonyl carbon and the more substituted alpha carbon, the one bearing the methyl group`],answer:3,explanation:`In the Baeyer-Villiger the group that migrates from carbon to the electron-deficient oxygen of the Criegee intermediate is the one better able to support positive charge, and a secondary carbon bearing a methyl group outranks a plain CH2. Inserting oxygen on the unsubstituted side is the standard error; it would require the poorer migrating group to move.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-rearrangements-12`,topicId:`oc-rearrangements`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which starting material and reagent combination reaches an isocyanate by way of an acyl azide?`,options:[`A primary amide with bromine and sodium hydroxide`,`An acid chloride with sodium azide, then heating`,`A hydroxamic acid with a dehydrating agent`,`A ketone with hydrazoic acid in sulfuric acid`],answer:1,explanation:`The Curtius rearrangement makes the acyl azide RCON3 first and then loses nitrogen on heating to give an acyl nitrene, from which the R group migrates to nitrogen to give the isocyanate. The amide with bromine and alkali is the Hofmann bromamide route and the hydroxamic acid route is the Lossen rearrangement; both reach the same isocyanate but through an N-bromoamide and an activated hydroxamate respectively, not through an azide.`},{id:`q-oc-rearrangements-13`,topicId:`oc-rearrangements`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`1-(aminomethyl)cyclohexan-1-ol treated with nitrous acid gives:`,options:[`Cyclohexanone`,`Cyclohexanecarbaldehyde`,`Cycloheptanone`,`Cyclohexene`],answer:2,explanation:`This is the Tiffeneau-Demjanov ring expansion, a semipinacol process: nitrous acid converts the primary amine to a diazonium ion, nitrogen leaves as the best possible leaving group, and a ring carbon-carbon bond migrates to the exocyclic carbon, expanding the six-membered ring to the seven-membered ketone. Cyclohexanone would require the ring to stay the same size, which cannot happen once the migration occurs.`,tag:`Mechanism`},{id:`q-oc-rearrangements-14`,topicId:`oc-rearrangements`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The oxime of acetophenone in which the hydroxyl is anti to the phenyl group is treated with PCl5. The product is:`,options:[`Acetanilide (PhNH-CO-CH3)`,`N-methylbenzamide (PhCO-NH-CH3)`,`Benzamide`,`Phenyl acetate`],answer:0,explanation:`Beckmann rearrangement moves the group anti to the departing hydroxyl onto nitrogen, so the phenyl group migrates and ends up bonded to nitrogen while the methyl stays on the carbonyl carbon — that is acetanilide. N-methylbenzamide would be the product from the other oxime geometry, where the methyl group is anti, which is why the question always specifies the arrangement.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-rearrangements-15`,topicId:`oc-rearrangements`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In a pinacol rearrangement where the migrating group may be any one of four aryl rings, which migrates fastest?`,options:[`p-nitrophenyl`,`p-methoxyphenyl`,`p-chlorophenyl`,`Phenyl`],answer:1,explanation:`Migration proceeds through a bridged phenonium-type transition state in which the migrating ring carries partial positive charge, so an electron-donating para substituent stabilises it best and the p-methoxyphenyl group migrates fastest. The p-nitrophenyl group, with its strong -M nitro, destabilises that transition state and migrates slowest; the full order is p-OCH3 > p-CH3 > phenyl > p-Cl > p-NO2.`,tag:`Mechanism`},{id:`q-oc-rearrangements-16`,topicId:`oc-rearrangements`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which rearrangement proceeds by the same concerted [3,3] sigmatropic mechanism as the Claisen rearrangement of allyl phenyl ether?`,options:[`Beckmann rearrangement`,`Wolff rearrangement`,`Fries rearrangement`,`Cope rearrangement of a 1,5-diene`],answer:3,explanation:`The Cope rearrangement moves a 1,5-diene through a six-membered cyclic transition state with six delocalised electrons, exactly as the Claisen does with its oxygen-containing analogue; the ring-forming step of the Fischer indole synthesis is a third example. Beckmann is an ionic 1,2-shift to nitrogen, Wolff a 1,2-shift to a carbene carbon, and Fries a Lewis acid-mediated acyl transfer, so none of them is pericyclic.`,tag:`Mechanism`},{id:`q-oc-heterocycles-9`,topicId:`oc-heterocycles`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Nitration of pyridine requires forcing conditions and gives mainly the 3-nitro isomer because:`,options:[`The nitrogen atom blocks C2 and C4 sterically`,`Attack at C2 or C4 places positive charge directly on the electronegative nitrogen in the sigma complex, which is strongly destabilising`,`C3 carries a formal negative charge`,`Pyridine is not aromatic and reacts as a diene`],answer:1,explanation:`Only attack at C3 gives an arenium ion in which none of the resonance structures puts a positive charge on nitrogen, so C3 is the least bad position on an already electron-poor ring. Sterics play no part, since nitrogen carries a lone pair rather than a bulky substituent, and nucleophiles show the opposite preference by attacking C2 and C4.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-heterocycles-10`,topicId:`oc-heterocycles`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Arrange in decreasing order of aromatic character (resonance energy): benzene, furan, pyrrole, thiophene.`,options:[`benzene > thiophene > pyrrole > furan`,`pyrrole > furan > thiophene > benzene`,`furan > pyrrole > thiophene > benzene`,`thiophene > benzene > furan > pyrrole`],answer:0,explanation:`Resonance energies run benzene 150, thiophene 122, pyrrole 90 and furan 67 kJ/mol, because sulfur donates its lone pair into the ring most willingly of the three heteroatoms and the highly electronegative oxygen of furan least. The sequence pyrrole > furan > thiophene > benzene is the electrophilic reactivity order, and swapping the two orders is the classic error.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-heterocycles-11`,topicId:`oc-heterocycles`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which of the following readily acts as the diene in a Diels-Alder reaction with maleic anhydride?`,options:[`Pyridine`,`Thiophene`,`Furan`,`Pyrrole`],answer:2,explanation:`Furan has the lowest resonance energy of the five-membered heterocycles at about 67 kJ/mol, so it behaves much like an ordinary s-cis diene and gives the bicyclic adduct with maleic anhydride. Thiophene and pyrrole are too aromatic to sacrifice their delocalisation easily, and pyrrole reacts with electrophiles by substitution at C2 instead; pyridine is pi-deficient and unreactive as a diene.`},{id:`q-oc-heterocycles-12`,topicId:`oc-heterocycles`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Hexane-2,5-dione heated with phosphorus pentasulfide gives:`,options:[`2,5-dimethylpyrrole`,`2,5-dimethylfuran`,`2,5-dimethylpyridine`,`2,5-dimethylthiophene`],answer:3,explanation:`The Paal-Knorr synthesis cyclises a 1,4-dicarbonyl, and the reagent decides which heteroatom is delivered: ammonia or a primary amine gives the pyrrole, an acid or P2O5 gives the furan, and P2S5 gives the thiophene. A six-membered pyridine cannot be formed from a 1,4-dicarbonyl at all, since only four carbons are available for the ring.`},{id:`q-oc-heterocycles-13`,topicId:`oc-heterocycles`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Pyrrole is sulfonated with the pyridine-sulfur trioxide complex rather than with concentrated sulfuric acid because:`,options:[`Sulfuric acid is not a strong enough sulfonating agent for pyrrole`,`Strong protic acid protonates pyrrole at C2, destroying its aromaticity and causing it to polymerise into a resin`,`The pyridine complex directs the sulfonic acid to C3 instead of C2`,`Pyrrole is insoluble in sulfuric acid`],answer:1,explanation:`Pyrrole is acid-sensitive: protonation occurs on carbon, not nitrogen, and the resulting 2H-pyrrolium cation is a reactive electrophile that attacks another pyrrole molecule, giving black polymeric material. The pyridine-SO3 complex is a mild, essentially non-acidic sulfonating agent that avoids this, and it still substitutes at the usual C2 position.`,tag:`Trap`},{id:`q-oc-heterocycles-14`,topicId:`oc-heterocycles`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The N-H of pyrrole (pKa about 17) is far more acidic than the N-H of pyrrolidine (pKa about 36) because:`,options:[`Pyrrolidine has a much longer N-H bond`,`The pyrrole nitrogen is sp3 hybridised`,`The anion left after deprotonating pyrrole is aromatic, with the negative charge delocalised around the ring`,`Pyrrole is a stronger base as well`],answer:2,explanation:`Removing the pyrrole N-H proton gives the pyrrolyl anion, in which the charge is spread over all five ring atoms of an aromatic six-pi-electron system, so the anion is unusually stable and the acid unusually strong. Pyrrolidine is a saturated amine whose anion has no delocalisation at all, and pyrrole is a much weaker base than pyrrolidine, not a stronger one.`},{id:`q-oc-heterocycles-15`,topicId:`oc-heterocycles`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In quinoline, electrophilic substitution occurs at C5 and C8 while nucleophilic substitution occurs at C2. The reason is:`,options:[`The carbocyclic (benzene) ring is electron rich relative to the pyridine ring, so electrophiles attack there, while the pyridine ring carbons next to nitrogen are electron poor and take the nucleophile`,`C5 and C8 are the only positions not blocked sterically`,`Quinoline is only aromatic in one of its two rings`,`The nitrogen lone pair is delocalised into the benzene ring`],answer:0,explanation:`The ring nitrogen drains electron density from the pyridine half, so an electrophile prefers the untouched benzene half and attacks the alpha positions of that ring, C5 and C8. Conversely nucleophiles add where the intermediate anion can put its charge on nitrogen, which is C2 (and C4) — the same logic that makes the Chichibabin amination of pyridine work at C2. The pyridine nitrogen lone pair is in an sp2 orbital in the ring plane and is not delocalised anywhere.`,tag:`Mechanism`},{id:`q-oc-heterocycles-16`,topicId:`oc-heterocycles`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`The Hantzsch synthesis combines an aldehyde, two equivalents of a beta-keto ester and ammonia. The 1,4-dihydropyridine it produces is the core scaffold of which drug class?`,options:[`Beta-adrenergic blockers`,`Proton pump inhibitors`,`Fluoroquinolone antibacterials`,`Dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers such as nifedipine and amlodipine`],answer:3,explanation:`Hantzsch condensation builds the 1,4-dihydropyridine ring carrying two ester groups and an aryl substituent at C4, which is exactly the nifedipine pharmacophore; mild oxidation would aromatise it to the pyridine. Fluoroquinolones are built on a 4-quinolone and proton pump inhibitors on a benzimidazole-pyridine sulfoxide, so neither comes from this route.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-protection-reagents-9`,topicId:`oc-protection-reagents`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`An alcohol protected as its tert-butyldimethylsilyl (TBDMS) ether is best deprotected with:`,options:[`Hydrogen over palladium on carbon`,`Piperidine in dimethylformamide`,`Tetra-n-butylammonium fluoride (TBAF)`,`Sodium borohydride in methanol`],answer:2,explanation:`Fluoride is used because the silicon-fluorine bond is exceptionally strong, so TBAF cleaves the silyl ether under neutral conditions; dilute aqueous acid also works. Hydrogenolysis removes benzyl ethers and Cbz groups, and piperidine removes Fmoc, so neither touches a silyl ether — this independence is what makes silyl protection orthogonal to the others.`},{id:`q-oc-protection-reagents-10`,topicId:`oc-protection-reagents`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which reagent converts an alcohol into a good leaving group without disturbing the configuration at the carbon bearing it?`,options:[`p-toluenesulfonyl chloride in pyridine`,`Thionyl chloride without a base`,`Concentrated sulfuric acid at 170 degrees C`,`Pyridinium chlorochromate in dichloromethane`],answer:0,explanation:`Tosylation attacks the sulfur atom, so the carbon-oxygen bond of the alcohol is never broken and the stereocentre is untouched, yet the resulting tosylate is an excellent leaving group for a later substitution. Thionyl chloride does break that bond and inverts or retains configuration depending on conditions, concentrated sulfuric acid causes dehydration, and PCC oxidises the alcohol instead.`},{id:`q-oc-protection-reagents-11`,topicId:`oc-protection-reagents`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A molecule contains both an aldehyde and a ketone. Treatment with one equivalent of ethylene glycol and a trace of acid protects:`,options:[`Neither group, because both are equally reactive`,`The aldehyde, because it is the more electrophilic and less hindered carbonyl`,`The ketone, because ketones form more stable acetals`,`Both groups equally, giving a mixture`],answer:1,explanation:`Acetal formation is under kinetic control with limited reagent, and the aldehyde carbonyl is both more electrophilic (only one alkyl group donating into it) and less crowded, so it is converted first. This selectivity is routinely used to mask an aldehyde while a Grignard or hydride reagent is allowed to act on the ketone.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-protection-reagents-12`,topicId:`oc-protection-reagents`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which protecting group and deprotection pairing is INCORRECT?`,options:[`Tetrahydropyranyl (THP) ether removed by dilute aqueous acid`,`TBDMS ether removed by fluoride ion`,`Benzyl ether removed by hydrogenolysis over palladium on carbon`,`Cyclic acetal of a ketone removed by aqueous sodium hydroxide`],answer:3,explanation:`Acetals and ketals are completely stable to base, hydride reagents and organometallics — that stability is the whole reason they are used — and they are removed only by aqueous acid, which reverses the equilibrium. The other three pairings are all correct and, being acid-labile, fluoride-labile and hydrogenolysis-labile respectively, they can be chosen to be orthogonal to one another.`},{id:`q-oc-protection-reagents-13`,topicId:`oc-protection-reagents`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The Swern oxidation of a primary alcohol to an aldehyde uses:`,options:[`Chromium trioxide in aqueous sulfuric acid and acetone`,`Dimethyl sulfoxide activated with oxalyl chloride at -78 degrees C, followed by triethylamine`,`Manganese dioxide in refluxing benzene`,`Sodium hypochlorite with catalytic TEMPO at pH 9`],answer:1,explanation:`Activated DMSO forms an alkoxysulfonium salt with the alcohol, and triethylamine then triggers an intramolecular elimination that delivers the aldehyde plus dimethyl sulfide, all under anhydrous non-acidic conditions. Chromium trioxide in aqueous acid is the Jones reagent and would carry a primary alcohol on to the carboxylic acid; manganese dioxide works only on allylic and benzylic alcohols, and TEMPO with hypochlorite is a genuine oxidation but is a different named method.`},{id:`q-oc-protection-reagents-14`,topicId:`oc-protection-reagents`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A compound containing both a ketone and an aromatic nitro group is treated with sodium borohydride in methanol at room temperature. The product is:`,options:[`The secondary alcohol with the nitro group untouched`,`The amine with the ketone untouched`,`The amino alcohol, both groups reduced`,`No reaction at either group`],answer:0,explanation:`Sodium borohydride is a mild hydride donor whose useful range stops at aldehydes, ketones and acid chlorides, so it reduces the carbonyl to a secondary alcohol and leaves the nitro group alone. Reducing an aromatic nitro group to an amine needs Sn/HCl, Fe/HCl or catalytic hydrogenation, so the option in which both groups are reduced would require two different reagent systems.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-oc-protection-reagents-15`,topicId:`oc-protection-reagents`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Cyclohex-2-en-1-one treated with sodium borohydride in the presence of cerium(III) chloride (Luche conditions) gives predominantly:`,options:[`Cyclohexanone`,`Cyclohex-2-en-1-ol, the allylic alcohol from clean 1,2-reduction`,`Cyclohexanol`,`Cyclohexane`],answer:1,explanation:`Cerium(III) hardens the reducing species by converting borohydride into an alkoxyborohydride and by coordinating the carbonyl oxygen, so hydride is delivered to the carbonyl carbon (1,2-addition) and the double bond survives. Sodium borohydride alone on an enone gives a good deal of 1,4-reduction and hence the saturated ketone or, after further reduction, the saturated alcohol.`,tag:`Mechanism`},{id:`q-oc-protection-reagents-16`,topicId:`oc-protection-reagents`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A ketone is converted to its 1,3-dithiane (thioacetal) with 1,3-propanedithiol, then treated with Raney nickel. The overall result is:`,options:[`The ketone is regenerated unchanged`,`The ketone is reduced to the secondary alcohol`,`The carbonyl group is converted to a CH2 group under essentially neutral conditions`,`The ring sulfur atoms are oxidised to sulfones`],answer:2,explanation:`Raney nickel desulfurises the thioacetal, replacing both carbon-sulfur bonds with carbon-hydrogen bonds, so the net transformation is C=O to CH2 — a third route alongside the strongly acidic Clemmensen and the strongly basic Wolff-Kishner, and the one to choose when the molecule tolerates neither. Simply hydrolysing the dithiane, which needs a mercury(II) or oxidative reagent rather than Raney nickel, is what would return the ketone.`,tag:`Frequently asked`}],n=[{id:`f-oc-bonding-effects-1`,topicId:`oc-bonding-effects`,front:`+I effect order of alkyl groups?`,back:`(CH3)3C- > (CH3)2CH- > CH3CH2- > CH3-`},{id:`f-oc-bonding-effects-2`,topicId:`oc-bonding-effects`,front:`Carbocation stability order?`,back:`Benzyl and allyl > 3 degree > 2 degree > 1 degree > methyl > vinyl`},{id:`f-oc-bonding-effects-3`,topicId:`oc-bonding-effects`,front:`Basicity of methylamines in water?`,back:`(CH3)2NH > CH3NH2 > (CH3)3N > NH3. In the gas phase it is 3 > 2 > 1 > NH3.`},{id:`f-oc-bonding-effects-4`,topicId:`oc-bonding-effects`,front:`Halogen on benzene: which effect controls rate and which controls orientation?`,back:`-I controls rate (deactivating); +M controls orientation (ortho-para).`},{id:`f-oc-bonding-effects-5`,topicId:`oc-bonding-effects`,front:`Acid strength ladder?`,back:`Sulfonic acid > carboxylic acid > carbonic acid > phenol > water > alcohol > alkyne > ammonia > alkene > alkane`},{id:`f-oc-bonding-effects-6`,topicId:`oc-bonding-effects`,front:`Hammett equation?`,back:`log(k/k0) = rho x sigma. Positive rho means negative charge builds in the transition state.`},{id:`f-oc-stereochem-1`,topicId:`oc-stereochem`,front:`Maximum number of stereoisomers for n stereocentres?`,back:`2^n, reduced when a meso form is possible.`},{id:`f-oc-stereochem-2`,topicId:`oc-stereochem`,front:`Specific rotation formula?`,back:`[alpha] = observed rotation / (path length in dm x concentration in g/mL)`},{id:`f-oc-stereochem-3`,topicId:`oc-stereochem`,front:`Meso versus racemate: which can be resolved?`,back:`Only the racemate. Meso is achiral (internal compensation); racemate is external compensation.`},{id:`f-oc-stereochem-4`,topicId:`oc-stereochem`,front:`Cyclohexane conformer stability order?`,back:`Chair > twist boat > boat > half chair (half chair is the ring-flip transition state).`},{id:`f-oc-stereochem-5`,topicId:`oc-stereochem`,front:`Which L-amino acid is R, not S?`,back:`L-cysteine, because sulfur outranks the carboxyl carbon.`},{id:`f-oc-stereochem-6`,topicId:`oc-stereochem`,front:`How many stereoisomers does tartaric acid have?`,back:`Three: (+), (-) and one meso, not the four predicted by 2^n.`},{id:`f-oc-substitution-1`,topicId:`oc-substitution`,front:`SN1 versus SN2 rate laws?`,back:`SN1: rate = k[substrate]. SN2: rate = k[substrate][nucleophile].`},{id:`f-oc-substitution-2`,topicId:`oc-substitution`,front:`Substrate order for SN1 and for SN2?`,back:`SN1: 3 > 2 > 1 > methyl. SN2: methyl > 1 > 2 > 3.`},{id:`f-oc-substitution-3`,topicId:`oc-substitution`,front:`Stereochemistry of SN1 and SN2?`,back:`SN1 racemises (slight excess inversion); SN2 gives complete Walden inversion.`},{id:`f-oc-substitution-4`,topicId:`oc-substitution`,front:`Which solvent type favours which mechanism?`,back:`Polar protic favours SN1; polar aprotic (DMSO, DMF, acetone) favours SN2.`},{id:`f-oc-substitution-5`,topicId:`oc-substitution`,front:`Leaving group order in SNAr?`,back:`F > Cl > Br > I - the reverse of aliphatic substitution, because addition is rate determining.`},{id:`f-oc-substitution-6`,topicId:`oc-substitution`,front:`SOCl2 with and without pyridine on an alcohol?`,back:`Without pyridine: SNi, retention. With pyridine: inversion.`},{id:`f-oc-elimination-addition-1`,topicId:`oc-elimination-addition`,front:`Saytzeff versus Hofmann product?`,back:`Saytzeff = more substituted alkene (small base). Hofmann = less substituted (bulky base or bulky charged leaving group).`},{id:`f-oc-elimination-addition-2`,topicId:`oc-elimination-addition`,front:`Geometric requirement of E2?`,back:`H and leaving group anti-periplanar (180 degrees); trans-diaxial in a cyclohexane ring.`},{id:`f-oc-elimination-addition-3`,topicId:`oc-elimination-addition`,front:`Two ways to hydrate an alkene, and the regiochemistry of each?`,back:`Oxymercuration-demercuration = Markovnikov, no rearrangement. Hydroboration-oxidation = anti-Markovnikov, syn.`},{id:`f-oc-elimination-addition-4`,topicId:`oc-elimination-addition`,front:`Syn or anti: Br2, OsO4, mCPBA then acid?`,back:`Br2 anti; OsO4 syn diol; mCPBA then acid gives the anti diol.`},{id:`f-oc-elimination-addition-5`,topicId:`oc-elimination-addition`,front:`Ozonolysis workups?`,back:`Zn/H2O gives aldehydes and ketones; H2O2 oxidises any aldehyde on to the acid.`},{id:`f-oc-elimination-addition-6`,topicId:`oc-elimination-addition`,front:`Which HX shows the peroxide (Kharasch) effect?`,back:`HBr only. Not HCl, HI or HF.`},{id:`f-oc-aromatic-1`,topicId:`oc-aromatic`,front:`Huckel rule?`,back:`Cyclic, planar, fully conjugated and (4n + 2) pi electrons. 4n pi is antiaromatic.`},{id:`f-oc-aromatic-2`,topicId:`oc-aromatic`,front:`Which deactivating groups are still ortho-para directors?`,back:`The halogens F, Cl, Br, I - deactivating by -I but ortho-para directing by +M.`},{id:`f-oc-aromatic-3`,topicId:`oc-aromatic`,front:`Intermediate of electrophilic aromatic substitution?`,back:`The arenium ion (sigma complex or Wheland intermediate); its formation is rate determining.`},{id:`f-oc-aromatic-4`,topicId:`oc-aromatic`,front:`Two limitations of Friedel-Crafts alkylation that acylation avoids?`,back:`Carbocation rearrangement and polyalkylation.`},{id:`f-oc-aromatic-5`,topicId:`oc-aromatic`,front:`Resonance energy of benzene?`,back:`About 150 kJ/mol (36 kcal/mol).`},{id:`f-oc-aromatic-6`,topicId:`oc-aromatic`,front:`Birch reduction: reagent and product?`,back:`Na or Li in liquid NH3 with ethanol; benzene gives 1,4-cyclohexadiene.`},{id:`f-oc-carbonyl-1`,topicId:`oc-carbonyl`,front:`Which aldehydes undergo Cannizzaro?`,back:`Only those with NO alpha hydrogen: formaldehyde, benzaldehyde, furfural, trimethylacetaldehyde.`},{id:`f-oc-carbonyl-2`,topicId:`oc-carbonyl`,front:`Aldol versus Claisen product?`,back:`Aldol gives a beta-hydroxy carbonyl (then an enone). Claisen gives a beta-keto ester.`},{id:`f-oc-carbonyl-3`,topicId:`oc-carbonyl`,front:`Grignard plus formaldehyde, other aldehyde, ketone, CO2?`,back:`1 degree alcohol, 2 degree alcohol, 3 degree alcohol, carboxylic acid.`},{id:`f-oc-carbonyl-4`,topicId:`oc-carbonyl`,front:`Wittig stereochemistry?`,back:`Unstabilised ylide gives Z alkene; stabilised ylide gives E alkene.`},{id:`f-oc-carbonyl-5`,topicId:`oc-carbonyl`,front:`Acyl derivative reactivity order?`,back:`Acid chloride > anhydride > ester > acid > amide > carboxylate.`},{id:`f-oc-carbonyl-6`,topicId:`oc-carbonyl`,front:`Kinetic versus thermodynamic enolate conditions?`,back:`LDA at -78 degrees C gives the less substituted (kinetic); NaOEt at room temperature gives the more substituted (thermodynamic).`},{id:`f-oc-name-reactions-1`,topicId:`oc-name-reactions`,front:`Reimer-Tiemann: reagents, intermediate, product?`,back:`Phenol + CHCl3 + NaOH; dichlorocarbene; salicylaldehyde (ortho).`},{id:`f-oc-name-reactions-2`,topicId:`oc-name-reactions`,front:`Four degradations that shorten a chain by one carbon?`,back:`Hofmann, Curtius, Schmidt and Lossen - all via an isocyanate, all with retention.`},{id:`f-oc-name-reactions-3`,topicId:`oc-name-reactions`,front:`Clemmensen versus Wolff-Kishner?`,back:`Both convert C=O to CH2. Clemmensen = Zn-Hg/conc HCl (acidic); Wolff-Kishner = NH2NH2/KOH in glycol (basic).`},{id:`f-oc-name-reactions-4`,topicId:`oc-name-reactions`,front:`Sandmeyer reaction?`,back:`Aryl diazonium salt + CuCl, CuBr or CuCN gives ArCl, ArBr or ArCN.`},{id:`f-oc-name-reactions-5`,topicId:`oc-name-reactions`,front:`Rosenmund versus Stephen reduction?`,back:`Rosenmund: RCOCl to RCHO with H2/Pd-BaSO4 and quinoline. Stephen: RCN to RCHO with SnCl2/HCl.`},{id:`f-oc-name-reactions-6`,topicId:`oc-name-reactions`,front:`Vilsmeier-Haack reagents and product?`,back:`DMF + POCl3 on an activated ring; installs a CHO group.`},{id:`f-oc-rearrangements-1`,topicId:`oc-rearrangements`,front:`Beckmann: which group migrates?`,back:`The group ANTI to the oxime OH, migrating to nitrogen with retention.`},{id:`f-oc-rearrangements-2`,topicId:`oc-rearrangements`,front:`Cyclohexanone oxime plus acid gives?`,back:`Caprolactam, the nylon-6 monomer.`},{id:`f-oc-rearrangements-3`,topicId:`oc-rearrangements`,front:`Baeyer-Villiger migratory aptitude?`,back:`3 degree alkyl > 2 degree alkyl, benzyl, phenyl > 1 degree alkyl > methyl. Retention at the migrating carbon.`},{id:`f-oc-rearrangements-4`,topicId:`oc-rearrangements`,front:`Pinacol migratory aptitude?`,back:`Aryl (electron rich first) > vinyl > hydride > 3 degree > 2 degree > 1 degree > methyl.`},{id:`f-oc-rearrangements-5`,topicId:`oc-rearrangements`,front:`Claisen rearrangement type and product?`,back:`Thermal [3,3] sigmatropic shift; allyl phenyl ether gives ortho-allylphenol.`},{id:`f-oc-rearrangements-6`,topicId:`oc-rearrangements`,front:`Favorskii intermediate and outcome?`,back:`Cyclopropanone; an alpha-halo cyclic ketone gives a ring-contracted ester.`},{id:`f-oc-heterocycles-1`,topicId:`oc-heterocycles`,front:`Electrophilic reactivity order of the five-membered heterocycles?`,back:`Pyrrole > furan > thiophene > benzene > pyridine.`},{id:`f-oc-heterocycles-2`,topicId:`oc-heterocycles`,front:`Aromaticity (resonance energy) order?`,back:`Benzene > thiophene > pyrrole > furan.`},{id:`f-oc-heterocycles-3`,topicId:`oc-heterocycles`,front:`Why is pyridine basic and pyrrole not?`,back:`Pyridine lone pair is sp2 and outside the sextet; pyrrole donated its lone pair into the ring.`},{id:`f-oc-heterocycles-4`,topicId:`oc-heterocycles`,front:`Position of electrophilic attack: pyrrole, pyridine, indole?`,back:`Pyrrole C2, pyridine C3, indole C3.`},{id:`f-oc-heterocycles-5`,topicId:`oc-heterocycles`,front:`Skraup and Fischer syntheses give which rings?`,back:`Skraup gives quinoline (aniline + glycerol + H2SO4 + nitrobenzene); Fischer gives indole (arylhydrazone + acid).`},{id:`f-oc-heterocycles-6`,topicId:`oc-heterocycles`,front:`Paal-Knorr synthesis?`,back:`A 1,4-dicarbonyl plus NH3, P2O5 or P2S5 gives pyrrole, furan or thiophene respectively.`},{id:`f-oc-protection-reagents-1`,topicId:`oc-protection-reagents`,front:`What does NaBH4 reduce that LiAlH4 also does, and what does it not?`,back:`Both reduce aldehydes, ketones and acid chlorides. Only LiAlH4 reduces esters, acids, amides and nitriles.`},{id:`f-oc-protection-reagents-2`,topicId:`oc-protection-reagents`,front:`Three oxidants that stop at the aldehyde?`,back:`PCC or PDC, Swern (DMSO/oxalyl chloride/Et3N) and Dess-Martin periodinane.`},{id:`f-oc-protection-reagents-3`,topicId:`oc-protection-reagents`,front:`Deprotection conditions for Boc, Fmoc and Cbz?`,back:`Boc = acid (TFA); Fmoc = base (piperidine); Cbz = H2/Pd-C.`},{id:`f-oc-protection-reagents-4`,topicId:`oc-protection-reagents`,front:`How is a carbonyl protected and deprotected?`,back:`Ethylene glycol with acid gives a cyclic ketal; dilute aqueous acid removes it.`},{id:`f-oc-protection-reagents-5`,topicId:`oc-protection-reagents`,front:`Nitrobenzene reduction: acidic, neutral, alkaline media?`,back:`Sn/HCl gives aniline; Zn/NH4Cl gives phenylhydroxylamine; Zn/NaOH gives hydrazobenzene.`},{id:`f-oc-protection-reagents-6`,topicId:`oc-protection-reagents`,front:`Alkyne to cis versus trans alkene?`,back:`Lindlar catalyst (Pd-CaCO3, quinoline) gives cis; Na in liquid NH3 gives trans.`},{id:`f-oc-bonding-effects-7`,topicId:`oc-bonding-effects`,front:`Carbanion stability order?`,back:`Methyl > 1 degree > 2 degree > 3 degree. By hybridisation: sp > sp2 > sp3.`},{id:`f-oc-bonding-effects-8`,topicId:`oc-bonding-effects`,front:`Degree of unsaturation formula?`,back:`DoU = (2C + 2 + N - H - X) / 2. Oxygen and sulfur are ignored.`},{id:`f-oc-bonding-effects-9`,topicId:`oc-bonding-effects`,front:`Why is m-nitrophenol less acidic than p-nitrophenol?`,back:`Resonance withdrawal by NO2 reaches only the ortho and para positions; from meta only the weaker -I operates.`},{id:`f-oc-bonding-effects-10`,topicId:`oc-bonding-effects`,front:`Steric inhibition of resonance in one line?`,back:`A bulky ortho group twists a substituent out of the ring plane, switching off its +M or -M and leaving only induction.`},{id:`f-oc-stereochem-7`,topicId:`oc-stereochem`,front:`CIP priority of -OH, -NH2, -COOH, -CH3?`,back:`-OH > -NH2 > -COOH > -CH3. First atom decides; COOH beats CH3 one shell out.`},{id:`f-oc-stereochem-8`,topicId:`oc-stereochem`,front:`ee of a 75:25 enantiomer mixture, and its rotation?`,back:`50 percent ee; observed rotation is half that of the pure enantiomer.`},{id:`f-oc-stereochem-9`,topicId:`oc-stereochem`,front:`Which dimethylcyclohexanes are diequatorial?`,back:`trans-1,2, cis-1,3 and trans-1,4.`},{id:`f-oc-stereochem-10`,topicId:`oc-stereochem`,front:`Single-enantiomer drug pairs?`,back:`Esomeprazole = (S)-omeprazole; levocetirizine = (R)-cetirizine; dexketoprofen = (S)-ketoprofen; levosalbutamol = (R)-salbutamol.`},{id:`f-oc-substitution-7`,topicId:`oc-substitution`,front:`Leaving group ability order?`,back:`Triflate > tosylate and mesylate > I > Br > Cl >> F. OH, OR and NH2 must be activated first.`},{id:`f-oc-substitution-8`,topicId:`oc-substitution`,front:`KCN versus AgCN on an alkyl halide?`,back:`KCN gives the nitrile (carbon attacks); AgCN gives the isocyanide (nitrogen attacks).`},{id:`f-oc-substitution-9`,topicId:`oc-substitution`,front:`Stereochemical result of neighbouring group participation?`,back:`Two successive inversions, so net retention - plus a large rate acceleration.`},{id:`f-oc-substitution-10`,topicId:`oc-substitution`,front:`Which halides do neither SN1 nor SN2?`,back:`Vinyl, aryl and bridgehead halides (the last blocked by Bredt rule).`},{id:`f-oc-elimination-addition-7`,topicId:`oc-elimination-addition`,front:`Alkene stability order?`,back:`Tetra > tri > di > monosubstituted > ethene; trans beats cis.`},{id:`f-oc-elimination-addition-8`,topicId:`oc-elimination-addition`,front:`When does E1cb operate?`,back:`Acidic beta hydrogen plus a poor leaving group; a discrete carbanion forms first.`},{id:`f-oc-elimination-addition-9`,topicId:`oc-elimination-addition`,front:`Buta-1,3-diene plus HBr: cold versus warm?`,back:`-80 degrees C gives the 1,2-adduct (kinetic); 40 degrees C gives the 1,4-adduct (thermodynamic).`},{id:`f-oc-elimination-addition-10`,topicId:`oc-elimination-addition`,front:`Hydroboration-oxidation of 1-methylcyclohexene gives?`,back:`trans-2-methylcyclohexan-1-ol - anti-Markovnikov regiochemistry with syn addition.`},{id:`f-oc-aromatic-7`,topicId:`oc-aromatic`,front:`Why does acylation stop at mono-substitution but alkylation not?`,back:`The acyl group is deactivating (-I, -M); an alkyl group is activating, so the ring is attacked again.`},{id:`f-oc-aromatic-8`,topicId:`oc-aromatic`,front:`Benzene plus 1-chloropropane with AlCl3 gives?`,back:`Mainly cumene (isopropylbenzene) - the primary cation rearranges by a hydride shift.`},{id:`f-oc-aromatic-9`,topicId:`oc-aromatic`,front:`Phenol with bromine water versus Br2 in CS2 at low temperature?`,back:`Bromine water gives 2,4,6-tribromophenol (white precipitate); Br2 in CS2 gives mainly p-bromophenol.`},{id:`f-oc-aromatic-10`,topicId:`oc-aromatic`,front:`Toluene plus Cl2: UV light versus FeCl3?`,back:`UV light gives benzyl chloride (side chain, radical); FeCl3 gives o- and p-chlorotoluene (ring, electrophilic).`},{id:`f-oc-carbonyl-7`,topicId:`oc-carbonyl`,front:`Extent of hydration order?`,back:`Chloral (isolable hydrate) > formaldehyde > acetaldehyde > acetone > benzaldehyde.`},{id:`f-oc-carbonyl-8`,topicId:`oc-carbonyl`,front:`Crossed Cannizzaro with formaldehyde - which aldehyde is oxidised?`,back:`Formaldehyde; it becomes formate while the other aldehyde is reduced to its alcohol.`},{id:`f-oc-carbonyl-9`,topicId:`oc-carbonyl`,front:`Robinson annulation = which two steps?`,back:`Michael addition of an enolate to an enone, then intramolecular aldol condensation.`},{id:`f-oc-carbonyl-10`,topicId:`oc-carbonyl`,front:`Benzoin condensation catalyst and the idea behind it?`,back:`Cyanide ion (or a thiazolium ylide); it reverses carbonyl polarity (umpolung) to give an acyl anion equivalent.`},{id:`f-oc-name-reactions-7`,topicId:`oc-name-reactions`,front:`Kolbe-Schmitt: reagents and product?`,back:`Sodium phenoxide + CO2 at 125 degrees C and 4-7 atm, then acid; gives salicylic acid.`},{id:`f-oc-name-reactions-8`,topicId:`oc-name-reactions`,front:`Arndt-Eistert sequence?`,back:`RCOOH to RCOCl (SOCl2), to diazoketone (CH2N2), then Wolff rearrangement (Ag2O, H2O) gives RCH2COOH - one carbon longer.`},{id:`f-oc-name-reactions-9`,topicId:`oc-name-reactions`,front:`What does the carbylamine test detect?`,back:`Primary amines only: RNH2 + CHCl3 + alcoholic KOH gives the foul-smelling isocyanide.`},{id:`f-oc-name-reactions-10`,topicId:`oc-name-reactions`,front:`Meerwein-Ponndorf-Verley versus Oppenauer?`,back:`MPV reduces a ketone with Al(OiPr)3 in isopropanol; Oppenauer oxidises an alcohol with Al(OtBu)3 and acetone. Same equilibrium, opposite directions.`},{id:`f-oc-rearrangements-7`,topicId:`oc-rearrangements`,front:`Benzilic acid rearrangement?`,back:`Benzil + KOH; hydroxide adds and a phenyl migrates to the adjacent carbonyl carbon, giving benzilic acid.`},{id:`f-oc-rearrangements-8`,topicId:`oc-rearrangements`,front:`Which named rearrangements are [3,3] sigmatropic?`,back:`Claisen, Cope, and the ring-forming step of the Fischer indole synthesis.`},{id:`f-oc-rearrangements-9`,topicId:`oc-rearrangements`,front:`Tiffeneau-Demjanov reaction?`,back:`1-(aminomethyl)cycloalkanol + HNO2 expands the ring by one carbon; cyclohexane version gives cycloheptanone.`},{id:`f-oc-rearrangements-10`,topicId:`oc-rearrangements`,front:`Aryl migratory aptitude order?`,back:`p-methoxyphenyl > p-methylphenyl > phenyl > p-chlorophenyl > p-nitrophenyl - electron-rich aryl migrates fastest.`},{id:`f-oc-heterocycles-7`,topicId:`oc-heterocycles`,front:`Where does pyridine nitrate, and why?`,back:`At C3 (beta). Attack at C2 or C4 would put positive charge on the electronegative nitrogen.`},{id:`f-oc-heterocycles-8`,topicId:`oc-heterocycles`,front:`Which five-membered heterocycle does Diels-Alder chemistry as a diene?`,back:`Furan - it has the lowest resonance energy (about 67 kJ/mol).`},{id:`f-oc-heterocycles-9`,topicId:`oc-heterocycles`,front:`Why sulfonate pyrrole with pyridine-SO3 rather than H2SO4?`,back:`Strong protic acid protonates pyrrole at C2, destroys aromaticity and polymerises it to a resin.`},{id:`f-oc-heterocycles-10`,topicId:`oc-heterocycles`,front:`Hantzsch synthesis: components and the drug class it gives?`,back:`Aldehyde + 2 beta-keto esters + ammonia gives a 1,4-dihydropyridine - the nifedipine calcium channel blocker scaffold.`},{id:`f-oc-protection-reagents-7`,topicId:`oc-protection-reagents`,front:`Silyl (TBDMS) ether: how is it put on and taken off?`,back:`TBDMSCl with imidazole installs it; fluoride (TBAF) or dilute aqueous acid removes it.`},{id:`f-oc-protection-reagents-8`,topicId:`oc-protection-reagents`,front:`THP ether: formation and removal?`,back:`Dihydropyran with acid catalysis; removed by dilute aqueous acid. Stable to base, hydrides and organometallics.`},{id:`f-oc-protection-reagents-9`,topicId:`oc-protection-reagents`,front:`Swern oxidation reagents?`,back:`DMSO activated by oxalyl chloride at -78 degrees C, then triethylamine; stops at the aldehyde.`},{id:`f-oc-protection-reagents-10`,topicId:`oc-protection-reagents`,front:`Dithiane plus Raney nickel does what?`,back:`Desulfurises it to CH2 - a neutral third route from C=O to CH2 besides Clemmensen and Wolff-Kishner.`}];export{n as flashcards,e as notes,t as questions};